      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0003" n="art0003">
        <p>F`Lit,hi',Ta:::reY McDOUGALL'S Patent SELF—RAISING FLOUR The Examiner, Saturday, August 21, 1909,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>[ALIT RlCartri RE'..Ell'. ED.] WHOSE HAND?</head>
        <p>[ALIT RlCartri RE'..Ell'. ED.] WHOSE HAND?</p>
        <p>By ANNIE 0. TIIIBIT3.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Lore that Wen," " Hunted Down," "Hidden Hands," "The Eng:lr of Fate." "Robes of s-t'.acle." fze.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER .Icll.</p>
        <p>How she tore herself away, what she said, what she Oid„ how she looi,:ed stle conhl not tell. But directly Ethel had closed the door behind her, h. dropped, face downwards, on the floor, with the lad licrr,or upon her. - She saw the Whole hideous tangle of her th.t horribl ; sname that had broo&amp;d over her for so. long, cl.epening and descend- ing upon her. . She saw the wreck of Geof- frey's life, the wreck of bar own. She saw the horror that would be his and hers if Ethel Verriker and Dick Sterry succeeded in Ending out who it was who killed Stephen Henderson six p.ars ago. Yet why should they? How could thew? At the time she had not even been suspected. There had been some tritce of a woman at the Hall, but nothing had over come of it, no one had ever . suspected her, __and why should they now? How, after all these y2ars, could Dick Sterry hope to discover what the police had failed to discover at the time? It was impossible that he should., Nothing could -ever come to light_ now. But if it did?. She sat upright, and tri2d to think. What were the chances for and against? What could he learn no that the police had-not learnt six years ago? Surely nothing? .</p>
        <p>But -suppose he did----suppose he did ! The thought and the fear liauntd her as .she dressed for dinner. How she managed to dress she did not know. The room wheeled round her, and danced up and down, and even though she told, herself she had nothing to fear there would come back the thought cf the confession she had made to Dr. Dacye, and signed on board the beat—the confession which some day, somewhere, might be washed to shore.</p>
        <p>When it came, what then? When the soda water bottle was put in the' hands of the police, what then? She was very white and told when she went down dressed at last. The sun. still flooded 4he old garden, but it was getting low, and the lawn looked cooler, and was beginning to get shadowy. ,She threw a shawl over her shoulders, and went on to the terrace. No one was f sight. She stood leaning over the balustrade, a dim, dark figure, staring put across the garden. She was glad no one was • about. She wanted a little more time to herself. She was not fit to speak to anyone as yet, and she wanted to hide, to get away.</p>
        <p>Scarcely knowing why she did it, she moved towards a big laurel bush, which grew at the edge of the terrace, close against the steps. It was a great thing, spreadinr, out its dark, shining peen leaves over -7the stonework, stretching its branches • high and low and wide.</p>
        <p>She wriggled through between the green. leaves and the stonework, and so stood, lean- ing over the balustrade, hidden from anyone who came from the house, and from anyon3 who walked on the grass immediately under the terrace, and visible cnly from the other part of the garden. There anyone Could see her, for the _sunlight fell full upon her white face. But she did not care for that. She looked out dully, trying to control herself, to get back a little colour to her cheeks, and to be ready for dinner when the gong rang. She tried .to shut her ears to the singing in them. She seemed to hear again the boom- mg •of the sea, the voice of Dr. Dacre, as she confessed to the murder. She tried to shut her ears to it all, and to the voice of Ethel us she had told her only a few minutes ago of what she and Dick Sterry were going to do. She trietl to shut her mind to the fact that their happiness would depend on finding the true murderer. She tried to forget that, but she could not succeed. It rose out of the shadows—even - out of the .dwindling sunshine, and came menacin7ly towards her. If Dick and Ethel .were to be happy they -must know</p>
        <p>the truth, and if they the - truth it would mean the end of GeofCr3y's happiness,</p>
        <p>and hers. The garden was very still, and she leant motionless, scarcely breathing, over the stone- work, and , suddenly the sound of a voice' from below startled her. Someone .was mov- ing just out of sight underneath. She strained forward. The voke was so low that</p>
        <p>she could scarcely hoar it, and her first im- pulse was to draw back.. She put up her hand to the tree behind her, to rustle the leaves, in order to warn whoever was below, and then suddenly, sharply, like a warning, came the remembrance of Ethel's " joke. ' Her words echoed in her ears, and her hand reMained rigid, raised half-way to the branches of the bush. as she waited for some furti_cr sound to come from beneath. And when it came Olga lowered her hand, and stood with beating heart to listen. " Whnre 7-e the' all ? " came the voice, low, but clear, and it was the reply, a little loiwer, alai a h cue more distinct, that ar- rested Olga. It was Julia Dunston's voice. " All out of sight and hearing," she said. "You need. not fear. No one can possibly overhear us. Geoffrey has gone into the Hall with mother in tow, and Mrs. Verriker is dressing. When Si:.! is ready she will stay with mother, while. Geoffrey goes and changes. - It is all right." • "And Ethel and that girl—Miss Murray ? " " Both dressing," was Julia's reply. `` All out of the way. Now tell me what you're going to do." There was the sound of a rustlinc, dress, as if Julia had dropp Ed to the seat beneath the wall. Apparently Edward did 'not follow suit, for Julia's voice came, sharp and quick. " Sit down, Edward," she said. " You look so uncomfortable standing up, and no one can hear. us. They are all indoors,-all the de- testable lot of them. Don't you hate them all, Edward? I do—from Geoffrey, with his airy, gentlemanly ways, down to that little white-faced beggar from the gutter that he ins picked up. I hate them all, and I be- lieve I hate Geoffrey even more than the *eat. It is so easy to be gentlemanly, and nice, and polite, when you are smothered in money, as he is. And to think of it—of us, with grinding poverty . before us, and hard work. Oh, how .1 hate work! Why, even the servants here will be better 'off than I shall be. Oh, if I had this. place, wouldn't I make them work. Slaves ! I wish I had a slave. I wish I could make somAody feel all that I feel now. I wish I could beat some- thing—hurt something, Edward." She broke off, and for a moment there was silence. With a white, desperate face, Olga bent forward cautiously and looked over the wall. Edward was pacing to and fro, a der!: figure in the fading sunlight. His head was bent, his hands clenched in his pockets. Julia sat upright, rigid, still, on the Non seat. A little jewel sparkling in her hair caught Olga's eye, and she drew back. A strange silence seemed to rush in after Julia's voice ceased. Apparently Edward still paced to and ffo, apparently Jtdia still sat rigid on the seat. A spci'den trembling seized Olga. At any • cost she must hear what they. were going to say, what they were going to do. Why didn't they speak? At any moment someone might come out from the open drawing-room win- dow behind her and disturb them. At any moment the chance of hearing might be gone, And some strong feeling within her warned tier that she must hear. She clenched her hand on the stone wall, and apparently the same feeling seized Julia, for her voice came again sharp and low. "Be quick, Edward, and tell me what you are going to do," she said; "someone will come if we don't take care. Be quick." Olga, leaning over again carefully, saw him stop and face Julia. Then he sat down, and Ins voice, low and husky, was difficult to hear. Olga strained every nerve; but at first, even with the conviction that they meant harm to Geoffrey, it was hard to gather what Edward meant. " We could do it—if we were careful," he was saying, "perhaps after dinner. . . :You have • been over the Hall. of course; but</p>
        <p>if we worked him up to shewing us the pic- ture - gallery . . . we could manage . . . the room is off there . . . if I could get him into it . . . some excuse . . . aud. kick open the bolt . . . the—the—accident woulc? be easy." He drew a breath so sharp finCr harsh that Olga above heard it. Her heart seemed to have stopped be?ting, then, as if to thwart her,' it stared again loud and hard,' as Ed- ward's voice broke the silence once .more. - "Nobody knows of it," -he was saying; the room is scarcely ever opened. Years and years and years ago a child fell down the shaft, and ever since then it has been the rule that the room is lotke6'. It is calk&amp; Bluebeard's Room. The wonder is that, the shaft has not been bricked up; but none of the Verrikers ever took the trouble, and it might be difficult. It was made originally for the escape of priests. A ladder runs down sheer to the bottom, almcst perpendicular, and if anyone fell or was pushed—"</p>
        <p>_ . He stopped. "Yes, yes—l see," Julia's voice came ,13arsli and eager. "Would—would - it—kill them outright 1 " _ ..</p>
        <p>Ec-iwarci. hesitated. Across the wide -gar- dens the sunlight came soft and red. It caught the water at, the far srde of the yew hedges and coloured- it lightly. Edward was staring at it as be spoke, and to all there, to Julia at his side, to Olga, who waited breath- less and ski% witL horror above, the glint of it was like blood.</p>
        <p>"It might," Edward said slowly. "or it might not quite." Tilere was another silencos " But if—if he knew—and spoke—" Julia sair.., slowly. We should have to chance ic," Edward "It would he scarcely likely— no, I do not think he would speak. again." Olga's hands seemed to have frozen to the wall. She tried to lift them,. and they were stiff with pain as though 5h7.7 had been grip- ping ice. She moved one of them, but the next moment clung to the wall again despe- rately, for the whole earth swayed about her when she moved, and the deadly horror, the cruel fear seemed' to be hurting her like a physical pain. The two below were still talking, but she could not hear. She tried again, leaning for- ward and holding her breath, but the rushing in her ears prevented her. She gave a .cry, and instantly the voices below stopped. "NN hat was that ? " cried Julia, sharply, after a moment; and Olga had the strength and the presence of mind to crouch in her corner as they rose, and walking backwards over the grass looked up. "It was nothing," said Edward. . Julia was gripping him with cold hands that clutched over his arm like steel. "It was something," she began; but at that instant a whistle sounded quick and clear, and then came Geoffrey's voice. At the same moment they both saw hirn.come to the drawing-room window, which opened on to the terrace. and 'look round.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>, ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <p>with. lesson - booßs nerore ner, and children staring at her with open eyes. 11E) was ovly dreaming now. "Olga darling, you are having no fruit." It was Geoffrey's voice. He was lean- ing towards her, and his hand had clasped hers as it rested on the table, in spite of Mrs. Dunston opposite, and in spite of the eyes of all who chose to loc. " on't ycu have a poach? You shall. I insist upon it, and I'll peel it for you myself." He took her plate, and presently handed it back to her with the peach, ripe and yellow and juicy, -ready for he.. "You can't say that I ill-treat you yet," he said, laughing. I shall make up for all this attention after we are married." He remained with his eyes on her face for , a-minute : and then suddenly the old vicar's voice brol7e in upon her with almost a shock. 1 "By the way," he said, " die: you 6.20 that another person had been • recovered .from the . sea—a passenger on board the Stella? I saw it in a paper a day or two ago, and this morning it appears that Le has been landed at Southampton. Ile was picked up by a sailing vessel in a very exhausted condition." Geoffrey turned his eyes from Olga's lace. has there ? " he said, eagerly. " scarcely *looked at the papers this morning. Did they give his name ? "Yes, it was Dacre—a Dr. Deere, I think." Olga fell sudc;enly sharply back in her chair. For a moment blackness swept down upon her, blotting out •everything—the bright room, the table the shining silver, the pink flowers—blotting out everything but Mrs. Dunston's eyes; apd out of the blackness they remained staring at her, pitilessly, • bright, and cruel. "-Dr. Dacre !" It was Geoffrey's voice that broke in upon her and pierced the Looming in her ears. "Dr. Dacre," he cried, laughinz. "Why. Olga, do you remember what a bore he :was? I. believe I was jealous of him. He called himself a woman - hater, but he was al- ways pestering us to death. He followed you about lire a cat. Why, even at the very moment when we were going down •he was close by you. Poor old Dacre ! I remember his telling me he hated' women, and he looked as if lie did, too. But Igo am glad' he is all right. - I daresay he was a decent sort, really, but he interrupted us too much for me to love him. Now that he can't bother me anydmore, let's drink his health. Here's luck and long life to him, Olga." He lifted his glass, and Olga turned her face slowly, almost as though he hypnotised her, towards him. .</p>
        <p>"'ldong life—and luck," she - repeated,, mechanically. Then she turned to find Mrs. Dunston's eyes still sharply upon her, and she shivered involuntarily: The web was clos- ing round' her. It was beginning to stifle her —beginning to press heavily.</p>
        <p>She looked round, and then staggered to her feet. Mrs. Verriker had given the signal</p>
        <p>r I</p>
        <p>A</p>
      </div>
