      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>1 /, 0/ .</head>
        <p>1 /, 0/ .</p>
        <p>_ leant motionless over the stonework]</p>
        <p>"ihere is nobody there," said Edward, sharply. "Look, there is no one there. He would see them if there was." Geoffrey had walked to the wall. Hallo, everybody! Where are you? Olga, Ethel—oh, there's somebody at last." He had caught sight of Julia an? Edwaree, and, walking alone, the terrace, ran quickly down the steps and then over the grass to- wards 'them.</p>
        <p>Olga, crouching in the shadow. breathing hepily, watched him with her hands clasped to her face. He was in evening dress and bareheaded; and he looked straight and good and handsome as he walked across the lawn to the two who, to Olga's feverish imagina- tion, almost seemed to shrink as he came. _</p>
        <p>"Everybody seems to be playing hide-and- seek," he exclaimed. "Mother and Mrs. Dunston are sitting looking harmless e nough in the hall, but where are Olga and Ethel? And does anybody know if Sterry has arrived yet ? " They had turned their backs to the house and were walking slowly in the sunshine to- wards the Water Garden. His voice grew fainter as they went. - _ _</p>
        <p>"Perhaps they are still looking at the foun- tains," he said. "Let us go and 4ee." Olga rose slowly from her knees. She was crampe and col', sick and anxious, and afraid. She moved back quietly, with her eyes carefully on the three until she had wriggled back from between the bush and the wall. She looked clown at her dress, at the marks of dust and green moss and cobweb upon it, and turning, ran coickly, trembling and breathless, to het room.</p>
        <p>and everyone had risen. Geoffrey - had 'gone to the door and was Opening it. Olga clutched at the back of her chair for a moment, and then went unsteadily across the room. Her eves were on Geoffrey's face as she neared Icim. How she loved him ! The thought ran through her mind. He would never know 'how much she had laed him. She woulci pass out of his life, and he would never know. He would only remember her as part of the ghastly mistake he' had made in loving her. He would remember- her cnly with shame- -with bitter shame ! She .stooti, cold .as tee, for a moment after she had passed out of the room, and then suddenly she felt Mrs. • Verriker put her arm through hers. . "Where shall we have coffee ? " she was asking. " Mrs. Dunston, would rou feel cold in the hall? It is so hot to-night, and perhaps for a little while it, would not be too chilly. We can go up to the Jrawing-room later."</p>
        <p>She moved forward as she spoke, and Olga went with her mechanically. The great hat, with its carved or and armoured figures and the great shield that belonged to a dead and gone Verriker hundreds of years ago, looked dark and dim. About the great carved oak fireplace were low chairs and' couches. They went towards them, Mrs. Verriker with 1102 arm still through Olga's. " You look tired, dear," she said. " You stooped too much over that lace you mended for me this morning. I h ould not have let you To it." Olga turned almost passionately. "It was notfiing," she. cried. "Of course it was not too much for me. I am quite well now."</p>
        <p>She would have said that nothing she could do for her would be too much, but she could not. Mrs. Dunston and Julia were on the hearth in front of the great open grate, and' Mrs. Dunston, with her lorgnette to her eyes, was watching her in that queer, curious way. Ethel had stopped to benci. over a silver sta- rer which the butler had placed on a side table. _ „ _</p>
        <p>" Letters ! " she 'cried. Two for Geoff and bills for you, mummy dear,. I can see. I don't mind telling you they are hills now that they won't frighten you. And, oh ! Olga, here's one for you. Mine don't look interesting, and I shall leave them." She turned away, and sat down beside her, mother and Julia and Mrs. Dunston, and with a queer, nervous feeling Olga got up and went to the salver.</p>
        <p>A-letter for her ! A letter, when in all the world there was no one to write, no one who knew her address ! - -</p>
        <p>She stood for a moment holding the enve- lope in her -hand. . Mrs. Verriker and Ethel and Mrs. 'Dunston and Julia were talking ap- parently unconcernedly. She could open it if she wished without them noticing If she wished! A sudden trembling, a sudden fear seized her. Who could this letter be from? She looked down at the crabbed, rugged handwriting, and the last dron if colour left her cheeks.</p>
        <p>Then suddenly it came back, and she tore open the envelope. She was absurd, ridicu- lous. No doubt someone in the town had written—the milliner, from whom she had bought a hat yesterday, or the dressmaker, or perhaps some other tradesfolks. She opened the envelope. and took out the sheet of paper which she had found insido it. It was white " and thick. She spread it out., =A her heart jumped into her mouth, for, printed in red letters at the top of the sheet, was the name of the hotel in South- ampton at which she had stayed on the night the and s Geollirey had arrived in Ehgland. She stared at it dumbly for a long moment before her eyes travelled to the writing be- neath, and then the horror burst in upon her in earnest.</p>
        <p>'Almost by an accident, I have to-day learnt that you were . saved from the wreck, and that von have gone into the country with Sir Geoffrey Verriker. The people at this hotel have given .me Sir Geoffrey's address, which he left with them, and I am writing to you at Kernscote Hall, on the chance of your being there. You will know that I must see you, and that I shall seize the first opportunity I have of doing so. I hope to be well enough to travel to-morrow, and shall reach you the day after you get this note. You will not ye- fuse to see me. /There are things I must say —things you must bean—JOHN DiCE.E." She put the letter down slowly.. She stared Out with blank, unseeing eyes at the figure in fluted armour before which she stood. It was a fins old suit, rare and valu- able, and after a moment, as her dazed eyes rested on it, she found herself noting the length of the spear which .stuck in his mailed hand, and then the gleam of light on the shield which was hung, with swords and battleaxes, on the, wall close by. Then sharply the thought of the .etter crashed back upon her, and her face grew set and dreary. The end had come! To- morrow—to-night, indeed, she . must go. When Dr. Dacre came he must not find her here. He could tell what story he likec, but her own story to Geoffrey must be written that night, and left for him to find to-morrow —after she had ,gone ! To-morrow! A great blankness swooped down upon. her. - The pain at her heart grew almost unbearable as she turned at last and faced the group round the great fireplace. - She had forgotten Mrs. Dunston and ,the pitiless eyes that had watched . her through dinner; but now as she crossed the hall she turned, an-1, like a flash, the dread and fear of .something unknown swept back upon her. She looked into the hard bright eyes, and something suddenly leaped to her heart. If they had meant anything—if Julia and Edward whispering under the terrace Avail had really meant what they said, she might save Geoffrey's life this night. She might, perhaps, - shew him how much she loved him before she, went out of his life for ever and for ever and for ever. _ _ _</p>
        <p>She . sat down- very quietly in one -of the big chairs. It nearly swallowed her, and Ethel peeped laughingly round the wing. " Good-night," she said, " we'll wate you early- in the morning." The butler was handing coffee. Olga took hers from the silver tray, and dropped sugar into her cup in a strange, clumsy way. A 9 she put back the milk her shaky fingers dropped the silver- jug with a thud', and', look- ing up sharply, her eyes instinctively went over to Mrs. Dunston. _ . _</p>
        <p>She was watching her still, waiting, vigi- lant and patient, for what? Olga felt the colour die away from her cheeks, and then she sank back in her chair. She too was waittg. She too was watching. But her watching an&amp; waiting would- end wall to-night, and to-morrow she would be gone. She put down her empty cup at last, giving the saucer a jerk with her trmbling fingers,, then Mrs. Verriker looked up. _ .</p>
        <p>"Shall we go into the drawing-roora now ? " sh 3 asked. - _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Olga got up only too readily. Anything was better than sitting still. Any movement was better than none. They went across the hall and along the, corridor to the drawing- room. It looked out on to the terrace, and for a moment Olga went to the window. Out- sic:e,• the night was growing dark and cool. shadows lay heavy now about the garden, and tha lagt _speck, of ted light had left the ponds and the darkening sky above them. Olga turned away and dropped into a chair, while Ethel began to play. It seemed a long time before the men came in at last, and Olgo watched them with bright, anxious eyes as they, came in one by one. Then her heart leaped. Edward was not there!</p>
        <p>_ Dick Sterry sauntered over to the piano, and bending across it began to talk to Ethel. Mr. Pendlebury and the doctor made their way towards Mrs. Verriker and Mrs. Dun- ston, and Geoffrey came straight to Olga. Her troubled eyes travelled beyond him. "Where is Mr. Yerriker ? " she asked in a whisper. He sat down besicte - her, with his face close to hers.</p>
        <p>"He suddenly thought of a note he ought to have written, and has gone up to my room to write it," he said.</p>
        <p>"To your room? Why didn't he write i in the hall ? " she asked, quickly. Geoffrey_ smiled a little.</p>
        <p>(ion't knot. He ruggested' it, my rcom, himself. He knows his way, about here, you know, and I didn't mind. You women were in the hall, and perhaps he was shy. Any way, he's upstairs. He's coming to have a talk with .me to-morrow, by the way. I wanted to mention it to you, my dear, be cause it will concern .you when you are my wife. I think I ought to make some pro- vision for him. Until a few months, ago he looked upon himself as my uncle's heir, and it is scarcely fair to him to be ousted now; and so I thought we could easily spare him ,part of our income, dear. Don't you think so ?</p>
        <p>He was looking with dusk, eager eyes straight at her face, that was growing hard and set.</p>
        <p>"You wouldn't mind, Olga? We shall have 30 much to • ourselves, and, we might spare him a decent income and not miss it. You wouldn't mind, dear ? " Mind? To think so carefully for a man who would take his life if he could? To offer money to a man who wished him dead? She shivered, and looked round almost wildly, and with some curiosity Geoffrey looked' at her. He would not believe that she would object to such an arrangement; and yet she was behaving rather queerly. She turned almost violently at last. "No, no—of course—it is nothing to do with me," she said, • a little huskily. " You can do as you like, of course. Why should you ask me.? _ . .</p>
        <p>"Why 1" The surprise and disappoint- ment in his face sent her cold. "Why, dar- ling, what is mine is yours and I thought I should like you to know. I Want you to knew everything. Dearesc, I never mean to keep anything back from you. I am giving my life into your hands, and I want you to know it through and through." She sat very still, wh the icy cold at her heart, and the lights growing black before her eyes. She felt cold, and. yet she could scarcely iraw her breath. The sound of notes on the piano as Ethel sat down again and be- gan to play made her start. She Stared round, and then back into Geoffrey's face. What would he say when he knew? What would he look like when her life was open and straight for him to read? What would he say when he saw for the first, time the ghastly shadow of shame that lay between them, separating them for ever and for ever and for ever? _ _ -</p>
        <p>What would he say? What would he do? Ah heaven help her, she dared not think. She could not bear to think, and ;he would be happy to-night for the last time. It would be time enough later to think how she was to tell him, what she was to write, time enough later, when she no longer saw his face, when she had gone. She could not wait to see his face change. She would rather he killeo her outright than turn from her; but he would not do that yet—not to-night. She had still a few hours left, and she would make the best of them.</p>
        <p>The thought of Edward s ,ruck her sharply. He was away a long time, his note seemed to take a long time to write; but suppose he was not writing his ~noce She looked' across quickly at Julia; and something in her set and tense face roused her worst fears.</p>
        <p>Julia wa.s waiting. She sat with her eves wandering uneasily about the room, with. her restless fingers playing with her fan, and with an air of listening, of waiting. She was waiting for Edward, of course, but not because she, loved him, not because she missed him, but because—what?_. _</p>
        <p>Olga got up suddenly. Geoffrey had turned and was speaking to Mr. Pendlebury. Ethel and Dick were turning from the piano, and both were speaking to Mrs. Dunston. Only Julia looked unoccupied. Only she would miss her if she went.</p>
        <p>She waited, a moment and then glided softly to the door.</p>
        <p>As she reached it the. handle was twisted sharply. in her fingers, and it was flung open. She gave a little gasp for breath and fell back.</p>
        <p>_ Edward stood outside, Edward, with his yellow skin and black hair, looking strange and unusual. He came in, and Olga saw his eyes go straight .to Julia as she sat, . rigidly upright now, still and expectant. Olga drew a sharp breath and slipped out. No one would miss her- for a moment, and even then it would be some time before they came to look for her, and meanwhile she .would find the Bluebeard'S room, and try to undo the work that she felt Edward Ver- riker had dole while he had pretended to be writing his note. She slipped up the great staircase and along the corridor. It ran the full 'length of the house, with windows lookinc, out on to the garden, and doors opening into rooms on the opposite side. Which of those doors opened into the Bluebeard Chamber? Behind which lay the trap that Edward had pre- .. pared? She went along the corridor slowly, hesi- tating, looking at each door as she passed. Then, with sudden resolution; she put her , hand on the handle of one at the end nearly adjoining Geoffrey's room. Close to it the corridor turned; and at the corner another staircase led to downstairs.</p>
        <p>The door yielded eaf.ily to her touch, and, pushing it open, she looked in. The room was in darkness, but the light from the corridor relieved it a little, and for a moment she hesitated.</p>
        <p>It seethed. a big room, and only scantily furnished. There was no carpet, only a few rugs scattered about. One lay close to the door. She looked round, titer' she fancied she saw something glitter on the floor, and, remembering the bolt Edward had spoken of, she put a foot forward. She must find the trap door and fasten it again securely. She must save his life before she went out of it altogether. She looked round before she moved. No trap door was visible from where she stood. She- put one foot forward on the bare boards which lay between the rug , and the door, and then sprang on to the rug. . . . • She sprang and caught her breath. It gave way quickly. She stumbled • violently and, throwing up her arms with a cry, fell head- long into a black abyss. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>CHAPTER XIV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV.</p>
        <p>Dinner that niglit was a nightmare to Olga. She sat, like one in a dream, listening with dulled ears arid a throbbing heart to the con- versation that went on. Ole: ?Ir. Pendlebury, the rector, and a young doctor had been asked "to balance the table," as Geoffitey had laughingly whispered to Olga. "We shall be quite proper now," he said, "the only bother is that I shall have to take in Mrs. Dunston instead of you. But. I've been and looked at the table, and you are quite close, to me—only on my left for once, instead of on my right, my sweetheart, with old Pendlebury to talk to. If you talk to him too much I shall kick you under the table." _</p>
        <p>Olga had laughed, and somehow or other she had managec . to go in smiling on Mr. Pendlebury's arm. Dut every now and then the face of Mrs. Dunston opposite to her fael'ed in.. a mist before her eyes, and every now and then the laughing voices round her grew into a meaningless babble that seemed to hold in it some sinister threat. The rich dining-room, the table, the shining silver and glass, and the great silver bowls of roses seemed all only part of a dream—an un- reality from which she would presently wake. It could net be that she was there? She would open her eyes presently to find herself in some garret—or, perhaps, with aching head'.</p>
      </div>
