      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>• Royalty in the Highlands.—The Highlands will not see very much of the King and Queen this season. The Queen and Princess Victoria, when they leave Balmoral, will be away until the late autumn, on their visits to Nora ay and Denmark, and it is not expected that the King's stay on Deeside next month will extend over nine or ten days. Since last year, extensive altelations and improvements have been made at Balmoral both internal and in the Castle grounds end gardens. The Queen and Princess Victoria spend most of their time out of doors when there, and the gardens are now at their Lest. The Prince and Princess of Wales will, as usual, spend several weeks at Abergeldie Castle, which was lent for many successive Autumns, to the present King and Queen during the lifetime ef the late Queen Victoria. The Princess of Wales is fond of Abergeldie and thoroughly enjoys her autumn visits to the High- lands, surrounded by her children. The King as Peacerralter.—King Edward's visit to Countess Loweay at Marienbad, recalls the friendship which Queen Victoria extended to Princess Frederick of Hanover, who was disowned by her family for marry- ing her father's seen tary—the Baron von Powell- Rammingen. The late Queen Victoria not only gave the King of Hanovei's daughter her wedding from Windsor Castle, but herself gave the bride away. There are similar family quarrels between between King Leopold and his daughter the Countess L9wgay, formerly the Austrian Crown Princess St4phame, but we may be sure that the King would not have paid his visit if it wererat all likely to give offence to the King of Belgium, and it is more than probable that it was undertaken with a view to bringing about. e: family reconciliation. King Edward is to pay a visit to the Grand Duchess Marie of Saxe-Coburg Gotha at Coburf, when he leaves Marienbad, and it is expected that his Majesty will exert his influence at Madrid, to bring about a reconciliation between the Spanish Court and his niece, Princess Beatrice of Saxe-Coburg, who recently married Prince Alfonso of Orleans, apparently giving great offence to the Spanish Royal Family: Lord Stafford's Coming-of-Age.—The Marquis of Stafford, who will come of age en August 29th, is the eldest son of the Duke and Duchess of Sutherland, and will, in the ordinary course of things succeed, not (illy to the many titles,but also to'great wealth and immense territorial influence. Roughly speaking, the Duke of Sutherland owns about one fiftieth part of Britain, and his estates exceed in area that of any county of England except Yorkshire, Lincolnshire, and Devonshire. Yet in spite of his great possessions, the Duke is less in the public eye then his brilliant consort. Fur the most part the Duke of Sutherland lives the life of a country gentleman, but the Duchess is constantly exerting her- self in matters of public interest. Lord Stafford is a lieutenant in the Scots Greys, and his coming of age will naturally be made the occasion of great rejoicings at Lilleshalj, the Duke's seat in Shropshire. Holiday Topics.—For holiday topics this season, one might suggest a discussion on "The proper age limit for matrimony." We have already discussions on "What is tht right age to marry" and "Are seaside engage- ments advisable," but it is never wise to argue from the particular to the general, on such matters, and it is of no use looking to the law of the land for guidance in respect of such matters. All civilised countries have a minimum age limit for matrimony, but it is only in Russia that there is also a maximum age limit, as there was in ancient Rome. Without a special dispensation, no man over GO, or woman over 50, is allowed to marry in Russia, but in no other country does the State prohibit marriage on the ground of too advanced years. In this country the minimum legal age for matrimony is fourteen for males, and twelve for females, and a marriage contracted at these ages would be if otherwise conforming to the law, a legal union for life, either with or without the consent of patents or guardians. Love Tragedies.—Where a young woman is murdered by a youth who has been paying herattentions, it is almost invariably found that the girls parents have cautioned her, and that she has neglected the warn- ing. In a case which occurred last week, the parents had advised their daughter to have nothing further to do with the men, on account of his drunken habits, but the advice was disregarded and, in the end he killed both her and himself. It was a melancholy ending for a girl of 20, but it was at any rate far less miserable than her fate would have been if she had married the man from whose savage will there would have been no appeal. Girls who think they know better than their parents may tead in the story of one of Pecksnifi's daughters in" Maitin Chuzzlawit what is likely to happen to them if they neglect their parents counsel, or adopt the hideous fantasy that a man who is cruel or a drunkard will reform when he is married, The Deadly Hatpin.—Owing to a large number of accidents in Paris, caused by hatpins—some of the said pins being nearly half-a-yard in length—the Prefect of Police is said to he contemplating steps that will make it compulsory for every woman to wear a safety-shield or point protector. It is to be hoped that not only will this excellent arrangement come into force in France, but in all other countries where the hatpin is in use. We are always hearing of accidents, more or less serious, caused by this barbarous item, and it is more than time the law stepped in and made the wear- ing of it with the point unprotected a criminal offence. A Becoming Mode.—Possibly there is little more</p>
        <p>becoming in the millinery world then the flat velvet lining to bat brims, and to such an extent have Parisid° milliners carried the idea, that they are even lieing hat brims in this particular way. When objections artr raised to the entire lining, the brims are simply bound with the velvet, this notion being followed when the most airy-fairy toilettes are under consideration. BY the way, the flat cloche shape—so many of us at the moment possess—can easily be brought up-to-date, bY simply turning up the brim sharply on the left side. The Tiresome Lautalress.—There would appear to be a large measure of success awaiting a laundry run IV" less energetic and violent lines than has been the csse. for many years past. For instance there seems, pitiful dearth of people capable of ironing a shi:2' without thrusting the necessary irnplen eat thron,,u delicate flounces, of the "getting-up" a blouse without nulling it out of shape, flattening the hooks, breaking the buttons, end otherwise working taro° with the garment generally. Another failing of the lapndress, is leaving ironmould marks round hooks and eyes. 1"w. with a little care this can be avoided, as the sensible girl or woman skilled in fine ironing knows full well' All that is necessary is, That the garment should not "e to damp when rolled up, and that an old hancikerchiefa cr cloth of some kind should be laid over the hooks eyes before rolling. Further, a garment bearing hooks and eyes should not be allowed to retrain too loug before being ironed. When, 'however, marks of iron -- mould are an unhappy fact, the best plan is to take some spit its of salts, diluted in water, and dab upon the spots, rinsing the parts immediately several tirafs,ll;' cold water. Many women and wisely, nowadays Prete!, to st nd " tub" clothes to the dry clearer, who. more expensive is much more careful of . althoug.the goods entrusted to him, and therefore economical in the lorg: run. Sweet Lavender.—" Lavender, sweet lavender" with us again and it is 'therefore well to remember the; there are other tees this fragrant flower can le put t, beside keing stowed away in na.slin Ulf s, 8r01.35 linen and wearing apparel. For example a very deslr:. able toilet water can le made by steeping handfuls et dried lavender in white wine and vinegar, and leavieg. it in stone jars upon a warm stove for about three dtc.rs, It should then be strained when the liquid is readY be bottled and med. Another notion—a very delight, ful one—is to rub the dried lavender flowers to powder, and mix this with the down or feathers when On: cushions. Two lining, by the way, are necessary vLei, this plan is followed, or the lavender dust will through. Many women add died and ground ro_sre. leaves, clover, sweet geranium and lemon, with th lavender when filling sachets' such mixture being e` particularly sweet and delightful one.</p>
        <p>MADGr._</p>
      </div>
