      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKS ASSOCIATION. ANNUAL HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>The annual handicap in connection with his Associa- tion opened en Saturday when the following games were played : J. Bandies - lO J. Looker l5 P. McGrath 4 S. Massey, jun. W. Bristow l5 H. Savory 8 R. Jefferson A. Gleave Jas. Wrench G. Savage A. Thornley l5 H. Daintith B. Tomlinson l5 C. Newton l3 W. Bibby l5 W. Bandies P. Green l5 C. Taylor l3 W. Andrews l5 W. Jones W. Roughley J.Skelhorne l5 F. Walker.. 8 T. O'Brien G. Shaw B. Webb J. Warburton W. Hall l5 J. Welsby ll J. Warburton W. Shaw R. 13robbin W. Si ca ister l5 G. Wilcooks 7 J. Stewart • 15 J. Webb 8 W. Clare ' H. Cook l5 J. Watts 14 W. Ryder l5 J. Melbourne J. Lloyd W. Thomason . R. Dagnall A. Middleton J. Boughley l5 B. Lowe Ike Barlow J. Gilbert A. Shaw l5 S. Williams 9 J. Woods l5 J. Potter F. Gauntz l5 D. Davies l2 A. Shelmerdine G. Maddock l5 A. Perry A. Burdett J. Meakin l3 J. Clare A. Broadhurst l5 J. Davies J. Fianagon l5 E. Warburton l2 G. Clive l5 P. Appleton 7 W. 11. Philips w.o. T. Gregsoa, absent G. Millington l5 A. Ball B. Reagon w.o. 141, ICenwright, absent 0. Hatton l5 A. Warburton • J. Bowyer 15 H. Holbrook J. Lyons __ _ ___ __</p>
        <p>~ S Riley l5 G. Li!ley T. Johnson M. Bart,n l5 Bromley, seri. W. Horrocks H. Halford J. Richardson Jack Woods l5 M. Barlow FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>CLEAVE FAMILY V. HARRISON FAMILY..—The return match between the Gleave family, of Warring- ton, and the Harrison family of Mossley Hill was decided at Mosslev Hill on Saturday. In the match at Warrington on August Bank Holiday the Gleaves triumphed by 16 points. In the return game which was watchea by a good number of spectators, they were defeated by four, but on the aggregate they have a majority of 12. Scores : J. Harrison Herbert Harrison 2l J. Gleeve F. Harrison l6 T. Cleave Jos. Harrison jun, l4 W. Cleave H. Harrison T. Harrison 2l A. Gleave ....... .......... 6</p>
        <p>222 Majority for Cenietery, 6.</p>
        <p>21 1G 21</p>
        <p>_ After the match, the cup was presented to Mr. A. Scott, the captain of the Cemetery team by Mr. Robert Williamson, vice-plesident of the association, in the absence of Mr. Coombs, the president, who had to leave. Mr. WILLIAMSON hear Lily congratu!ated the winners and also gave the losers credit for the magnificent struggle they had waged. Mr. Scorr said there was not a prouder man en the green than he gas. (Loud applause.) They had set their hearts on winning the cup after losing in last year's final to another Warriugton team (Bull's Head) and to their great delight they had at length succeeded. Mr. Crosfield, 1.P., he'was sure 'would be delighted to present him v ith the cup when the cup and medals were prerented at the annual dinner. (Applause.) Mr. T. PRESCOTT, president of Leigh Railway, said the match had been conducted in such an admitable manner that Leigh bad no need to go lack ashamed. ( Applause.) 'I he 7 had been beaten by a better team. As a Lancashire man he liked holding up his hand when beaten. (Loud spplause.) Mr. ARTHUR JONES moved a hearty vote of thanks to the referee (Mr. J. W. G. Coombs) and the measurers (Messrs. R. Witliamaon and J. S. Kay) and this was seconded by Mr. G. BALL, jun., and carried.</p>
        <p>, LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE COUNTY neutralised, however, by J. Grundy, one of Leigh's , ASSOCIATION FINAL. old stagers, who beat Alf Scott 21-11. WARRINGTON V. LEIGII RAILWAY.—After working ---- - into the final for the Crosfield cap and championship Matters took % more favourable hue from War- of the County Association, these clubs met on the grem rington's standpoint, both J. Woods and T. Harrison of the George Hotel, Prestwich, on S tturday afternoon, winning by the odd one, the latter having played the When the last pair entered the green the teams were, best game of his side up to then. H. Harrison did not almost level. The Lancashire champion, Tom Glover, do so well at the stirt, as J. Gleave, cousin to the and the ex-champion, Tom Ellison, were opposed to web.' known players, Albert and Joe Gleave, led 7-1. each other. The champion got to 16-9, and from this point Ellison treated the spectators to a fine piece of Harry, however, had a run 0f412, and keeping up his bowling. He ran away to 20-17, only to have a good form ran out a winner by 8, and this gave Leigh an wood along with the jack knocked into the gutter. advantage of 5. Glover addei a couple, peeling at 20, and he finished . one point ahead of the ex-champion. Ellison's fine effort gave Warrington the charupiontlip by fi,-e points. Scores : WARRINGTON CEMETERY. LEIGH RAILWAY. - J. Woods 2l A. Mole 2O A. Scott (captain) ll J. Grundy 2l G. Bill, sen T. Harrison J. Gle4ve l3 H. Harrison ' 2l G. Ball, jun. T. 11mphreys 2l R. Smith .1. Ashton 2l T. Cross " A. J. Jones - A. Newton R. Roth%% ell 2l A. Boon T. Bolton 2O J. Harrison T. Ellison</p>
        <p>The next game reversed the position for J. Ashton bowled in magnificent form against T. Cross. End after end he was touching the jack, and he deserves all credit for his splendid exhibition. From 7-1 he went to 15-5, and finished up the best winner of the day, 21-7.</p>
        <p>Warrington thus secured the lead. and maintained it throughout. A brilliant effort was made by G. Ball, jun', who was badly behind with J. Rothwell, who was badly behind with J. Rothwell, who bowled in extraordinary fashion on a short round peg mark, and at the 10th end he led 16-7. At this point Ball had almost a clear run and eventually won.</p>
        <p>Leigh reduced this lead by the help of A.Renyon,and they still had their champion, T.Glover, who in the last game opposed T. Ellison. Ellison at one period of his gprie appeared hopelessly beaten, but on getting possession of the jack he gradually overhauled the Leigh man, and left him standing at 16 whilst he ran Up to 20. As a " striker " Glover has no equal, and with a well-judged shot he took the ex-champion clean, off the jack when he lay up with a toucher, finally winning by the odd point. But Ellison's run from 7 to 20 gave Warrington the championship,</p>
        <p>The cup was then formally presented to the cAptain of the winning team, A. Scott, by Mr. R. Williamson, vice-president of the Lancashire and Cheshire Associa- tlon.</p>
        <p>Prior to the match Mr. J. W. G. Coombs, deputy president, said that Mr. Crosaeld, M.P., the generous donor of the cup and medals, was unable to be present, though he was sure he would have been delighted to see so large an attendance.</p>
        <p>The Wariington Works Association handicap made an auspicious start last Saturday and it will be resumed to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, August 21, 1909</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 21, 1909</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Good luck to Warrington in their all-important match at St. Helens to-day ! As we have already pointed out, much depends upon this encounter with the Recs. If Warrington win they become the proud holders for twelve months of the West Lancashire Cup and local enthusiasts are all anxious that they shall win the trophy. If the result is a draw War- rington will have to meet Prescot in a final bid for the honours, and if the Warringtot,ians lose—unhappy thought—they will be relegated to the second position in the League table. That they will return .home victorious to-day is the fervent hope of their supporters.</p>
        <p>The following are the League tabb s up-to-date : FIRST DIVISION. Club. P. W. L. D. Pts. l'rescot Warrington tit. Helens Recs. St. Helens lO ... 3 ... 4 ... 3 ... 9 E2r lestown Net on Two points deducted for playing ineligible player. SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Prescot</p>
        <p>_ St. Helens Rees IVarrington Earlestown Newton St. Helens</p>
        <p>Warrington journeyed to Werneth with a weak team on Saturday and they were fortunate in making a draw. Werneth had first 'turn at the ,wicket and declared after making 165 for seven wickets. War- rington had a lithe over ninety minutes in which to save the game and they had put together 109 with 8 wickets down when time arrived. The feature of the' Werneth innings was a dashing display by J. Chester who had made 72 when he was magnikently caught by Partridge.</p>
        <p>That promising young player S. Partridge was Warrington's most successful batsmen. He hit out freely all round the wicket and placed 39 to his credit.</p>
        <p>Although weakened by the demands of the first eleven, Warrington second had much the better of the draw with Werneth second. A. E. Walker (35) and J. Duckworth (30) were in grand form with the bat.</p>
        <p>St. James's are once more the champions of the Warrington and District League, and all honour is clue to them for their brilliant play throughout the season. Tliey have not lost one of their 14 matches, and have_ only drawn 1. On Saturday they achieved another fine victory at the expense of Sankey Wire Mills second. J. Hallam wielded the willow in fine style for his 34, and also bowled effectively, taking five wickets for 16 runs. Green captured four for 22. J. H. Constable (26) and F. Tole (24) batted in good style. C. Hawthorn (19) was the top scorer for the Wire Mills.</p>
        <p>There was a keen match between Walker's Brewery and Rylands Bros., the wire men winning by two runs. Bamber(l9) headed the -score list.Rylance accounted for seven wickets for 29 runs and McGowan took four for 21.</p>
        <p>The "Guardian " eleven had considerably the better of their encounter with Latchford Baptists, H. Paton (17) and H. Scott (16) being the highest scorers. Clarke took four wickets for nine runs and Paton three for seven. For the Baptists Fletcher captured eight for 30.</p>
        <p>Whitecross Recreation triumphed over Wilderspool Recreation in a match of low scores. Howard with three wickets for 22 mils and Lloyd two for 13 bowled finely for Wilderspool, while Jones with four wickets for seven runs and Wright four for nine, did good work for the Recreation.</p>
        <p>• A Junior League match between Stretton and Appleton and Sankey, ended in victory for the latter, Bingham (15 not out) and Bradburn (14) being the only batsmen to reach double figures. Foster captured five wickets for ten rims and Webb five for eleven.</p>
        <p>Lymm were in a scoring mood on Saturday and trounced Ashley by 108 runs to G. T. Marsh batted brilliantly for his 39 and J. liampson was also in capital form, knocking up 27.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall journeyed to Runcorn and were triumphant by a majority of 20. T. P. Baxter (24) batted well for the home side, and R. Gifford (37) and J. Burrows (2.1) did splendid service for Grappenhall, Watson accounted for six wickets at a cost of 43 runs. and A. Fenton took three for nine.</p>
        <p>An encounter between Arley and Grappenhall resulted in a draw, but the Grappenhall eleven's prospects could be considered rosy. J. Mottram, who carried out his bat with 15 to his credit, was the most successful batsman for Grappenhall and H. Hart (20), made the best display for Arley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>Bravo Warrington Cemetery! All local trundlers will congratulate them on winning the Lancashire and Cheshire Club championship after a tough struggle with Leigh Railway. They thuS become the holders for twelve months of the handsome cup presented by their president, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.. The trophy thus comes, to Warrington for two years in succession, for last year the Bull's Head were victorious. Leigh Railway had won the cup five times, and made strenu- ous efforts to regain it. There was a grim tussle, but Warrington had the satisfaction of winning by six points.</p>
        <p>Our veteran, G. Ball, played as well as ever, and against the junior of the Leigh team, C. Baines, ran from 10-2 to 18-8, and eventually landed home a winner by 11 points. This win was irfactically</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Swimming.</head>
        <p>Swimming.</p>
        <p>Swiramipg enthusiasm has considerably revived lately, owing to the warm weather, and now the annual gala is in sight, many miniature handicaps and racei are taking place amongst the intending entrants.</p>
        <p>The local team have had a nice spell of rest, since their encounter with Woolton on July 23rd. The next match will be decided next Tuesday, when they will play Birkenhead at Warrington.</p>
        <p>This match should not be missed, as in addition to this, the works squadron races will be swum between the Co-operative Stores (holders) and Crosfield's, and Rylands Bros. and Corporation.</p>
        <p>The gala night has been fixed for September 23rd,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Rugby Football</head>
        <p>Rugby Football</p>
        <p>Warrington are getting into stride for the opening of the season and the practice matches on Saturday and Tuesday evenings were largely attended. In the latter game Frawlev, Warrington's new Australian three-quarter, and brooks had the bud luck to be damaged, but it is hoped they will be fit again for the match with Leigh.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday I had a chat with Daniel Frawley, one of Warrington's Australian captures, who shoulki. prove a great acquisition to the team. He was for the time being a prisoner, the injury to his ankle sustained in Tuesday's practice match making it impossible for him to venture abroad. Frawley is a well-built young man, of cheerful, open countenance, and, his impressions of Warrington and of his new club mates up to the present appear to be entirely favourable. He is twenty-five years of age and comes from Sydney, New South Wales. He told me that he had been going in for football seriously for the past five years and that he has played for the Eastern Suburbs team, to which Messenger also belonged. Frawley has .played wing threequarter practically all .his time, although he gas taken the centre position at a pinch. He came to England last season with the Kangaroos and holds the second.best scoring record for the tour. Just before leaving home this time he played in the match between Australia and New Zealand and. scored three tries. He and Rosenfeldt had a most enthusiastic " send-off " on leaving Sydney. They experienced a very rough passage so there was little chance of training on board.</p>
        <p>Frawley speaks hopefully of the prospects of the Northern Union g,aqie in Australia. "All they want out there," he remarked, "is a fit St class Northern Union team." As showing the interest uhich the game arouses he pointed out that there was an attend- adce of 30,000 at the match between New. Zealand and New South Wales. From what he has seen of English football, however, he considers it to be far ahead of the play in his colony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON NURSING ASSOCIATION Successful Year's Working.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON NURSING ASSOCIATION Successful Year's Working.</p>
        <p>Tbat very us:=ful and deserving Institutien the Warrington District Nursing Association—held its 12th annual meeting in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall on Monday evening, for the purpose of receiving the report and the balance sheet. The Association during the past year has continued its noble work with undiminished enthusiasm, and although at the end of June a balance of £l2O 17s. id. was in hand, subscriptions aro urgently required to widen tho scope of its operations. The meeting was presided over by the Mayor (Alderman W. J. Forshaw J.P.,) and amongst those present were: Alderman J. Smethurst, Coun- cillors J. Fulton-Smith, H. Roberts (hon. treasurer), Rev. John Yenge, Colonel J. D. Fairclough (hon. secretary), Niessrs. J. H. Smethurst, J. Green, J. Fargher, H. Hope, W. T. Williams, and J. Fairclongh. A letter of apology from Mr.W. P. Rylands was read. The MA von said he understood that that excellent* Association bad been in existence for 12 years and that during the past year the cases visited totalled 14,499, while the number of the cases nursed were 631. Such figures proved that it was a. most valuable association and one that certainly ought to 131 supported and encouraged. lie was pleased to be able to say that under the able treasurership of Mr. Roberts the sub- scriptions and donations this year had been consider- ably increased. The Mayor concluded by s tying that they all wished the Association every possible success and God speed. (Applause). The report of the Committee recorded high apprecia- tion of the excellent work carried out by the Superintendent and Nursing Staff during the past year and thanked all those who by subscriptions, donations and gifts of various kinds had helped id conferring the benefits of the institution upon so many very needy and deserving cases. The report of the Superintendent showed that 631 cases were relieved and 14,499 visits paid by their nurses during the year, as compared vr ith 487 cases and 12,787 visits during the preceding year. Early in April last Miss Trunell resigned her position as superintendent, in order that she might take a course of study for the purpose of qualifying herself in a very important branch of her profession. The Committee, took the opportunity of thanking her for the courteous and assiduous manner in which she invariably carried out the duties of her position. They also desired to bespeak for Miss Milne (the new-appointed Superin- tendent) the confidence and sympathy of all friends of the Institution, which they felt would bo fully justified by her faithful and capable discharge of the responsi- bilities devolving upon her. The Hos. TREASURER (Mr. H. Roberts) reported as follows :—The very generous donation of Mr. W. Johnson of £62 148.1 d. wiped out the adverse balance of .the year ending June 30th, 1908. The income of the Association, however, has for a long time been much below the normal annual expenditure, but, in response to the appeal made some months ago, I am glad to report that the annual subscriptions have increased from £230 ss. ed. in 1907-8 to £316 14s. Gd. in the year just. closed. The Workingmen's Committee have done splendidly, the amount which they have paid over to me through their excellent Treasurer, Mr. 11. Hope, having increased from £199 19s. 11d. in 1907-8 to .4:279 is. 2d. for the past year. Congregational Collec-</p>
        <p>tions, &amp;c., have gone up from £27 its. 11d. in 1907-8 ta £5B 17s. sd. in 1908-9. It must be pointed out, how- ever, that the congregation of Cairo-street Caapel have sent 1113 the procesds of two collections mule during the year—one of which was announced by mistake to be made for our Association. There are still many churches an 1 chapeis lilt included in our list of sup- porters. I hope some if nit all, of these may be able to help in future. Various Societies and friends have sent us donations out of the proceeds of performances and other funds amounting to £33 us. 81. Other personal' donations have increased from £5 21. 103. in 1997-S to £l7 2s. While the yetr has closed with a balance in favour of the Association of £l2O 17s. 11—a most gratifying fact—l must point out that so large a proportion of the am-runt received is the result of special efforts of various kinds, which we cannot expect to be repeated every yew., that it is absolutely essential to add to our list of aaaual s ibscri iera, if the increasing demand.; upon the services of our Nursing Staff are to be aderria'ely met. 1 desire to offer my sincere thsn'is to all the subscriber, to the Werkingmea's Comrnittee,and to those who have in so many other directions assisted in bringing ribint the present satisfactory conditions ef the fininces of the Association.</p>
        <p>I The Rev. JOHN YONGE propased the resolution' that the annual v.-ports an] Eta' eatents of acclunts for the year ending June 30th, 1909, be approved and adoptei." IHe said the fl:ittres treat to shsw that the institution oas gro ing tha confidence both of the doctors of the town and of the inhabitants, as they found a greater number of cases sent to the nurses by the medical men Referring to the increased contribution from the Workingmea's Committee Mr. Yonge said he really di I not know what the Infirmary and the Association would do if it wsre not for the Workingmen. Continu:ng, the rev. gentleman said that of course as time rent on they mould need more money than they even did now for the work was becoming greater. He was sure Mr. Roberta rejoiced in the fact that through his earnest efforts he had brought. the Society out, of dast into a balance in hand. (Applause). The increase in the needs of the Ins.titution celled for the increase in the amounts received in support of it, and he most earnestly trusted th,.t the appeal of the SJciety made would be responded to in the most generous aay on every hand. He lines, it was very difficult to gst more money from the churches and chapels for the Institution, owing tb special collections for many objects, but he sincerely hoped that they of the churches and chapels would get a special day on which collections could be made for the Association. lie saw that there had been received .£5B 7s. sd. from the congregations. It did not strike cne as if they realized their responsibility in the matter and he hoped that the clergy, ministers and officers of the chnrches would take the matter into more serious con- sideration and see whether they could not in some way make a response. (Applause). Mr. HOPE, in seconding, slid that there was a' doubt that the poorest. of the poor appreciated the value of the Institution. If they could arrange to collect pennies from them every now and again it would be a good source of income in the future, and the poor would be willing to give what they could. . . The resolution was csrried unanimously. Alderman SMETHURST proposed that the following be re-elected :—President,the Right Hon. Lord Lilford; vice-presidents, the Mayor, Rev. Canon Willis, Ziba Armitoge, Esq, J.P., J. J. Meekly, Esq., J.P., W. H. Bleckly, E,q.,J.P.,Lieutenint-Colonel J.D.Buckton,J.P., J. Malkin Edelsten, Esq., J.P., Alderman W. J.Porsh sw, J.P., Wm. Lone, Esq., J.F., F. Monk:, Esq, J.P. F. W. Monk. Esq., JP., C. E. Parker, Esq., J.P., Esq., J.P., Alderman J. Smethurst, J.P., La ly Greenall, Lady Walker, Mrs. Armitage, Mrs. Buckton, Mrs. Crosffeld, Mrs. Frost, Mrs. 11. Greenall, Mrs. Long, Mrs. Lyon, and Mrs. Willis. Committee of Management,: Mrs. Broadbent, Mrs. Carleton, Nfrs. Fairclouoh, Mrs. John Fairclough, Mrs. Harrison, Miss Massy, Mrs. Monks, Mrs. Roberts, Mts. P. Rylands, Mrs. R. Smith, Mts. Yonge, Lieutenant- Co'cnel J. D. Buckton, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. .1. Fairclough, Mr. .1. Fargher, Dr. E. J. Fox, Mr. H. Hope, De. Joseph, Dr.l.angdale, Alderman J. Smethurst, Mr. Geo. Walker, Rev. Father Wilson, Rev. John Yonge. He heartily congratulated their treasurer on the great effort he had put forth, and also Mr. Hope and Mr. Walker, for their extr.iordit.ary interest in the institution. No one kttew the amount of work 'those gentlemen had to do in connection with the recent sports. Ceuncillor FULTON-SMITII, in seconding, said that in view of the great assistance the friendly societies had had ftom the Chancellor of the Exchequer he hoped they would have more money for that Institution. The proposition was carried unanimously. Mr. J. H. SMETHURST proposed that Colonel Fair- dough be re-elected secretary. The success of the Institution was largely due to his unselfish efforts. Mr. FARGHER, in seconding, said he had served them ably and well.</p>
        <p>The proposition was carried unanimously. The CHAIRMAN proposed the re-election of Mr. Henry Roberts as the bon. treasurer. He said he was a good man for collecting funds and he was sure they could not do better than re-elect him. Mr. FAlRcLorcir seconded and it was carried. Mr. ROBERTS replying said he would do the best he could to maintain the Society in as satisfactory condi- tion as it was at the present moment. First of all he must express the deep spmpathy they all felt in the loss which the president of the Society (Lord Lilford) bad recently sustained in the death of his only child. There had not been an opportunity for that Association to give en expression of its feelings before. He there- fore would suggest that the.: secretary convey to Lord Lilford the deep sympathy of the Association. Lord Lilford had been a liberal supporter of that Institution and other Institutions of Warrington. Their late superintendent, (Mies Turnell) had bee- succeeded by Miss Milner and he would like to a, for sympathy and every possible assistance for her. (Hear, hear). nurse less than they were when the institution was at its best. It, was quite probable that the Committee, if it found it desirable, would have an additional nurse. That would entail greater expenditure, and therefore they would need an increased income. The weak spot was that they had to depend so largely upon special efforts for their friends. He would be the last one to go against them. because they were very welcome, but be did want to increase the annual subscribers so that they could absolutely depend upon a certain amount from year to year. lie would like to pay a tribute to the Workingmen's Committee. He had been brought into personal contact with their methods of organisa- tion, and had seen the efforts put forth by them, and he had reason to be thankful. He concluded by proposing a vote of thanks to the Mayor for the manner in which he had occupied the chair that evening, and also for the use of the Town Hall. Mr. FULTON-SMITH seconded and it was carried. The MAYOR replying, said that anything that he could do for that Association he would only be too willing to do it, and if he were 7-layor next year they would he welcome to that room. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>I have now dealt with all the principal points of practical poultry farming. This week I intend con- cluding for the season. In doing so I ask my readers to continue theorectical studies by constant reference to previous articles. In taking a review I find that their is sufficient information to guide my readers through the winter months. PerhaPi some are con- templating operations next season, to thee I urgently recommend a close perusal of back numbers. Accommodation and capital are not always the only essentials required. A sound educational- course in conjunction with frequent visits to other poultry yards is a cheap way of obtaining knowledge. I would advise any who intend going in for poultry to get to know all they can without expenditure. • I don't advise this from a selfish point of view. Generally speaking you will find fellow fanciers and farmers very kind. 'I have never yet been refused admission to any yard I have gone to. And further more I have always come away knowing more about poultry than bithertofore. So, my readers in conclusion, I ask you to read thi§ series of articles through once more, make special note of . anything which specially applies to your requirements and apply it at the proper time. Next season I intend re-opening the poultry column, I suppose I had better say March, as this is the real beginning of the breeding season in .general. What I want my,readers to do in the meantime is, to recom- mend the poultry columns to their friends. I have to thank ;nany people for the kind interest already shown. I have frequently heard of satisfactory results as a consequence of following the articles. This is very pleasing to me, and I hope still more profitable to my readers.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failure's recordei in England and Wa!es during the week ending Friday, August 6th, was 124, viz. :—New Bunkruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 59 ; Deeds of . Arrangement registered, 65. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year : Bankruptcies, 55; Deeds of Arrangement, 53—Total 103; being an decrease of 16. This total number of Commercial Failures recoried during the 32 weeks of the present year is 4,895; the total number recorded in the corresponding 32 weeks of last year was 5,191, showing a decrease of 293.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>BABY'S gni – CASE Of ECZEMA</head>
        <p>BABY'S gni - CASE Of ECZEMA</p>
        <p>Terrible Humour from Head to Foot —Had to be Wrapped in Cotton Wool—Discharged Uncured After Three Months in Infirmary— Scratched Until Bandages were Soaked with Blood,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DREADFUL DISEASE CURED BY CUTICURA</head>
        <p>DREADFUL DISEASE CURED BY CUTICURA</p>
        <p>"My little boy is barely two years old, and when he was about eight months old he had to undergo an opera- tion and have something taken out of his face. But when the time came for the operation to take place he had broken out with eczema to such an ex- tent that he' was one mass from head to foot and had to be wrapped in cotton wool. He was for three,months in the - Infirmary under three doctors. They managed to get him sufficiently well to operate on him, but could not cure him, and at the end of three months turned him out with his shoulders and top parts of his poor little arms one mass of ti•iies scabs, so bad that they sent splints with him in case they would be needed. The worst places were, unfor- tunately, just where all the weight of his clothes hung on them, and one had only to look at the child to know what he suffered. Though I put bandages on at night, he managed to rub or scratch them till he was soaked in blood and corrupt matter in the morning, and the rags had to be made wet to get them off, as they were stuck fast to the places. I get one each of Cuticura Soap, Cuti- cura Ointment and Cuticura Resolvent. From the very first application he began to mend, and in less than a fortnight he was quite clear and now, at the end of a few weeks, it is scarcely possible to tell where the places were. He cannot say many words, but I was quite touched when he cnce showed his arms, telling me "it had all gone," and the subject had not been mentioned. It has quite set him up and he is getting quite a nice strong boy, not a bit like he Was. I have recommended Cuticura to several suffering with eczema. Mrs. F. Webb, Alderton, nr. Towcester, Northampton- shire, Eng., July 3, 1908."</p>
        <p>Complete External and Internal Treatment for Every Humour of Infants, Children and Adults con- sists of Cuticura Soap to Cleanse the Skin, Cutlenra Ointment to Heel the Stat and Cuticura Resolvent Pills to Purify the Blood. A SinVe Set often Cures. Sold throughout the world. Depots: London. 27, Charterhouse Sq.: Paris. Rue de La Palx: Aus- tralia, It. Towns &amp; Co.. Sydney: So. Africa, Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town, etc.; U. S. A.. Potter Drug Chem. Corp.. Boston. WI-Post-free from London depot, sample of Cuticura, with book on skin diseases</p>
      </div>
