      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, AUGUST 21st. 1909.</p>
        <p>BACON observed that OPPOSITION there are men who are LOGIC. - such self-lovers that they will set their neighbour's houses on fire to roast their own in the embers. This may have been 'lle origin of CHARLES LAMB'S essay on (11.13ast pig. But LAMB'S Chinaman had the :eeelloY to set his own house on fire when he b`lished to have his pig nicely roasted. We allot help thinking that the wealthy etl3Ponents of the Budget are following ,4:1113's principle rather than BACoN's. They ilsh to set their neighbour's house on fire 4'14 is evident. But they are really burning their own. ,It is frequently represented that the House .:31 Lords represents, not only the wealth ,o°fllt the intelligence of the country. Neither Clefse statements could be successfully „sended in argument. First, there are 'B4IY enormously wealthy Commoners. The ds for the second have been wholly ePt away by the manner in which some the most intelligent of the Peers have ~'Plained their attitude in regard to new 'axatiOn. Equally wanting in logic is the ,411/.se that is being pursued by the friends the great landowners in the House of tlietrinions. Their excuse -is, of course, that eY belong to the party of which Lord ,(Ileturn 4CoNsFIELD was the head, and it was a ,a of that eminent statesman that we te not governed by logic. Obviously we •Ze not. If we were governed by logic, the °llse of Commons would on Wednesday _ea've escaped a long, trying, techni- tal debate on the subject of the valua- •,tl°ll of land. For what is the situation? 1411 e Lords say that they are goiug to "do their duty" in regard to the land clauses of e Pinance Bill, and particularly in respect ° the valuation of land. ItThoing one's duty" is a vague phrase. abllsnally means doing something disagree- le, and doing it with a relish. What is :clerstood in this instance is that the Lords 13te screwing their courage up to the sticking with the view of cutting certain uallses out of the Bill. They are being urged this course by the leading men on the ieellservative benches, and by some of the Ita.cling organs of the Conservative Press. 18 contended that the valuation clauses are linlllething extraneous, that they really do „et belong to a Finance Bill, that they are iltacked on," in fact, for a revolutionary ,511113013 e. Very well. Let us admit this for a ,47nent. Let us suppose that the CHANCELLOR EXCHEQUER has been guilty of the jeadful offence of " tacking." What should le the line taken by the Opposition under' :eh circumstances? Surely it should be tre,t,ermined by the not unreasonable resolve 11; leave the matter entirely in the hands .of e }louse of Lords. If the Lords are going 2 throw out any portion of the Budget Bill, to throw out the Bill as a while. because ;I Certain of its portions, their friends in the ionuse ought, if we really were governed by to refrain from any such discussion of -thet.alls as must make them parties to any- that the House of Commons may Ilei.terniine upon. Yet practically the whole of ednesday's discussion was concerned with Points about valuation as ought not to 13a\re been brought up at all unless the ,tilloiple of valuation had already been eepted. in fact, is what has already taken „tlia'ee• The Opposition in the House of Com- -41(3°s does not believe that it can depend on Peers, and on that ground it is taking Possible precautions for the protection of ° landowners. It is a situation full of the aterials for humour.</p>
        <p>The cost of a total :&amp; CONTENTION valuation of the land in TIIAT MEANS the United Kingdom, MISCHIEF, with the exception of tor 4 land held by statutary Panies, has been computed by the Inland :41,Venue Board, Mr. Asquith has stated, to qpint to an initial expenditure of two Ab„"ns, with a subsequent annual charge of • £150,000. The Opposition set them- Nues on Tuesday to challenge the expert l'es• That was rather a futile proceeding. e Serious was the contention which they 11,38 ed that the House of Commons had constitutional right to impose a tax 10 ess its yield in a given year was exceed the cost of collection. It 'ztie: contention in which the Opposition mischief. All over the country the liest enthusiasm for the Budget is Iths:ePing away, like a cobweb, the pretext had been sought for the slaughter of inance Bill in the House of Lords, on 'of 4g.jound that it ran contrary to the wishes electorate which sent the present °lll/I-lent into power. A new pretext has</p>
        <p>been invented, and the Opposition are practi- cally clinging to it as their last hope—the pretext that there is a constitutional flaw in the Finance Bill, since valua- tion has nothing to do with finance. It is- not likely that public opinion, firmly oonvinced of the justice and usefulness of the Budget, will attach any importance to this pseudo-constitutionalism, But Mr. Asquith has done well to give a thorough exposure to its hollowness and perverse misinterpretation of the real principles upon which the control of the Commons over Finance is based. For the position now occupied by the Opposition is only part of a general campaign for . enlarging the powers of the non-representative at the expense of the representative and elected Chamber. The Lords have been steadily nibbling away at the established privileges of the Commons, filching something here and something else there. But if the claim now put forward were to go unchallenged and undefeated—a thing unthinkable—the especial and inex- pugnable stronghold of the Commons, from the days of the absolute Stuarts, their control over Finance would be demolished. The text-books of constitutional theory and practice would have to jpe re-written, if the dicta of Mr. Balfour and Lord Lansdowne, Mr. Pretyman and Lord Robert Cecil were accepted.</p>
        <p>The new Bridges Corn- WARRINGTON mittee, which now con- BRIDGE. sists of all the mem-</p>
        <p>bers of the Council, held its first meeting on Monday, when it elected Alderman Smethurst chairman. This was a complimentary recognition of the deep interest the Alderman has taken for so many years in the much-delayed Warrington Bridge scheme. As he was chairman of the recently dissolved sub-committee it was thought only fit and proper that he should be chairman of the new Committee, though the Mayor is the usual chairman of the General. Purposes Committee. We learn that the meeting decided to invite com- t•etitive designs for a new bridge. Such designs to be submitted for selection to an engineer. This is the sort of step forward which usually precedes a step backward. How many times in the past dozen years the responsible Committee has invited competi- tive designs we cannot compute, but there must be enough designs somewhere in the archives of the Town Hall to provide bridges over all the rivers in England. We are not really sure whether ferro-concrete has been definitely and irrevocably settled as the structural material, but it is to be presumed that these designs will be for a ferro-concrete bridge.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Agri- PEDIGREE cultural Show is so AND POLITICS. largely indebted to and dependent upon Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart., that one feels almost inclined to excuse the introduction of politics at the post-prandial proceedings on Show- day. But if Sir Gilbert wish-es to talk politics and call it something else, he should be very careful not to misrepresent facts even where no one can get up and challenge his statements. To put matters in a nutshell, Sir Gilbert told the largely agricultural assembly he entertained to lunch on Thurs- day that the land proposals of the Govern- ment would ring the death-knell of pedigree stock-breeding. His actual words were: "How long we shall be able to have any ' pedigree stock in this country it is difficult to say. New burdens put upon land, the breaking up of many large estates, and the cutting up into a general system of small holdings of our large • farms will ring the death-hell of pedigree stock- breeding."</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert added that he did not coesider this was speaking palitics. We do not agree with him. He certainly was talking politics—Tory politics, and bad politics. The Government has been particularly consider- ate of agricultural interests- in framing the land proposals of the Budget and it is not quite playing the game for• Sir Gilbert to suggest that the new" burdens upon land" will break up large estates, and farms, and ruin stock-breeding. The Government is not purblind, and it is to be hoed Sir Gilbert's hearers were free from the same complaint. He raised a similar kind of bogey when the Licensing Bill was before the country, but people are becoming chary of crediting all they are told by large landed proprietors who prophesy ruin for them- selves and the country immediately their nest eggs of unearned increment are taxed for the benefit of the community. We have no more fear of the Government ruining the stock-breeders than have hope of the brewers going out of business. The land and the national thirst can be relied upon to support both classes for a long time yet. Meanwhile more power to the Government, and success to the Walton Show! The two things are not so incompatible as the Knight of Walton would have his tenantry and his guests believe.</p>
        <p>The co-operative move- DANGERS OF ment has received a THE TIME. good deal of praise and a good deal of abuse in its time. But we do think there is one good point about it the existence of which nobody would be bold enough to deny; and that is that it always contains men who are ready to speak out, to warn their fellow-members against the pitfalls which beset them, to tell them frankly where they are exhibiting a tendency to err. At the opening of the Co-operative Festival at the Crystal Palace speeches were made by Mr. Lander, a director of the Wholesale Society, and by that veteran champion of co-partnership Mr. Edward Owen Greening, President of this year's festival, and, moreover, a Warrington man. Each of these gentlemen devoted his time not to mere self-satisfied lip-smacking over increase of capital, increase of member- sjiip, increase of dividends. Instead of that they delivered sober lectures on the need to cherish the moral principles of co-operation. Self-reliance, one of those great principles, was referred to by Mr. Greening as in danger of decay at the present day. We do not know that we quite agree with him there. It is true that all parties are teach- ing men to look more and more upon the State as a protecting mother, but we do not observe that these doctrines weaken the characters of those who preach them, or adhere to them. Nevertheless, it would not be difficult for fancied security to breed laziness, and the warning is not superfluous. Mr. Lander's, however, has a distinct and immediate application. The second great principle of co-operation is consideration for other people, and once such consideration ceases to exist on any large scale away goes 'all that is worth preserving in our social life. Mr. Lander deplored the fact that many people, even so-called trade unionists, either looked upon their co-operative organisation as a mere profit-at-all-costs concern, or else mechanically voted for short hours and high wages for the employees and 'then deserted to other stores, and left their fellows in the lurch. We do not know how prevalent this sort of thing may be; but a spread of it would be disastrous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>1410'FwB iND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>1410'FwB iND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The many friends in Warrington of the Rev. John Francis Matthew, now minister of Glossoli-road Baptist Church,Sheffield,will be interested to learn that on Sunday last he occupied Dr. Clifford's pulpit at Westbourne Park, London. • ** *</p>
        <p>A newspaper correspondent writes under date, August 17th, to say: "I learn from the "Burton Evening Gazette" that, notwith- standing all the calamities that have over- taken, or are impending over, the brewing trade, Messrs. Bass and Co. at their meeting yesterday authorised the .increase of their directors' fees from £4,000 to £5,500.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rockefeller, wiio had only £l,OOO in 1865, is to-day worth' about £140,000,000, says the "Daily Telegraph's New York correspondent. This computation is made by the financial expert of the "New York World," who declares further that the Standard Oil King, if he lives until 1919, and attains his 80th birthday, of which there seems . every chance, will be worth £200,000,000. There must be a considerable amount of unearned increment here, which might be taxed for the:•good of the community.</p>
        <p>* * Even the "Daily Dispatch" has taken to blessing the Government and its works. Like the " Mail " the Manchester journal is usually " agin the Government," but yester- day, in an outburst of candour it said : History holds record of few pieces of work as magnificent and as complex as the achieve- ment of union in South Africa. It is a wonderful performance, ad full of most happy omen for the future." By the way, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., voted in the minority for Mr. Barnes' amendment against the colour bar in the Sonth Africa Constitu- tion Bill, on Thursday evening. * *</p>
        <p>The Budget is booming, and the open-air campaign of the Warrington Progressive Labour ..League continues with unabated success. Monday evening's meeting was well up to the high standard which has been maintained from the start, and the speaker's strong advocacy of the land clauses met with a ready and enthusiastic response from the large audience. The Tories who had the temerity to attack the Budget at an open-air meeting at Lymm on Monday fared very badly. Heckling was the order of the even- ing, and Mr. W. P. Rylands had a most uncomfortable time. The promoters of the meeting wisely refrained from putting any anti-Budget resolution. It would undoupt- edly have been lost by a. large majority.</p>
        <p>* As an evidence of the way in which the Budget is gaining ground even in the City, the London correspondent of the "Liverpool. Daily Post and Mercury" quotes the follow- ing striking instance :—I hear of the case of a wealthy man who took no interest in politics,and who was disposed at first to take the City or stockbrokers' view of the Budget. He stands to pay a good deal more than he now does, and he proceeded to examine him- self the proposals of the Government. The result has been to convert him into an aetive politician. He regards the proposals of the Budget, and especially the land tax, as extremely equitable, and he has just aslced a friend, a member of the Cabinet, to propose him for the National Liberal Club. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith's great speech at Bletchley Park so bristled with "arrows of the chase," which transfixed the fallacies and misrepre- sentations of the Opposition that it is hope- less to try to compress them into a paragraph. His case against the Tariff Reformers has already been mentioned. Perhaps it is most Arth while to recall his pithy summing up of the principles on which the Budget has been framed. The Government has (1) made adequate provision for the extinction of the National Debt; (2) they have had regard,not only to the deficit of the current year, but to prospective liabilities; and (3) they have sought to call upon all persons, classes and individuals, and upon all forms of property and income, to pay, in view of their respec- tive capacities and opportunities, an equitable share.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Chaplin calls upon Mr. Lloyd-George to apologise for his use of the word " black- mail" in referring to the Forringe case. If Mr. Chaplin will read the Limehouse speech carefully, he will find that Mr. Lloyd-George strictly applies that language not to any individual, but to the system of leaseholding which enables such heavy tolls to be levied. But in view of all the cant that is being talked on this matter, it may as well be said at once that though the Duke of Westminster undoubtedly exercised his legal rights in taking this monopoly value, yet he exercised them in such a way as to throw an excessive strain on any system of private ownership in land. The Irish landlords had the strict letter of the law on their side when they evicted the Irish tenants in the famine days. But where are the Irish landlords now? The fact is that every system of land-holding in the world will require tO be exercised with equity as well as legality, even by a Duke. * * *</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society are to •be congratulated upon having at last secured an ideal day for their annual show. For some years the weather has been consistently bad and the show invariably good, but this time both the elements and the show were Al. Although Warrington is not an agricultural community it is • a centre easy of access for people from a rich and wide farming tract, and the annual show is fit to rank with any one day exhibition, particularly in the magnificence of its display of horses. This year the hunters were the great feature and it was the opinion of experts that these classes could not have been beaten at any Show. The Warrington Show is doing a splendid work which is deserving of every encouragement. It is perhaps too much to hope that it will ever pay its way but a few days like Thursday, with 'brilliant sunshine and a gathering of 4,500, should help to put a rosier complexion on the financial outlook.</p>
        <p>Again we shall be hearing much of the fall in the birth-rate. The returns of the Registrar-General show a decline of 1.9 per 1,000 in the quarter ending June 30 as com- pared with the average of the tame period over the preceding ten years. The death- rate, however, is also down by 1.3 per 1,000, so that the figures are not so alarming as they might otherwise be to those who believe with Napoleon . that the nation with the largest population must ultimately be victorious in the struggles of the battlefield. The net effect of the births and deaths during the quarter is to add 135,581 to the number of those dwelling on these islands. That is the population of a very considerable industrial centre springing up in three months. So long as we can increase at that rate there need be little fear of the national future. And let us add that the landlords need have no apprehension about unearned increment so long as 135,000 more people a quarter are crowding upon the land of the country. Values will continue to rise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WALTON SHOW.</head>
        <p>WALTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>FINE WEATHER AND A GRATIFYING SUCCESS</p>
        <p>Remarkable Display ,of Hunters.</p>
        <p>It was an experience as welcome as it is rare, for the Warrington Show to be favoured with ideal weather, but such were the gratifying conditions on Thursday when the twentieth annual show of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society was held in Walton Park. In the course of its history the society has frequently been unkindly treated by the elements, but the members have taken a philosophical view of matters, and have come up smiling year by year in the hope of better things. This time their optimism had its reward. All day long the sun shone brightly, and the -beautifully-situated and spacious grounds at Walton were as pleasant a resort as one could find within such easy reach of Warrington or, indeed, of most industrial towns. From an early hour in the morning there was an almost endless procession of vehicles conveying visitors to the show ground, and the turnstiles clicked mer; ily. Agricultural experts came from a wide radius, but the bulk of the I spectators were Warringtonians attracted not so much</p>
        <p>well supported as one could have 'wished, and the most striking feature was the parade of heavy horses (not for competition) by Messrs. Peter Walker and Sous, Ltd. The entries of cattle were not quite up to the average, lint the quality was excellent. The arrangements on the show ground left nothing to be desired and not the slightest hitch occurred to mar theday's proceedings. The competition was so keen—the leading breeders in the country sending of their best stock—that judging was no easy matter, and. it was almost seven o'clock when tie last awards were made. The society was fortunate in having the services of the Earl of Orkney as one of the judges in the hunter classes. A popular adjunct of the Agricultural Shaw was the annual Walton Flower Show which was inaugurated by Lady Greenall and' is making wonderful progress. The famous band of the "Queen's Own Yorkshire Dragoons' was in attendance and submitted a varied and high class programme in an eminently capable and pleasing manner. One of the leading items was Tschaikowsky's overture solonelle " 1812 " which was given With thrilling effect, and the programme also included several other favourite overtures. Lieutenant S. Suckley conducted</p>
        <p>and won general approval for the skilful way in which he directed the musical forces under his control. Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall, whose interest in the success of the show is so much appreciated by agricul- turists, were on the show ground during the whole of the day and their two little sons also seemed to derive keen pleasure from' the proceedings. The glorious weather had, of course, its effect on the attendance which numbeied about 4,500, a considerable advancd on last year. Altogether the 1909 show will be remembered as one of the best in the history of a society whose traditions are of the highest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WATCHING TIIE JUMPING</head>
        <p>WATCHING TIIE JUMPING</p>
        <p>by any critical interest in the stock as by the oppor- I Tie foals were wonderful, and a well-known expert tunity presented to while away a few hours amid. ventured the opinion that they were equal to any shown picturesque surroundings and hear the choice music at any exhibition this year. Mr. James Gould, or mm' again achieved a big series of successes In the of a military band. From small beginnings the blirrenma re class he secured first prize w‘ Holme Warrington Show has attained a standing in the Lacy Starlight, a handsome nine year old bay of good agricultural world which is most gratifying and one build. This was the first time she had been shown this can always rely upon seeing some of the best horses 3 ear, but last year she won at the Cheshire Show, in the kingdom assembled there. This year's display Altrincham Birkenhead, and also gained first in the was no exception to the rule, indeed critics declared district and open classes at Walton Show, as well as that it was the finest show of horses of the three lead- Sir Gilbert Greene' I's cup. The same owner frig types that has been seen this season. While the gained the verdict in the brood mare with foal class aggregate entries were about forty below the last with Springs Darling, a Food lengthy mare, and with her was the champion fiily foal. The latter is a superb show, the total of 372 could nut be considered as animal,and won Sir Gilbert Greenall's cup. Her mother other than satisfactory. The falling off was occasioned also won the district champion cup for the best mare, chiefly by the fact that the classes for shires were con- the property of residents in the society's district. fined to the Society's district,but this restriction had no In the colt foal class * Mr. Gould was again detrimental effect on the quality of the horseflesh to the foie with _a _ fine four months bay exhibited, for had the classes been open it is doubtful athe samwal IwtaswaCsonasnideeraesd whether any horses would have been forthcoming to beatit° rioaarin h for the owner, Mr. Billington, of Dutton, being the winning shires. Warrington show is famed for its placed second. The class for filly foals was well con- -1411 Ye winnernm smartestCornqcuoe hunters and these classes made a magnificent show— tested, and again Mr. Gould was returned the winner quite the best which has been seen at Walton. The with LTIIIM quality. A fine bay colt Lymm Minstrel classes or local tradesmen's turnouts were not as gained iurther hononrs for Mr. Gould, and looks like making a high class shire animal, being possessed of good legs and feet. In the yearling filly class Mr. Lewis Dodd was placed first with a nice bay, and was closely followed by Mr. Thomas Smith's bay. Mr. Wright's bay filly Model, by Mr. Gould's Lymm Lion, was declared the winner in the two-year-old gelding or filly class, and she is a well built animal of which more will be heard in the future. The four-year-old gelding class was easily won by Mr. Peter Davies, of Warburton, with a really handsome bay of five years, Midlands Eitg. He is the stable companion of Midlands Boss, who was champion in London last year. The winner of the class is a splendid specimen, bang possessed of good liras and a splendid hover. The cattle classes were small but of grand quality, many of the animals -being equal to those shown at the Royal. The dairy cows were particularly good. in the class for dairy cow in calf, Mr. Dan Massey showed a fine upstanding and weighty animal. This was the first time he had exhibited her, and he gained premier honours, being closely followed by Mr. S. S. Raingill, a well-known exhibitor. The latter, however, succeeded in gaining first and second prizes in the class for dairy cows in milk. Heifers were poorly represented, but the quality was.maintained, and the same remark applies to the bull classes. In the class for bulls (over two years) Mr. S. W. Gould was awarded the first prize but a pro- test was lodged and upheld by the Council,' with the result that Mr. Ralph Tickle was placed first. The duties of judges were discharged by the follow- ing :—Hunters, the Earl of Orkney and Mr. Walter C.</p>
