      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>FEATURES OF THE SHOW.</head>
        <p>FEATURES OF THE SHOW.</p>
        <p>The outstanding feature of the show was undoubtedly the display of over ninety hunters, which is probably un'que. The, best horses in England were there, and they were carefully and strictly: judged by men who have got the courage of their opinions. It would pro- bably be imgossible to find an equally good class of hunters in the saddle at any other show. They took ft great deal of placing, and Mr. W. A. Simpson- Hinchcliffes's champion of England (Broadwood) a six-year-old broad gelding, had to meet such excellent company that it took some time to reach the top place. The hunter brood classes were local</p>
        <p>and it was a pleasure to see them -so well supported by farmers with animals bred on the land and not merely exhibited by ' professional exhibitors. The hackneys, although probably not on quite as high a level as in previous years, contained some representative animals which have competed at leading shows and the corrpetition, especially in the harness classes, was very keen. One of the principal successes was Sir Lees Knowles' splendid black chestnut, Knowle Halrna, which is very familiar in the leading show rings. Mr. John Makeague's Golborne hackney, Flora Hastings, was a good second to it, the third fall- ing to Sir Lees Knowles. The baronet had to be content with third in the three-year-old gelding or filly class, Mr. Makeague's brown filly, Satire, taking the , eyes of the judges. A notable success was secured in the class for pony brood mares—a very nice lot,; by the way—by Miss M. E. Knott, of Hooton. The harness classes, open, were a strong department, and it was gratifying to notice the _names of several new exhibitors which were much needed. The mares or geldings exceeding 14 bands produced a fine contest, and at the sime time a surprise, Mr. William Foster's Mel-Valley's TIE si ngton Belief experiencing her first defeat from Mrs. Edwin Goad's Westerham Pick-em-up, from the South Of England. There was no questior, however, as to the superiority of the latter. Ridden round the ring by a lady, Webter- ham Pick-'em-up hat, a magnilictnt knee and bock action, and moved with fine spirit and grace. The popular mare, Fylde Sabrinetta, the property of Mr. V. S. Cunard, did not show her best form, and was consequently not well placed. The shires were a splendid lot and a credit to the district, affording striking evidence of what can be done locally if proper encouragement is given.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. PALMER, Lymm, (Chairman of the Committee).</p>
        <p>Tinsley; hackneys and harness, 'Mr. C. W. Wilson and Mr. T. A. Jackson- shires, Mr, C. W. Tindall and Mr. John Taggart ; tradesmen's or tenant farmers' turnouts, Mr. C. E. Greenall and Mr. Frank Lloyd; jumpers, Mr. A. C. Burder and Mr. C. W. %Nilson; cattle, Mr. T. S. Minton and Mr. Robert W. Hobbs; pigs, Mr. W. H. Carter; butter and cheese, Mr. George Platt: Mr. W. Bainbridge acted as referee and Mr. Hugh Ferguson and Mr. S. J. Hewitt were the consulting veterinary surgeons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>JUMPING CONTEST.</head>
        <p>THE JUMPING CONTEST.</p>
        <p>To the average visitor, the jumping contest was probably the most attractive feature of the day. The enclosure which is bordered with clumps of trees and commands a pleasant view, presented a scene of anima- tion, the grand stand being filled with spectators while the pretty dresses of the ladies added a nice dash of, colour. The entries for the contest timbered 20,which is a record, but only 15 competitors put In an appearance. There were the usual obstacles of fences and water to be negotiated and the contest was not without excitement.A familiar figure was Mr. John-Taylor,of Ormskirk, wh) as usualirode a high- spirited animal which went at a rattling pace, and called forth rounds of applause. The winners were :- 1 (220), F. V. Grange, Alvaston, Nantwich ; 2, (215), Whittingham Bros., Wellington-street, Burton-on-Trent; 3 (210), F. V. Grange, Aivaston ; 4, (25), Thomas and Henry Ward, Pinchinthorpe, Great Ayton, Yorkshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>TRADESMEN'S EXHIBITS.</head>
        <p>TRADESMEN'S EXHIBITS.</p>
        <p>As usual there was a representative collection of tradesmen's and specialists' exhibits which attracted much attention. The exhibit made by Messrs. Carton's Limited of their new and improved breeds of Farm Plants well maintsined that firm's high reputa- tion. Their new breeds of oats, "Abundance," "The Yielder," " Bountiful" etc., were represented by excel- lent specimens drawn from field crops in the neigh- bourhood, as were also their new breeds of wheats, " Victor," " Red King,"" Reliance," etc., all of which have been raised by the Carton's system of plant breeding. A very fine sample of the new breed of perennialised ryegrass grown at Acton Grange was also exhibited and called for special comment. Messrs. Carton's exhibits are always unique in the fact that their system of Plant Breeding is illustrated by micro- photographs, in which great interest is always taken by progressive agriculturists. Messrs. Dutton and Hudson, implement merchants, Warrington, bad a splendid collection of up-to-date machines, calculated to meet the requirements of the most enterprising farmer. The advantages of the farming and ranching in Rhodesia, which presents unique openings for farmers, and it is a land of sunshine with millions of acres of</p>
        <p>land to cultivate, were well set forth at the Rhodesian stand of the British South African Company. THE OFFICIALS. . _</p>
        <p>The Society is fortunate in having an excellent band of officials who throw themselves whole-heartedly into the work. They are :—President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.; Vice-President, Mr. T. H. Lyon; chairman of Council, Mr. Joseph Palmer; vice-chairman, Mr. Thomas Warburton; hon. treasurer, Mr. Alfred Broome; secretary, Mr. James Bainbridge. The secre- tarial duties were undertaken by Mr. Bainbridge for the first time and great praise is due to him for the smooth- ness with which the show passed off. The stewards were :—Hunters and jumpers, Messrs. Joseph Palmer, J. W. Boumphrey, H. G. Roberts, F. J. ,Barnish, T. Houghton, I'. M. West, V. V. Davies and P. Remund Monks; hackneys, harness tradesmen's and tenant farmers' turnouts, Messrs. R. H. Lapage, Jas. V. Moore, F. A. Milling, Harry Gandy, Henry Milling, C. B. Davies and T. Ridgway; shires and draught geld- ings, Messrs. William Richardson, jun., John Whitlow, Joseph I'. Baxter, Edward Davies, and A. Weir; cattle, Messrs. H. Singleton, Joseph Percival, P. L. Leather, and H. Brocklehnrst ; pigs, butter and cheese, Messrs. F. A. Milling, John Cooper, and Bayly Isaac ; medals and champion prizes, Mr. James Tinsley; show yard, Mr. J. E. Seeker.</p>
        <p>IHE LUNCHEON. SIR GILBERT GREENALL AND THE BUDGET. At noon Sir Gilbert Greeuall entertained a large company at luncheon. The gathering included leading agriculturists and also many of Warrington's best known townsmen. The catering of Messrs. Agar, of Manchester, gave every satisfaction. Mr. ALAN SYKES proposed "Success to the War- rington Agricultural Society," coupling with it the name of Sir Gilbert Greenall. He said that the success which had all along attended their shows had been achieved by the continued and steady efiort of the members, with their head guiding and directing them—. Sir Gilbert Greenall. (Hear, hear.) To mention the name of Sir Gilbert was to speak of success, whether as r( prds their ()en show, or in the larger arena—the Royal Show of England. (Applause.) Ihe toast was Ip,neured with enthusiasm, the com- pany singing "For he's a jolly good fellow." Sir GILBERT GREENALL, in responding, thanked them for the enthusiastic way in which they had drunk his health. The Society was to be congratulated upon having a fine day for the show, especially as hitherto they had had ten wet days, but they bad not grumbled. It had been thought by some that in view of the fine cc( ather of last week they had acted unwisely in changing the date from last week to this week, but they could not have got the entries together in time, and when they remembered the splendid entry of hunters that day they were fully justified in arranging matters so as not to clash with the Yorkshire Show. (Hear, hear.) There was no doubt that such shows as the one they were holding, did an incalculable amount of good, and it was a great pleasure to seethe splendid exhibition of pedigree stock. How long they would be able to have any pedigree stock in this country it was difficult to say. New burdens put upon land, the breaking up of many large estates, and the cutting up into a general system of small holdings of our large farms would ring the death knell of pedigree stock breeding. He did not consider that was speaking of politics; it was a fact nobody could get over. If we had the country cut up into little farms, where were our big stock breeders to go to? They would go abroad where they would not be interfered with. Pedigree stock was peculiar to this country. It had been nurtured here, it had men associated with it who meant to have the best of every- thing in it, and nobody had been able to touch them. Before another year was over they would be having the Royal Show of England held at Liverpool, and he believed that would be an exhibition which had never been equalled in the history of that society. (Hear, i hear). Backed up by the agriculturists n the country, including the members of the Royal Lancashire Society, they were all determined to see flirt the Royal was a magnificent success. (Applause). It was intended to have prizes for farms— certainly in Lancashire and Cheshire. These competi- tions had proved a great success in the last four or five years, and had done much to improve farming, and create an interest in agriculture which perhaps nothing else could do. He hoped when the time came that the Warrington district would not be behind in competing for the farm prizes, and that they would be successful in winning some of these. (Applause). Speaking with considerable emotion, Sir Gilbert alluded to the great loss they bad sustained ln the death of Mr. John White, who bad been chairman of the Royal Lancashire for fourteen years. Mr. White was a close friend of his and would be greatly missed by agriculturists generally. (Prize List on Next Page.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>GUARDIANS' ELECTION.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The Vacancy in Orford Ward.</p>
        <p>An election to fill the vacancy in the representation of Orford Ward on the Board of Guardians,occasioned by the resignation of Mrs. Wilkinson,will take place on September 3rd. The following candidates have been nominated :</p>
        <p>Peter Peacock,- traffic manager, 135,- Bewsey-road proposed by William Smith, 15 and 17, Earl-street, seconded by R )bert Cade, jun., 8, Silver-street. James Armitage Rushworth, licensed victualler,2l2, Winwick-road, proposed by Charles Lindsey Rogers, 37, Clegge-street, seconded by John Thomas Monks, 10, Clegge-street.</p>
        <p>Monday at noon is the latest time for the receipt of withdrawals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly meeting of tha Warrington and Dis- trict No. 4 of the Independent Order of Rechabites was held in the IVesleyan Schools, Penketh, on Saturday, Bro. T. Longshaw, D.C.R., presiding.—After the usual business and District Secretary's report, in Which it was ststed that the membership of the District was now 1,365 adults, the delegates to the H.51.C., Bro. T. Massey, D.T., and Bro. J. Hill, P.D.C.R., gave a very t interesting report of the Conference recently held in Birmingham. The following will show the progress of the Order, as reported at the said Conference, since 1907. There had been four new districts opened, and 214 adult and 288 Juvenile Tents had also been opened. The increase of n-embership for the last two years had been 17,588 adult benefit members, and 20,097 juvenile benefit members. The total increase of members during the past ten years had been 190,1?0, 88,747 having been added to the adult section, and 101,483 to the juveniles. The total membership at the end of 1908 was 241,796 adults and 188,943 juveniles, and the total funds, £2,013,724 19s. 2d. At the close of the meeting the members adjourned to tea with the members of the Warrington Band of Hope Union Council in response to an invitation given by Mr. and Mrs. J. Dodds who have recently been admitted honorary members of the Independent Order of Recbabites. After tea, to which about 120 sat down the party went to Penketh House, where some enjoyed tbe old-fashioned game of bowls and others a ramble round the garden and Penketh Flower Show. Before leaving Bro. E. Walkden of Bryon moved a vote of thanks to Mr. sad Mrs. J. Dodds for their hospitality which was ably seconded by Mr. J. Owen. Mr. and Mrs. J. Dodds suitably replied. A very pleasant evening was brought to a aose by the singing of "God be with you till we meet again."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>(Continued froma Page 3). WARRINGTON THURSDAY V. PRESCOT THURSDAY. These teams met at Prescot under delightful conditions the match resulting in a splendid victory for the War- ringtonians who inflicted the first defeat of the season on the Prescot team. Prescot batted first' Bud through some good batting by Reichardt, Pickle and Jones the large total of 152 was amassed. 11. Banner bowled very well indeed, and kept a gond, steady length, taking six wickets at a total cost of 63 runs. J. G. Bryant also bowled well and captured three wickets for 39 runs. Warrington Thursday opened well W. H. Podmore and J. G. Bryant until the latter failed to get home and was run out after making 20 in excellent style. J. W. SNells and J. F. Foster were next dismissed cheaply and with 62 runs on the board, things were not looking over well for Warrington. With steady defensive bat- ting by T. G. Bate and excellent square strokes by W El. Poimore, a total of 154 runs was compiled with the loss of six Warrington wickets. W. H. Podmore was bowled after an all-important contribution of SO rims. After one mis-hit at the commencement of the innings —which chance was not accepted—he set about the bowlers in fine style, his drives being all that one could wish for. T. G. Bate deserves to be mentioned, his effort coming at a point of the game when things were not looking too healthy for IVarrington. Scores :</p>
        <p>PRESCOT THURSDAY. WARRINGTON THURSDAY. E. F. Reicba-clt b H. W. H. Podmore b W Banner 18 Jones BO F. Pickie bH. Banner ... 34 J. G. Bryant run out 2O J. :41. Swift lbw b J. G. .. J. F. Foster c Gleave b</p>
        <p>Bryant - 0 W. Jones o J. Wells b J. W. Wells bW. Jones 3 Banner Rev. A. E. Bass c and b H. Banner c Gleave b Banner W. Young c and b J. E. H. Bibby bW. Young 0 Bryant T. R. Gleare cJ. W.Wells bat :—G. Haley, G. b Banner R. Pennington st J. W and T. H. Gregson. H. Podmore 1 J. Pennington not out Extras</p>
        <p>Extras</p>
        <p>Total 152 Total (tor 6 wkts)ls4</p>
        <p>WALNUTS FOR PICKLING, ls. 3D. Fort 120. CHERRY PLUMS, 2s. 6D. Doz. McDERMOTT, "Cheapest Man Alive." MARKET &amp; BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>(Continued from precious page). W ALTON SHOW.</head>
        <p>(Continued from precious page). W ALTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST. SHIRES. DISTRICT CLASSES. Mare, 4 years old and upwards, without foal— First prize, £5; second, £3 ; third, £2 : I. James Gould, Crouchley, Lymm ; 2, Alfred Broome, Windmill Farm, Preston Brook; 3 and r, Sara S. Raingill, The Grange, Ringway, Altrinchara ; h c, Dan Massey. Crumley Heath, Little Leigh, Northwich. Novice Brood Mare, 4 years old and upwards, with foal at foot, not having won a first prize asa brood mare, with foal at fcot at any show prior to August 10th, 1009.—First prize, £5 ; second, £3; third, £2: 1, Joseph Billing- ton, Daresbury ; 2, Anthony Garnett, Spring Farm, Tarbock ; 3, Richard Billington, Brookside Farm, Stratton. Brood Mare, 4 years cld and upwards with foal at foot.—First prize, £10; second, £8 ; third, £4 ; fourth, £2: 1, James Gould, Crouchley, Lymm ; 2 William Blackburn, Bowdon View Farm, Rostherne, 3, William Henry Carter, Moss Hall, Carrington, Cheshire; 4, John Blackburn, Rostherne, Knutsford. Colt Foal.—First prize, £4; second, £3 ; third, £2; fourth, £1: 1, James Gould, Crouchley, -Lymm.; 2, Zenas Billington, Dutton, Warrington; 3, George Smith, Cranshaw Hall. Bold, near Widnes; 4, Thomas Warburton, Hall Lane Farm, Daresbury ; r, John Whitlow, Broomfields, Appleton, Warrington. Filly Foal.—First prize, £4 ; second, £3 ; third, £1 : 1, James Gould, Crouchley, Lyman; 2, Herbert Walton, Oversley Farm, Norley ; 3. Richard Clarke, Warburton Park, near Warrington; 4, John Longshaw, Elowshoot's Farm, Grappenhall ; William Henry Carter, Moss Hall, Carrington. Yearling Colt or Gelding.—First prize,£4; second, £3 ; third, £2: I,James Gould,Crouchley,Lymm; 2, Sam S. Raingill, The Grange, Ringway ; 3, H. and R. Ainscough, Woods End Farm, Risley; r, Thomas Morris Marsh house Farm, Dutton, Preston Brook. Yearling Filly.—First prize, £4 ; second, £3 ; third, £2 : 1, Lewis Dodd, Park House, Rushton, Tarporley ; 2, Thomas Smith, Union Bank, Bold, near Ihidnes ; 3, William -Henry Carter, Moss Hall, Carrington; hc, Thomas Smith, Union Bank, Bald, near Widnes; r, Alfred Broome, Preston Brook. Twa Year Old Gelding or Filly.—First prize, £4 ; second, £3; third, £2: 1, Benjamin Wright. Baguley Green Farm, Mobberley ; 2, John Wright, Back Lane Farm, Ashley; 3, 11. and R. Ainscough, Woods End Farm, Risley; r, John H. Turton, Burtcn Mill Farm, Hale. Three-year-old Gelding or Filly.—First prize, £4; second ; third, £2: 1, H. and R. Ainscough, Woods End Farm, Risley; 2. John Richardson, jun., Old Hough, Warmingham, Middlewich ; 3, Gartons Ltd., Acton Grange, Warrington; r, William Blackshaw, Greenside Farm, Hatton. Gelding. 4 years old and upwards.— First prize, £5; second, £3; third, £2: 1, Peter Davies, Midlands Farm, Warburton, Cheshire; 2, Gartons Ltd., Acton Grange, Warrington; 3, The Corporation of Warrington.</p>
        <p>HUNTERS. DISTRICT CLASSES.-Brood Mare having produced a living foal 7, or stinted to, a thoroughbred or regis- tered hunter sire in 1909. First prize, £5 ; second, £3 ; third, 22 : 1, Joseph Millington, Sutton Hall,Kinderton, 3liddlewich ; 2, Robert Needham, Bixton Lodge, Knuts- ford ; 3, Ernest C. Griffith; Broken Cross, Northwich ; r, W. H. Shiers, The Red House, Hartford Cheshire. Foal by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire. First prize, £4; second, £3; third, £2 : 1, W. H. 'Shiers, The Red House, Hartford, Cheshire; 2, Ernest C. Griffiths, Broken Cross, IsTorthwich ; 3, Robert Needham, Bixton Lodge, Knutsford ; r, Joseph Millington, Sutton Hall, Kinrierton, Middle wich. Yearling Colt, Gelding or Filly by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire.-F;rst prize, £1; second, £3; third, .£2 : 1, Ernest C. Griffiths, Broken Cross, Nerthwich ; 2, John G. Peel, Colshaw Hall, Over Peever, Knutsford ; 3, Robert Shepherd, Packside, Aston, Preston Brook; r. C. L. Evans, Norton Hill, Runcorn. Two-year-old Gelding or Filly by a thorough- bred or registered hunter sire.-First prize, £1; second, ; third, £2 1, Robert Needham, Bixton Lodge, Knntsford ; 2 'and 3, Thomas Prescott, Kinderton, Middlewich. Three-year-old Gelding or Filly by R thoroughbred or registered hunter sire.-Fist prize, £4; second, £3; third, £2: 1, T. A. Owen, Marbury Farm, near Northwich ; 2, Colonel Hall Walker, S Ind - brow, Tarporley ; 3, Ernest C. Griffiths, Broken Cross, Northwich ; r, Richard Billington, Stretton, Warrington. OPEN CLASSES (To be Ridden).-Novice Hunter Mare or Gelding, 4 years old and upwards, equal to carry 13 steno and not more than 14st. 71bs. No entry in this class has ever been awarded a prize exceeding the value of £5 in a riding class at any show previous to IGth August, 1909. First prize, £10; second, £5; third, £3: 1, J. H. Stekes, Great Bowden, Market Harborough ;; 2, R. L. Fenwick, Wymondhara, Oakham ; 3, John Drage, Chapel Brampton, Northampton; 4, His Grace the Duke of Westminster, Eaton Hall, Chester ; 5, J. S. H. Fullerton, Noblethorpe, Barnsley, Yorks; r, Edward Harrison, Amster Hill, Easingwold. Novice Hunter Mare or Gelding, 4 years old and upwards equal to carry over 14st. Vbs. No entry in this class has ever been awarded a prize exceeding the value of £5 in a riding class, at any show previous to 10th August, 1909.-First prize, £10; second prize £5; third prize, £3 ; 1, W. Gale, Waltham, Melton Mowbray; 2, J. H. Stokes, Great Bowden, Market Harborough ; 3, John Drage, Chapel Brampton, Northampton ;4, F. C. Faulkner Wood, The Den, Singlebro', Winslow ; r, J.S. H. Fullerton. Nobletherpo, B rrnsley, Yorks. Hunter Mare or Gelding, four years old and upwards, equal to carry 13 stone, and not more than list. 71bs.-First prize, £l5; second prize, £10; third prize £5; fourth prize £3 ; 1, W. A. Simpson-Hinchcliffe, 9, Park Parade Stables, Harrowgate ; 2, John Drage, Chapel Brampton, Northampton ; 3, R. L. Fenwick, Wymondharn, Oak. ham ; 4, J. H. Stokes, Great Bowden, Market Har- borough ; h c J. S. H. Fullerton, Noblethorpe, Barnsley Yorks ;r, W. Gale, Waltham, Melton Mowbray. Hunter Mare or Gelding four years old and upwards, equal to carry ovea 14st. Ms.-First prize, £l5 : second prize £10; third prize £5; fourth prize £3; I, Jehn Drage, Chapel Brampton, Northampton :2, W. Gale, Waltham, Melton Mowbr.ry ; 3. J. H. Stokes, Great Bowden, Market Ilarborough ; J. 4, S. H. Fullerton, Noblethorpe, Barnsley, Yorks; r, W. A. Simpson- Hinchcliffe, 9, Park Parade Stables, Harrotvgate.</p>
