      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FLOWER SHOW.</head>
        <p>THE FLOWER SHOW.</p>
        <p>The Walton Flower Show which is held in conjunction with the Warrington Agricultural Society's Show at Walton is a striking example of the splendid work which can be done by horticultural shows. Established three years ago in a very small way, with the object of encouraging gardening in the village, it has been supported with commendable enthusiasm, and each year has seen a great improvement in the .quality of the exhibits. Thursday's show was certainly the best on record, and must have given great satisfaction to the President (Lady Greenall), who is the moving spirit in the enterprise. The judging was undertaken by Mr. C. Sprackling, Tarporley, and Mr. Herbert, Tarporley, the exhibits numbering 283. The groups of plants attracted particulgr admiration. Mr. F. Baker's artistic display, which was awarded first prize, was composed chiefly of coleus, hydrangeas, creepers, abutilon, eupatoria, moot- bretia, and streptocarpos, surmounted with a large palm. The second prize was secured by Mr. P. Wilkinson, who showed a very beautiful group consist- ing of croton, caladium, variegated begonia, Miura longiflorium, companula and fancy grasses. The third prize group, exhibited by Mr. A. W. Nicholls, was made up of chimney companula, celosia, liliura auratum and other choice plants. Mr. F. Baker suceeeded in winning the first prize for a collection of vegetables, consisting. of onions, green peas, potatoes, carrots, berms, celery cauliflowers, leeks and tomatoes. Mr.J. G. Hatton dis- charged the duties of steward, and Mr. James Bainbridge was an efficient secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT.-For the neatest and best stocked cottage flower garden : 1, William Berry ; 2, E H Cotterill. For the neatest and best stocked cottage kit _!hen garden: 1, J W Wright; 2, George Green; 3, IVilliam Moulsdale. For the neatest and best st.miced cottage flower garden (estate workmen) : 1, John Kippax ; 2, James Kippax; 3, Peter Cliffe. For the neatest and best stocked cottage kitchen garden : I, John Kippax : 2. James Chile ; 3, T H Rutter. Champion Prize, for the neatest and best cottage flower and kitchen garden: John Kippax, Hatton. GREENHOUSE PLisTs.-One single fuchsia: 1, F Kippax; 2, Peter Chile. One-double fucbsia : I, F Kippax • 2, Peter Chile. One begonia: 1, F Kippax; 2. James Kippax; ; 3. Peter Chile. Three plants in or out of flower, any valiety : 1, F Kippax; 2, Peter Cliff. WINDOW PLANTS.-00e begonia: 1. John Eippax ; 2 James Rutter. One double fuchsia: 1, 10 Hanllett •, 29 John Kippax. One single fuchsia : 1, John Kippax •, 2, James Rater ; 3, F Hamblett ; h c,J W Wright. Geranium : 1, John Kippax; 2, T-11 Rutter. Pot of musk: 3, James Rutter. Ivy geranium : 1, E 11 CAterill ; 2, James Rutter. Three plants in or out of flower, any variety : 1, John Kippax ; 2, James Rutter; 3, J NV Wright. CUT FLOWERS.-Six double : 1, E H Cotterill ; 2, James Rutter. Six single dahlias : I,James Rutter ; 2, F Kippax. Six cactus dahlias : 1, H J Hopkins ; 2, E II Cotterill; 3, James Rutter. Six pompon dahlias: I, James Rutter; 2, E HCotterill ; 3, Peter Chile. Six asters : 1, Peter Chile ; 2, W Berry; 3, E II Cotterill. Six marigolds (French) : 1, H J Hopkins; 2, E H Cotterill ; 3, F Kippax. Six marigolds (African) : 1, E II Cotterill. Six carnations : 1, Peter Cliffe. Six pansies (3 varieties): 1, F Kippax ; 2 T II Rutter ; 3,James Chile. Six violas (3 varieties) J W Wright; 2, E H Cotterill ; 3, Peter Cliffe. Six spikes ten week stocks (3 varieties) : I, E H Cotterill ; 2, W Berry ; 3, H Belt. Six spikes gladioli (3 varieties) : 1, ,1E H Cotterill. Six bunches of sweet peas (3 varieties): 11 W Yarwood ; 2, E H Cotterill ; equal 3, W Berry; land W Monlsdale. Six roses (3 varieties) • I, Peter Chile ; 2, J %V Wright. Collection of cut blooms: 1, Peter Chile ; .2, John Kippax : 3, James Kippax. VEGETABLES (District). -Three rarities of potatoes: 1, J W Wright; 2, W Berry ; 3, G Green. One cabbage (red) : I, J W Wright ; 2, W Moulsdale 3, E H Cotterill. One cabbage (white): 1, J W Wright; 2, F Hamblett ; 3, E H Cotterill. Two cauliflowers: I, G Green; 2, H J Hopkins; 3, J W Wright. Six spring onions: I, G Green; 2, W Moulsclale •, 3, H J Hopkins. Twelve bulbs shallots: 1, U Bell ;,2, J W Wright; 3,</p>
        <p>W Moulsdale ; hc, G Green. Six leeks: 1, W Moulsdale; 2, F Hamblett; 3, E H Cotterill. One dish of peas (12 pods): 1, W Moulsdale; 2, H Ball; 3. G Green. One dish broad beans (six pods): 1. J W Wright; 2, II J Hopkins; 3, W Yarwood; h c, G Green; One dish French beans (12 pods) : 1, J W Wright; 2, El J Hopkins. One dish scarlet beans (12 pods) : 1, II J Hopkins ;2, J W Wright. Two sticks celery (white) : 1, E H Cotterill ;2, F Hamblett ;3, W Yar- wood. Two sticks celery (Ted) : I, W Wright :2, E H Cotterill : 3, F Hamblett; hc, W Yarwood. One vegetable marrow (white) : 1, E H Cotterill ; 2 H Bell. One vegetable marrow (green) : 1, E. H. Cotterill. Two cabbage lettuce: 1, J W wright ; 2 EII Cotterill '• 3, W Berry. Two cos. lettuce : 1, W IVright ; 2.E 11 Cotterill ; 3, W Berry. Six Carrots : I, II .1 Hopkins. Six turnips : 1, H Ball ; 2, W Moulsdale ; 3, F Hamblett. Six beetroots: I, 17 Hamblett; 2, W Moulsdale ; 3, .1 W Wright. Six parsnips: 1, J. W Wright. Twelve Sprays Parsley ; 1, W Wright; 2, E H Cotterill. Collection of vege- tables : 1, G Green: 2, EH Cotterill; 3,J W Wright ; h c,H J Hopkins. FRUIT.-One dish of cooking apples: 1, G Green: 2, W Mouldsd ;3, J W Wright : hc, W Berry. One dish of dessert apples: 1, Peter Cliffe ; 2, W Berry • 3, .1 W Wright. One dith pears: 1, J W Wright; John Kippax. One dish gooseberries ; 1, W Berry 2, T H Ratter; 3, H Bell. One dish rel currants: 1, Tll Rutter. One dish black currants : 1, E H Cotterill ;. 2, G Green ; 3, r H Rutter. VEGETABLES (Estate Workmen.)-One cabbage (white': 1, John Kippax. Six spring onions: I John Kippax; 2, T Kippax; 3, James Chile. Twelve bulbs shallots : 1, F Kippax; 2, James Rutter ; 3, James Chile. Six leeks: I, John Kippax; 2, r Kippax; 3, James Cliffe. One dish peas (12 pods): 1, F Kippax; 2, John Kippax; 3, T H Rutter One dish broad beans (six pods) : 1, John Kippax • 2, T H Rutter ;3, James Rutter. One dish French beans. (12 pods) : I, James Cliffs; 2,.T H Rutter •' 3, John Kippax. One dish scarlet beans (12 pods) : 1, F Kippax; 2, James Cliffe ; 3, John Kippax. Two sticks celery (white) : 1, James Kippax ; 2, F Kippax ; 3, T II Rutter. Two sticks celery (red) : 1, John Kippax ; 2, F Kippax ;3, James Kippax. One vegetable marrow (white) : 1, James Cliffe; 2, T H Rutter. Two cabbage lettuce: 1, John Kippax • 2, F Kippax. Two cos lettuce: 1, James Ratter; 2, F Kippax. Six turnips : I, F Kippax; 2, T H Ratter; 3, John Kippax. Six beetroots: 1, John Kippax; 2, J Kippax; 3, James Kippax. Six parsnips: 1, John Kippax. 12 sprays parsley: 1, James Ckiffe. Six tomatoes (red) ; 1, Peter Chile. Collection of vegetables (six varieties) : 1, John Kippax ; 2, F Kippax; 3, T H Rutter. GROUPS.-Group of greenhouse and stove plants: 1, F Baker, Walton House; 2, P Wilkinson' Walton Let; 3, A W Nicholls, The Oaklands. Collection of vegetables 1, F Baker, Walton House; 2, A W Nicholls, The Oaklands. Collection of cut blooms: I, F Biker.</p>
        <p>There were some magnincent exhibits of cut 116wers (not for competition) by Messrs. Dicksons, Ltd., Chester; Messrs. Caldwell and Sons, Knutsford ; Messrs. Clibrans, Ltd., Altrincham ; Messrs. Dickson and Robinson, Manchester ; Messrs. It. P. Ker and Sons, Liverpool ; Mr. Charles A. Young. West Derby; Mr. Henry Middlehurt, Liverpool ; Messrs. Thomas Davies and Co, Liverpo )1. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" The Man on the Box."</p>
        <p>A -smart, sparkling comedy is a welcome change from the round of melodrama, and this is exactly what patrons of the Royal Court Theatre find in "The Man on the Box" an adaptation by Miss Li% ingstone of Harold McGrath's celebrated novel, which is pre- sented by Mr. Dore Davidson's Company. The management are to be congratulated upon their enter- prise in securing such a high class attraction and there is no doubt that it has afforded immense pleasure to the audiences. "The Man on the Box" is the story of a gay young lord whose fondness for practical jokes and his love for a fascinating girl cause him to do ridiculous things. In the hands of such a capable company the comedy goes merrily from beginning to end and those who want to spend a really amusing time should make a point of visiting the theatre this (Saturday) evening. The action of the play revolves round two individuals —Lord Kentire, the irrepressible Robleman above mentioned, and June Annersley, the object of his affections. Mr. C. Leveson Lane impersonates Lord Kentire with consummate skill. There is a delightful freedom and enthusiasm about his acting which soon captures the audience, and he is able to extract the maximum of fun from the part. We could hardly imagine it in better hands. Miss Rose Temple achieves a triumph as June Anhersley. She is charmingly vivacious and her portrayal is a clever one in every respect. Mention must also be made of Mr. Alfred Bristowe who infuses into his study of the Hon. Charles Henderson, a good-humoured buoyancy which is quite infectious. It will interest local playgoers to know that Mr. Leveson Lane and Mr. Bristowe will be seen at the Court Theatre again in September; for they have been secured for Mr. B. S. Townroe's invasion play, "A Nation in Arms." All the other characters are admirably played, Mr. Oswald Marshall, as Mr. Watts, the magistrate, being especially meritorious. "The Man on the Box" is preceded by a one-act play "A Dance at Dawn," an episode of "The Reign of Terror," the chief feature being the exquisite dancing by Mddle. Dolli, late of the Verdi Theatre. and La Scala, Milan, who appears In Italian, Eastern and Russian dances, with remarkable success. The orchestral selections, under Mr. Noiman Latham's conductorship, are well selected and creditably performed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</p>
        <p>Visitors to the Royal* Court Theatre are promised a treat next week in' Rollicking Rory 'land the visit of that favoulite comedian Chalmers Macke). It is described as a musical Irish fairy play, and is a wholesome, effective story of love and romance, teeming with natural Irish wit, in a setting of Ireland's most beautiful scenery the pkincipal being the" Fairy Path "which is described as a triumph of stage effect. The picturesque periods of the play afford great scope for elaborate costuming, of which fall advantage has been taken.. Music too is a feature of the production,and old Irish melodies and rollicking Irish songs and dances add greatly to the local colour- ing. Much of the success of this most successf,il Irish play is due to the. personality of the author- manager hi,.nself, who 11,ill be well remembered here i for his fine performance n " The Wearing o' the Green."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TERRITORIALS RETURN FROM CAMP.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS RETURN FROM CAMP.</p>
        <p>Looking bronzed and the picture of health the War- rington Territorials returned home on Sunday after a fortnight's training under canvass. In many years Caerwys Camp has been memorable. In the first place the weather has been superb. As for the lessons of the "war "they have been, and are, many and crave. With- out doubt the Territorial troops of the IVestern Division of Lancashire form now a much more efficient body than they did a brief two wedts ago. On Saturday Major-General Bethune visited each of the units in turn and made to it one of his terse and characteristic speeches. He reminded the men that at Caerwys Camp they had had the opportunity for the first time of taking part in operations in which troops of vstrious arms had co-operated. From those operations be him- self had le trnt much, and he was certain that not o nly the brigadiers and the officers commanding units, Lut even the rank and file could conscientiously express themselves in similar terms. The work bad been carried out so far as possible under active service conditions. Yet he trusted and believed that it had been thoroughly enjoyed. At all events, it was his duly and pleasure to acknowledge the ready willingness of officers, non-com- missioned officers, and men to realize that they were now the real article and to act as such.</p>
        <p>Colonel Fairclough addressed the Warrington battalion before they marched off to Holywell and com- plimented them very warmly upon their behaviour in camp. He regretted thlt the" men" had not been provided with boots by the War Office and that the water supply bad been extremely " limited" ; the men had, however, met their hardship in a soldierly spirit and did their washing in lemonade and soda-water at their own expense. The battalion reached Warrington about 1 p.m. and received a very hearty welcome. ZEAL AND DISCIPLINE.</p>
        <p>From, an occasional correspondent, the " Times " pubiishes some notes on the tratning of the West Lanes- sh're division, which has furnished an opportunity of studying the work done by troops of the second line other than those in the metropolis. As regards the various arms (says the writer), the material of the infantry was excellent, and the comp discipline and the zeal of the men deserved all praise. The company officers varied from excellent to moderate; and it is to be hoped that commanding officers will for the future be stronger-minded in regard to getting rid of those who have mistaken their avocation. Company discipline in the field, which was bad at the beginning of the training, improved day by day. Caerwys itself was a new district, in which everything had to be arranged for. The inhabitants were most friendly, and there were but few complaints and but little compensa- tion to e paid. The terrain itself was varied and good. Woods, fields, hills, mountains, and rivers were all features of the mancenvring area, and the result was a general education of all concerned. With three more similar trainings—and if one could be arran&amp;ed for in the proximity of regular troops, so that theittandard to work up to can be realised—the division will, in the opinion of its commander, be capable of holding its own anywhere ; but the class of horse provided for the artillery must be better, and it must have, the same training as its rider.</p>
        <p>IARGE AGAINST A WARRINGTON FOOTBA LLER.•</p>
        <p>At Warrington Police Court, on Tuesday, J“ho William Chesters. a well-known Warrington footballer. who lives at 26, Charles-street, was charged with inflict- ing grievous bodily harm on John Rowan, of Hale-street, by fracturing .his jaw with a blow from his fist.—Chief _ _</p>
        <p>—.— Inspector Williatnson said that the prisoner and the prosecutor were acquainted with each other. On Satur- thy night, whilst on the recreation ground in Winwick- road, a dispute arose between the prosecutor and the prisoner's father. The prisoner came on the scene afterwards and struck Rowan a fearful blow on the face, fracturing his jaw in two places. He was conveyed to the Infirmary, where his injuries were attended to. The matter was repotted to the police, who arrested the i prisoner. A remand was necessary n order that the surgeon at the Infirmary could explain the nature of the injuries —The maeistrates remanded the prisoner, hail being allowed.—Cbesters was again brought before the magistrates on Tuesday when the police had had an interview with the doctor at the Infirmary, who stated that the fracture was a bad one, extending into the mouth. SJ far the injured man was progressing favour- ably, and if he continued there was no fear of any serious complication. A further remand for a week was necessary. The magistrates accordingly remanded ptisoner on bail.</p>
        <p>, _ , . :., , 1 ,Min th e 0 1---' 0 I i„ , _ _ -40 u nTS - , • J „,„ , •-_ ~ CI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court. SATURDAY.—Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (chairman), Dr. Markel, Councillor Dr. Joseph and Mr. A. J. Jolley.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court. SATURDAY.—Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (chairman), Dr. Markel, Councillor Dr. Joseph and Mr. A. J. Jolley.</p>
        <p>„. A WOMAN'S THErTS.—Beatrice Parkinson, of Back Dallam-lane, was charged with stealing on the 10th of August from No. 63, Church-street, a pair of men's boots valued at 4s. 6d., the property of Thomas Domville, also with stealing a boy's suit from the Manchester Tailoring and Clothing Depot.—The Chief Constable stated that on the previous Tuesday the boots were seen safe in the shop of Messrs. Domville, of 69 and 71, Church-st reet. They were not missed until seven o'clock when they were brought to the shop of Mr. Tyrer, pawnbroker, and it wis discovered they had been stolen. The pr is( ner took them to Tyrer's shop to pledge, and stated that they were her own. The assistant, seeing the private marks on the boots, knew they belonged to and going out of the shop he took them there to ascertain if they were really theirs, and on his retarn the prisoner had disappeared. The police were informed and inquiries were made by Detective Reddington. He visited the prisoner's house, and on seeing her she stated that the boots had been given to her by a woman she did not know.—Continuing, the Chief Constable said she was brought before the magis- trates the previous thy, but a remand was granted in order to make inquiries with regard to 13 nawntickets which were found at prisinee's house. The result of thesa inquiries was that there was the second charge of stealing the boy's suit. In that Case an assistant at Messrs. Tyrer's pawnshop gave information to Detective Reddington the previous day that prisoner had pledged the suit of clothing about half-past five o'clock the previous rues lay for ss. 63. and she stated that she had bought it from a Scotchman.—Prisoner, who had been three times previously before the Court, was sentenced to one month's imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
        <p>MOND AY.—Before the Mayor (chairman). Aldermtn Smethurst and Evms, Messrs. W. Roberts, S. Wright, H.Thornten,,G. Filling and %V. Furness.</p>
        <p>ROBBED MS FELLOW-WORKMEN.—James Diggings, residing at Plinstou's lodging-house, 13arbauld-street, was charged with being on enclosed premises on Saturday night with intent to commit a felony.— William Heaton, the deputy manager of the lodging- house, said he knew prisoner very well. He went to the house about 7.15 on Saturday night and asked for 2d, and witness gave him the money. Prisoner did not engage a night's lodging and accordingly had no right to be on the premises that night. The next morning witness saw him in the kitchen. He looked as if be had had a wash. Witness was awakened by a man named Saunders who said he had been robbed of 7s.—Edward Burrows, who lived at the lodging-house, said that about half-past seven on Saturday night, prisoner asked him if he bad any coppers as he was "stony broke." About a quarter to one the next morning he was awaken--d by some clothes being thrown on his bed. On looking round he saw the prisoner, at another lodger's bed. He had a box of matches in his hand. Witness saw him "go through " the clothing. He persuaded him to put the matches back in the clothing and go away. Witness had to again caution him and he then left the room.— George Saunders another lodger sad he went to bed about ten o'clock and put his trousers under the pillow. There was about 7s. in them, including a The next morning ha found the clothing on the floor and the money gone.—Thomas Mather said he did the same as the last wit Less and in the morning he missed 61d.. in cash and an ounce of tobacco. George Smith sail he went to Sinkey on the Sunday morning along with prisoner and two other men and prisoner paid for 3 pints each. He (witness) was a friend of, prisoner and did'nt want to do him any harm.—Detec- tive Reddington stated thit in comequence of Saunders and Mather making complaints as to being rotlbed in V.° lodging-house whilst asleep, be made inquiries in company with P.C. Holland. It came to his knowledge as to the prisoner being seen spending money freely and he proceeded to Mersey-street about ' three o'clock in the afternoon and there saw prisoner I drunk. He had a quart bottle'of beer in his pos-ession. Witness questioned him as to how he came by the money he was spending and he said be had no money. Witness brought him back to the lodging-house and brought him face to taco with the witness Burrows. He then admitted being in the house during the night. Witness searche I him and found a piece of tobacco correspond- ing to the kind Mather used in his pocket. Prisoner could not give any satisfactory account of it. Witnest charged him and he answered that he "never touched it."—lnspector Williamson stated that prisoner had been before the Court ton times previously, four for ste ding. He sponged on workingmen and then robbed them. It was the most contemptible thing that a man could be guilty of and it was a pity that honest work- ingman should be at the mere,y of such a" land shirk" as prisoner. Prisoner was senteeced to three months imprisonment.</p>
        <p>SMASHED THE WlNDOWS.—Margaret Palmer, a well- known character, was charged with breaking eight panes of glass in a lodging-honse in Ship-yard.—A constable stated that the prison r was acting in a very disorderly manner. He arrestei her and she admitted having broken eight panes of glass.—She was sent to gaol for one month. WEDNESDAY,—Before lfr. Charles White (chairman) and other magistrates. _ . _</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A BICYCLE.—John Faulkner, a youth residixv, in Bostock-street, was charged with stealing a bicycle.—The evidence showed that the bicycle was left outside the shop of Mr. John Stubbs, pawnbroker, Lovely-lane, and was subsequently missed. Informa- tion was given to the police, who at once circulated a description of the bicycle. It transpired that whilst the bicycle was being sold at an anctioneeris sale in Chester, the Chester police recognised it by the descr:p- Lion giuenfthem and !apprehended prisoner.—Prisoner was also wanted for thefts In Altrincham and Frodsharn. He was sent to gaol for one month. FRIDAY. —Before Alderman Smethurst (chairman) Councillors Wilkinson, an Messrs. A. J. Jolley, 11. Thornton, W. Furness, and W. T. Williams. A VIOLENT PRISONER.—WiIIiam Wanchard of no settled abode, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Arpley-road the previous day and also with assaulting Detective Reddington —The officer stated that he was on duty at Bridge Foot when he saw the prisoner who went up to him from out of Mersey-street. Ile kept shouting" Hello." Witness told him to go away. Prisoner did so and went along Arpley-road and there created a distulbance. Witness followed him and had to take him into custody. Prisoner slid he would go quietly but after going a short distance he commenced to kick witness and tripped him up. An old gentleman came along and assisted witness with the prisoner who again promised to go quietly but after going for a while he pushed the gentleman for- ward and kicked witness. A young man named Burrow's then c.ime on the scene and assisted in taking the prisoner to the Police Stltion. After Burrows had given corrborative evidence, prisoner was sentenced to &amp;months imprisonment. ASSAULT.—Jobn Rhooney, of no settled abode, was charged with assaulting Mrs. Rogerson,a lolging-house keeper, of Ship-yard, on the 29th o° May.—Mrs. Roger- son stated in her evidence that the prisoner was drunk and he struck her.—Prisoner said he never touched the complainant. He was ordered to pay 10s. and costs or 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Juvenile Court.</head>
        <p>Juvenile Court.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY..—Before Messrs. C. White (chairman), and</p>
        <p>A. J. Jolley. „ JUVENILE STREET TRADING.—Harry Elliot, aged 12 of 62, Haydock-street. was charged with street trading without a licence in Winwick-street on the 7th August. Detective Reddington. said he saw defendant shouting end selling papers. Witness called him to him and defendant said be had no licence. On promising not to repeat the offence the case was dismissed.—Jarnes Barber (15), 37, Scotland-road, was charged with the same offence in Winwick-street,on August 13th. Detective Reddington wbo probed the case said that on going up to defendant he ran away. Witness had advised defendant time after time to get a hadge but he had not done so. Defendant said he went to the Police Station a few weeks ago to get a badge, but Reddington would not give him ore. Reddington said that was untrue. Chairman to defendant: Why don't you get a badge the is., would not be taken from you. Reddington said he had seen the lad's motbet and she said she could not do anything with him. The Clerk to defendant: Why don't von get some regular work. ' Defendant: I cannot I have an abscess on my neck. Chairman : Oh, that won't hinder you from working. Can you raise a shilling. Defendant: No</p>
        <p>Detective Reddington : I think Mr. Woolley will pay a shilling for the boy. Chairman to defendant: What would you like to do. Defendant I don't know. I cannot write. Chairman: You must be able to write or you would not sell papers.—The case was adjourned for three montbs, the clerk advising defend9nt ta get a badge out in the time or he would have to go to prison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BACKING THE BUDGET.</head>
        <p>BACKING THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER SUCCESSFUL OPEN-AIR</p>
        <p>MEETING.</p>
        <p>The campaign in support of the Budget continues with unabated vigour and enthusiasm, and there was a- lirge and attentive audience at another of the series of open- air meetings promoted by the Warrington Progressive Labour League which was held near Arpley Cannons on Monday evening. The principal speaker was Mr. W. Ramage (National Reform Union) who explained the Budget proposals in a homely and convincing manner. Mr. J. Morton presided and was accompanied on the platform by Metsrs. E. Wynne Jones, E. England, W. H. Sherturn and J. Howes, jun. The CHAIRMAN said an attempt was being made to liken the Government policy to an effort to bring about Socialism. Mr. W. P. Rylands seemed to think the Government was putting the country on an inclined plane leading to the abyss of Socialism. (Laughter.) It was rather curious that Mr. Rylands should know exactly what Socialism was and especially that the Liberal leaders should know the way to it, because he (the speaker) had seen it stated in a prominent Socialist publication that even Mr. 13Iatchford did not know the way to Socialism ; all be could do was to lay downebroad outlines of what he thought would be the course of If-gislation that would bring about Socialism. He (Mr. Morton) had scarcely met two Socialists who gays him exactly the same description of what the Socialistic State would be. How Mr. Rylands could say that Liberal legislation was going to lead to anything of the sort he could not see. The electors must not be lei astray by opponents of the Government, describing Liberalism as Socialism. The word Socialism had bad a bad reputation in times gone by, and there seemed to be an attempt to describe Liberalism as Socialism with no other intention than to discredit the Liberal party. He asked his hearers to beware of those tactics.</p>
        <p>Mr. RAMAGE, in telling the meeting that he bad come to deal with the Budget, said it was no use dealing with anything else before an English audience at the present time. Why had it aroused such remarkable interest? Because it was a bread and butter question. It touched their pockets, their future lives, and tbe development of the country. They were not old nobility living upon a great historical past, but they were English workingmen living on the future, earning their daily bread with daily toil. As Mr. Asquith stated on Saturday the Budget was</p>
        <p>THE KEY OF THE FUTURE and it was unlocking some doors, fields and castles, and letting the light of day into some of their friends the enemies' possessions. They did not like the light of thy, because it was not always convenient to say how they got the property. The Budget had came as a cun- suming fire. It was like Aaron's rod of old which ate up all the other rods. They were hearing a great deal about tariff reform in the past. To-day they beard some faint voices in the wilderness, echoes from the distant mountains, pleading for tariff reform. They had their meetings but none went near to listen. Why? Because Aaron's rod in the form of this Budget, bad eaten up tariff reform and killed it. All countries of the world were unfortunately in the same position at the present time—that of having to find money. Tariff reformers would point to Germany as a brilliant example of what Protection did for a country. But tariff reform had not benefited that country as they would have us believe. That home of Protection and sausage—(laughter)—had to find 20 or 25 millions more to make up the deficit on the pest year's income. They had lost their Chancellor in trying to get new taxation. (Laughter.) They had had, as far as they could in Germany, a change of Government, but new taxes had to be imposed and even the landlord classes hal had to consent to taxation being placed upon land there—as it was going to be placed upon land in England.(Applause) Our deficit had not arisen through the Budgets of the past not being successful ; it was</p>
        <p>A PROSPECTIVE DEFICIT. What had the Government done since they came into office? They had reduced the tax upon tea from Bd. to 5d., reduced the tax upon sugar by half, swept away entirely the tax upon coal, and in spite of those remis- sions of taxation, this magnificent Government bad wiped out over 40 millions of national debt. (Applause.) That saved us over a million per annum in interest. The debt had now gone back to what it was twenty years ago—before the South African war added so much to it. This Government had not left debt behind for building ships or forts ; or for providing guns and ammunition. Like honest men they had paid their way as they went along. Not so their predececeors, who left debts which the Liberals had to clear off. This Near the Government had broken new ground. Thank God for it! We bad got old age pensions—(applause)—and we were not going to stop where we were in that respect. Old age pensions would develop and he emphatically stated that the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PENSIONS WERE EARNED</head>
        <p>PENSIONS WERE EARNED</p>
        <p>and not given. (Applause.) The soldiers of toil were e irning heir pensions by the work with which they were serving the country. The workmen of this cotartry were as much entitled to old age pensions as any man who had served asa soldier. (Applause.) Mr. Ramage characterised the existing Poor Law as an "exceedingly poor law " which was condemned by its past and would have to be ended. Old age pensions were one step in ending it. This Government would go down to history as the Government which had the courage to grant old age pensions. Tney did not go into office pledged to give them. The late Sir Henry Camphell-Bannerman did not premise them as Mr. Balfour did in 1895. lie (the speaker) had one of Mr. Balfour's 1893 poll cards which stated "Vote for Balfour and old-age pensions and social reform." (Laughter). .When 1900 came old age pensions were not mentioned ; social reforms were forgotten. Why ? Because Mr. Balfour did not mean to give either. He was not in earnest; he did not feel for the workingmen. It was enough to get into power by their votes. Mr. Balfour gave old age pensions—to his brother and relatlons. (Laughter). .Some of the men in receipt of handsome pensions derided the granting of ss. a week. 1 hey might well. That was ss. a week to a man with £1,200 and £2,000 a year in adiition to private income? The age limit of seventy was not going to satisfy the Liberal party. It was only the beginning; the founda- tion upon which they intended to build. He looked forward, as many more did, to reducing the age by at least five years, and as the fund increased and the land brought in more taxes, it might be made a little lower. Dealing with the increased expenditure on the navy, Mr. Ramage said none of them wished the power and prestige of England to decline, and therefore they would have a navy which could be depended upon, no matter what the cost. It was, however, only just that those who had great wealth and possewions should be called upon to pay for their defence. Why should they tax bread to defend their acres ? If a man had great possessions he must hire people to protect them. If he had great wealth,he must pay for someone to guardit for him. The</p>
        <p>LAND CLAUSES of the Budget were creating a great hullabaloo. The landlords were weeping and groaning, not because we were asking them for something that was theirs, but for romething that was ours. The land of this country did not belong to the people who held it to-day. There were very few of them who could show a proper title deed. Mr. Lloyd-George said it was about time the landlords paid their share of taxation, and the Budget would give them the privilege of doing so. There would be a t..x of id. in the £ on the capital value of undeveloped land. Was that a killing amount? (A Voice: "It ought to he a shilling.") He agreed with that remark. The moderation was too great. With regard to the taxation of increment, be agreed that if a landlord bad worked and developed his land and made it valuable by his own efforts he should be entitled to the value. But if the land owner had done nothing to develop the land but bad allowed the public to come in and develop it and create the enhareed value, he was not entitled to 20 per cent., let alone the 80 per cent. he was getting under the Budget. That was also too mild and moderate. He (the speaker) had to pay a little tax on land, and he did not object at all. It was perfectly just. He was brought up a Conservative, trained in Conservatism. He was a member of a Conrervative fdmily and in the past he had worked and voted Conservative, but he was satisfied that this Government bad dealt honourably with him— more than honourably (applause)—in fact generously. They ensured him possession and allowed him to take SO per cent. of the increased value. He was thankful to the Government for tiOID2' it. He expeoted far worse—(laughter and a voice "You will get it too"). He expected to get it. As to the reversion cf leases, the Government proposed to take 10 per cent, of the increared amount levied by the landlord. That was a grand proposal for the laadlord—that he should get 90 per cent. for himself. It was more than moderate; the Government's demand was not half enough. At first the Opposition complained loudly about the landov,aer having to bear the cost of valuation. The Govern- ment, being desirous of meeting all complaints, said " We will take this duty off your shoulders and value the land for you." Then the fat was in the Ore. (Laughter). Up to now land had not, been valued too closely until the owner wanted to sell it for purposes of docks,scbools or puPolic improvements. The Govern- ment were going to •</p>
        <p>VALUE IT SCIENTIFICALLY. Sir William Harcourt's death duties would probably bring in a little more money now. There had been a great deal of laxity touching the question of land valuation for death duties. A great deal of kindness had teen shown to the owners of large properties and they had not paid anything like their share of death duties. They would hive to pay more in future—and they were beginning to see it. First the Opposition grumbled about agricultural land and the lot of the small market gardener, and the Govern- ment made him free. They grumbled about small estates of £5OO and the Government made them free. They grumbled about land valuation and the Govern- ment made that free, and now they did not know what on earth to grumble abo ut—(laughter). They had had protest meetings up and down the country but in many cases, as in Manchester on Saturday, those who came</p>
        <p>apparently to support the Protest League remained to vote against the resolution and support the Govern: ment. (Applause). It was remarkable bow the Budgel' had turned the public thought away from everything else and was training the public instinct. The Tories said it was Socialistic. It was far from Socialistic because he (the speaker) bad in his band a cutting from a speech by Richard Cobden in which he showed that for 150 yeats after the Conquest land paid all taxation. The amount gradually leasened till at the time of tb? Commonwealth, it was three-fourths, and in 1542 it bac got clown to one-25th. To-day land did not Pay 200th part of the taxation of this country. (" Shainet The Government proposals were not going to date ba9s. to the time of the Conquest, however, but only to tl3! introduction of the Budget. All the incremellb up to that time belonged to the landowner. speaker showed how the income tax proposals of the d Budget differentiated between earned and unearn% incomes, and he also contended that the death &amp;toil were most reasonable. The workingmen had Eb„Try themselves willing to pay their share of taxation. lia were patriotic and did not look to the Dukes for a le_ in the matter. (Laughter and applause.) Referring T 4: the spirit taxes, the speaker pointed out that the an3oo°: imposed by the Government ens 3s. 9d. per prow... gallon of whisky, but as the law allowed the breve!: to sell 22 per cent. below proof, they were receiving much as 89. and 9s. to recoup themselves. The mot?! car tax would be most useful as it would go mainly w the making of new roads and thus find more emploY; went. Mr. Ramage appealed to his hearers as whether they were goinc, to vote for a tax on tobaooo_,,," a tax on bread? (Crres of "tobacco.") That „ because they were patriotic and English. they think it was right to put a taS °— luxuries or necessities? Would they tax land or the children's stomachs? The Government looked tdorle tb° working classes to stand by them. Lord Lanso.nt said the Lards would not take the Budget witut,"ff wincing. They could take it mincing, wicta or in any way they.. liked, but they got to have it. (Applause). The Lords threatene! an election. If they would only face the country ia manly way upon this Budget—</p>
        <p>THE LORDS VERSUS THE PEOPLE Of le —what would the answer be ? Were the peop , 'England afraid to draw the sword in defence ef people's charter? Were they going to stand by OA see the rights of the House of Commons defeated alro taken away? Were they going to see the Lrie dictators and owners of this country? (" No.") Liberals were not afraid to put the justice of their calls., and the principles that animated them, to the test. felt certain that the people of England would stand them and carry them back to office, and clinch once en/ for all the power of the Lords in this country. (Le° applause). 'fr. J. HOWES, jun., announced that the visit of A. Ure, K.C., the Lord Advocate, to the Parr Hall, VII, been fixed for September 13th. Having urged upon !fi bearers the importance of securing their votes he a 1.% that at the next election they would have to dece..% whether they would vote for Mr. Pierpoint and IT Peers or Mr. Crosfield and the Commons. (Apelauseto Mr. E. WYNNE JONES, in moving a vote of thanks to the chairman and speaker, said the Budget was Olio, put the burden on the right shoulders. He was glaue.r... read the report of the meeting at Manchester on day when 15,000 people attended and three to one votorn in favour of this great democratic Budget. The Libe party were the champions of Free Trade but they vc'er something more—the champions of free land (Applaos.`" The land taxes were a step in the right direc.tion' 11143 alternative which the Conservatives proposed was tairl,, reform, although Lord Avebury said that was ne alternative and that the positicn could not be met by r tection. The alternative was no alternative at _4ll This great question could only be settled in the that w•s proposed in the Budget. (Applause). Mr. Sim-Inuits in seconding said the Opposition co.o 11: tended that the small tax la hich it was proposed L impose on mining royalties would be a tax upon enclusl; It must be borne in itind that industry had alle:o been rubbed by the mining royalties and the la' is would only take 5 per cent of the proceeds and girt/ back again to industry. (Applause.) The vote was be Artily passed. 'Mr.RANtAee.in responding,said ha had bad a converis- tion with Mr. Robert Lewis who told him that a gel.cb man named Mr. Chrimes attended the meeting "40 he (Mr. Lewis) addressed but apparently had net et. courage-to ask him a question. Since then, howev he bad written a peculiar sort of letter to the Press tit Mr. Lewis wished him (the speaker) to ask that wil`f; Mr. Lewis again came to Warrington Mr. Chricatco should stand up to him and ask his questions face face and man to man. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>!mitt</head>
        <p>!mitt</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. There was a good meeting of this Society on 5ur1.154,1 presided over by Mr. C. Cotton. Mr. George Sol- gave the address upon " How long halt ye LetwiNt,t"i; opinions. If Buil be God serve him. If the Lora •ve God serve Him." Mr. Smiley related the narratite from which the text was taken, and dwelt upon i foolishness and danger of being undecided, the 111311,,,r ness of being one thing or the other, out and out God, or out and out against Him. Mr. A. Littlew.t?Sr, read the lesson. The Secretary led the prayel• W. H. Payton Mus. Bac., presided at the organ. men received their prize list for the half year it- closing. - - _ -</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. John Parkinson. q. At St. Barnabas' Church on Sunday morning, the nt, J. W. Glendenning alluded to the death of Mr. Parkinson, of Lovely-lane, who, he said, lived arao% thetn,and was loved and respected by all. He was 06,41 of no mean attainments.He was loyal to his own and ever ready to lend a helping hand at St. Barnaby',. They would miss his presence very much, but what 5r7 their loss they believed to be his gain. Their deePes. sympathy they extended to his widow, and his soul thef:, commended to the gracious keeping of their Heavev,; Father.—Mr. Glendenning again alluded in fitting to the the late Mr. Parkinson at the evening service.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. John White. f The Rev. J. W. Glendenning, in the course 0 ~e sermon at St. Barnabas' Church on Sunday evenulll) made reference to the death of Mr. John White,. Nv""0-5 life, he said, vas full of energy in any cause v-hich !id for the well-being of the comn.unity. He was a 1°'71,0 and devoted Churchman—devoted to their branch of t Church Catholic. He was a keen Church worker, 8.13 b was chairman of the Warrington blanch of the Logi° Church Onion, a society in which be took the deepe!, interest, because it was a society which bad conterile„,' and was contending, for the faith which he love° "" "to well. In these days,when attempts were being made of filch away their faith, they needed men of the tyPe ye Mr. JOhn White, who, by his social position, must bt:to had great influence upon those with whom he carosirolt, contact. "Eternal rest grant to him, 0 Lord, and perpetual shine upon him. May he rest in peace</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golhorne-street. On Sunday evening the Rev. R. E. Lethercn gave„7o, third of a series of addres.es on Millet's picture or "Angelus." The service was decidedly a moral triuoll,„ over atmospheric conditions that were a temptatioll;t "fainting" both in the apostolic and literal sense. l'it the freshness and force with .which the preacher deas,, with his theme found welcome response in the sustatoe! interest with which he was followed. Mr. Lethere: first pointed out that simple and even commonplace the picture seemed, it yet contained all the elements,L a complete life—work, love, religion. These are re; greatest things in the world and it was because 3filio, had given perfect expression to their union in this Ell„e that the "Angelus" was a great picture ; just as '4O only is a perfect life in which each member of tut. glorious trinity of powers has its proper place and P%t His subject was the" Gospel of Work '; and his t‘e.:it, "In the sweat of thy face shalt th( u eat bread,': a which the artist constantly quoted as exgressing th inmost spirit of his art. Let us consider work kllch curse said the preacher. That is the character in we all all naturally most inclined to consider it. -rite, however, contrary to Scripture and experience. I" To of elements of conflict and compulsion that lend to e view, some show of justification men have discovecs that idleness is the cure and work the blessing. la. preacher then passed on to consider work as a corameT,ti eth ment. Referring to the words "six days shalt t"- labour," he said that was as much a part of the Commandment as "Remember the Sabbath Day to,kedefre, it holy." After speaking of the false ideas of the Irn rich" Mr. Letheren referred to the problem of ent:s-: ployment. The nation, he said, that tolerates the esi-,,h ence on the one hand of a class of people, who thoo 5,, they neither toil nor spin yet are clothed with a gl,c'bre that would leave Solamon in the shade ,• end at ether extreme of numbers who would work but caunt?,, is flying in face of a Divine ordinance, and must reale o'7, the wrong or pay the inevitable penalty of traesgressi? After analysing the various motives with which ruerk work the preacher showed how the consideration of cvero as a commandment of God lifted it into a 111°'00 atmosphere. In the next place work was thought of is a calling and it was pointed out that Bezaleel's metals was as much a Divine calling as that of that through a man's qualifications, choice, and cirolst stances God still makes his will known to those tur, seek him and that this conception of work lends Irlett. to the lowliest labours. The preacher concluded 1-7,0 speaking of work as- culture, the qualities that Ali evolved by honest work are the constituents of Nrc'Ar in manhood and have a value that cannot be express :re wages. The ruling idea of the savage is to scclu'eb possessions without labour. It, is surprising ho•.v 113jtier savagery there is still in civilized countries. oe pointing out what an antidote against temptation sc.t is, the preacher concluded by saying that the ricl,l,_3fs reward of work is increased capacity. That a man lri:t or grows rich by his labour is a by-product. The c''f workman is he whose ruling purpose is the rrerfectloo4 his work. His best gain higher power for higher sreof hereafter. So Christ taught, thus linking the laboura the passing years with the life of immortality.</p>
