      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>THAMES SWIM.</head>
        <p>THE THAMES SWIM.</p>
        <p>The long-distance swim through London was watched by thousands of spectators who lined the banks of the Thames all the way from Richmond to Blackfriars. H. Taylor, of Lancashirc, whose photograph we give, was the winner, J. A. Jarvis being second, and F. Kearsley third. Taylor's time for the Wteen miles was 3 hours 28 mins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>POPULAR BUDGET. I/Poteit Meeting Fiasco at Manchester.</head>
        <p>THE POPULAR BUDGET. I/Poteit Meeting Fiasco at Manchester.</p>
        <p>ex4ll Manchester is laughing about Saturday's sorry e,rienCe of the anti-Budgeters and Protectionists. 1•„"th a tremendous noise of drums and trumpets the s 11;7 leaders of the city—Mr. Joynson-Hicks, M.P., at vlr head—di ed a it for the makers of the Budget, rtecl then gg • P ignominiously found themselves forced to ;titePY it. Their "demonstration "was arranged in a ,accoho.park in Sir Charles Schwann's constituency, and pee ;chug to the advertisements, there were to be 30,000 Arjhe there. Preparations were made on so vast a I e to sweep in Conservatives from all parts of Man- that the Corporation Tramways' Committee were ebti Pur.suaded to put on a service of special cars re Police in large numbers were detailed to Watt) the traffic. Alas! for all these great tlicilectations. The special tramcars were empty, and e,rolice had a compatative holiday. The Queen's 'PK is in a very populous district. Before the meeting tiere wasa concert, and the afternoon was fine. These tl,reenastances helped to collect a good-sized crowd, but ti!.e total was less than one-third of the figure at which I;2' Promoters had aimed, and, as events proved, less (4)4 one-third had come to "curse "the Budget; the ners were all there to shout for the Budget. te`if the speeches of Mr. Joynson-Hicks and of two tt,•ll,tlenten who are candidates for Manchester con- etncies nothing need be said except that they were tCulously aimed chiefly at diverting 'attention from the Budget to Tariff Reform. "Stick to the Budget ! " ire- crowd should, and Mr. Joynson-Hicks showed I.,!tation and threatenened to sit down. be,o usual formula of proposing a resolution at the !Inning of the meeting was dispensed with. tw. Where's your resolution ?" the crowd persistently pp'ecli and the chairman, Mr. Howell, pacifically iiiterelsed that there would be a resolution if a r erruPters would be patient- But before he submitted 4,Bolution he endeavoured in several adroit ways to the proportions of the meeting. One of his tricks to call for cheers for the Chancellor of the drictheqtuer. Many responded, but many, scenting the the invitation, were quiet. Then the chairman "foe cheers for Mr. •Balfour, and the response,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Cle.o\eWke.e"."."o.."‘lll SUMMER PILGRIMAGES.</head>
        <p>Cle.o\eWke.e"."."o.."‘lll SUMMER PILGRIMAGES.</p>
        <p>GREAT BUD WORTH AND FRANDLEY.</p>
        <p>Does the pilgrim seek a respite from the vain and rushing world in which he makes his pence? Would he see what manner of life his forebears lived before they were beguiled to the smoky towns by the prospect of an extra crown a week ?_</p>
        <p>Tliere is an easy answer. Let him take wheels and go to Budworth. A good and direct road will lead him there, but for ourselves having developed a healthy and unabating hatred for motor cars, we choose a happier path and meander through the zig zag roads of Appleton and Antrobus.</p>
        <p>Great 13-udworth makes its whereabouts known to us by the fine embrasured tower of its old church, which, as we first see it rises, solitary above the tree tops. The red and white of old cottages begin to show with rich effect through the green and embowering trees and we ride into the tiny street that runs north from the church to feel that we are in the nattiest, tranquilist, homliest village the county can show. Our coming makes no stir. Two yellow cats walk past us with stony indifference, and a village maiden bestows an unsmiling stare. It is all the welcome we desire. Emboldened by the quiet we walk to the village pump, wash our hands, and take a long and satisfying drink.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>FROM THE CHURCH TOWER.</head>
        <p>FROM THE CHURCH TOWER.</p>
        <p>We decide to take our geographical bear- ings by a climb to the top of the tower, and after exchanging the proper financial civili- ties with the sexton, are rewarded by one of the most enchanting views that can be found. At our feet is the great lake of. Budworth eighty acres in extent, its shores decked with the green pasture land and woods of Marbury Park, the Hall of Marbury with its lawns and gardens standing beautiful and inviting at the western end. Beyond, the eye travels over a reach of fertile country that stretches far away to sea. To the south-east lies Pickmere, its waters dotted with pleasure boats and glistening in the Sun. Eastward we have the charms of Tabley and of the rich country that lies around Tatton Park and Knutsford, whilst to the north almost hidden amid the luxurious foliage of its Park is Arley Hall. But we have not escaped entirely from reminders of the smoke amid which we earn our daily bread, for the chimneys and vapours of Northwich and Middlewich, black and hideous, dot the landscape on the southern side. Along the valley to the south they spread for miles and inspire a momen- tary dread lest they should advance to poison and transform the sleepy little paradise at our feet. But some Canute, of more potence than he who tried to stay the waters has said "Thus far and no farther." Budworth is in the great surging world of salt pans and steam hammers but it is not of it. The quaint streets to ,which we descend are such as our great-grandfathers looked upon in days when railways ran only in the dreams of Mother Shipton and when motors lay far in the dim unknown. STILL WITH THE RHYMING SQUIRE.</p>
        <p>At Budworth as at Arley we cannot escape the spell of the dear old rhyming Squire. Under the shadow of the spire stands the village hostelry, the George and Dragon, over which there hangs in hammered iron a spirited representation of the fietce struggle of our patron saint. With a desire for the pilgrim's welfare the thoughtful owner has placed in conspicuous letters in the porch— As Saint George in armed array Doth the fiery dragon slay, So mayest thou, with might no less, Slay that Dragon Drunkenness. But the most beautiful of his verses is in the lych house at the bottom of the hill. Spring- ing from the cold rock below and running unaffected by heat or frost, from time immemorial is the famous Budworth well.</p>
        <p>The late patron of the village has enshrined it in a beautiful timbered house and engraven above it a fitting epitaph. Blessings in never ending love -Are on us poured from heaven above; This running stream with ceaseless flow Springs Imin the bounteous earth below, Alike to both his goodness shown Whom heaven and earth their Maker own. A MEMORY OF BARNABY.</p>
        <p>But the inscription over the George and Dragon has started another memory. Has not Budworth a place in the verse of that roistering genius Drunken Barnaby " ? It must be two and half centuries since he wandered here in search of sentiment and fresh air but his song as he recalls the visit is of neither. Thence to the Cock at Bud worth where I Drank strong ale as brown and berry : Tin at last with deep healths felled, To my bed I was compelled : I for state was brively 'scorted, By two porters well supported. We seek the "Cock o' Budworth " and find a spacious inn rendolent of the past. If the pilgrim cares to step within he may see in the parlour a painting which shows Barnaby being carried to bed and he may be be moved to a sigh at the remembrance of this, another of the long line of the inspired ones—of whom Bobbie Burns was chief— slain by "that dragon drunkenness." There is much we love to remember about these children of Nature. Alas that there should .be so much for us to forget! A beautiful spout head dated 1713 adorns the gable of 'the Inn and tile sun-dial with its motto "So! motu gallus cantu moneat " will appeal to all lovers of the picturesque.</p>
        <p>We- -admire to our full content the fine timbered houses and pause to look over gate- ways at the gardens filled, in old-world style, with flowering plants. Again we are arrested, yes, it must be the old squire for we know his style by now. Cut large upon the house front in letters big enough for every passer by Take thy calling thankfullie, Love thy neighbour, neighbourlie, Shun the path to beggarie. BUDWORTH'S FORMER PARISH.</p>
        <p>We stroll back up the steep street to spend another brief half-hour at the Church. The old schoolhouse is there to admire though shorn by neglect of its beauty, and we speculate upon the age of its massive stones. The fabric of the Church impresses us, and, as we reflect upon its size, find explanation in the remembrance that this stately pile was once the centre of the largest, or next to the largest, parish in Cheshire. Twenty villages were compassed in it of which Stockton Heath was one. If the reader will take a map and trace out the large parish of which this was the only church it will give him a curious and not very pleasant reminder of the spacious days of long ago. To this quiet "Gods acre" were carried the bodies of many in whom we have an interest, and we seek out in the pleasant shade of a yew tree the resting place of Thomas Eaton the first Quay Manager, the first and honorary schoolmaster of Stockton Heath and the pioneer of total abstinence.</p>
        <p>We came to Budworth primed with dates and facts and architectural lore and were to write an article that would flavour of deeds, and parchments. But the day is fine and</p>
        <p>there may be wet Saturdays beyond, when readers will not disdain large doses of Anno Domini. No, we , will bestride the wheel and spend the golden hours in leafy lanes. Pausing only to buy a picture post- card we take the road to Comberbach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MARBURY HALL.</head>
        <p>MARBURY HALL.</p>
        <p>Again we are beguiled. A quiet footpath guarded by a wicket gate leads from the roadside through the park to Marbury Hall. Who could resist the shelter and the scent of pines? And who could help pressing further to where the waters of the Mere meet the emerald green of the meadows. We sit down to watch the sparkling waters, to admire the fine Renaissance Architecture of the Hall. We remember too the wealth of art objects that are sheltered there, and wonder whether some day we may be per- mitted to pass the portals and survey them. We bestow a lingering thought too upon the Barrys of Marbury, who for generations have paced these terraced lawns, and left them to their successors. And we remember— the swans that glide lazily at our feet, don't share our feelings, for they don't know their masters griefs—we 'remember that for dwellers in our stately halls the light of life has now gone out—all because of that extra halfpenny in the Budget.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>FRANDLEY AND ITS QUAKER CHAPEL._</head>
        <p>FRANDLEY AND ITS QUAKER CHAPEL._</p>
        <p>We leave them to their sorrows and after a glimpse of Comberba,ch emerge at the village of Frandley. The traveller on the main road ought by all means to spare a moment for the village of Frandley. Only a few yards from the highway and barely hidden from it is the quaintest _Quaker chapel we shall find for many miles. Fully two hundred years old it takes us back almost to the days of Penn and Fox. A modern roof covers the old walls but does not take away the fine flavour of age which is the chief glory of an old building. The ground floor is occupied by stables and a spacious shed, and we ascend an outside time-worn stairway of stone to reach the meeting room. To this quiet chamber Cheshire Quakers have gathered for generations and it requires no effort of imagination to people the still room with the figures who thronged it long ago.</p>
        <p>" Reader " wrote Cliarles Lamb " wouldst thou know what true peace and quiet mean, wouldst thou enjoy at once solitude and society? Dost thou love silence deep as that 'before the winds were made ' ? Go not into the wilderness, descend not into the propudi- ties of the earth; go with me into a Quakers meeting. To pace alone in the cloisters or side aisles of some cathedral, time-stricken, is but a vulgar luxury compared with that which those enjoy who come together for the purpose of more complete, abstracted solitude. This is the lonliness 'to be felt.' The Abbey Church of Westminster hath nothing so solemn, so spirit-soothing, as the naked walls and benches of a Quakers' meeting." Here surely if anywhere one would find that primeval stillness which so mightily impressed the poetic soul of the essayist. At the- west end of the building stands an ancient oak full fifteen feet in girth and noble even in its lingering days of decay. No one surely can look on such a monarch of the soil and remain unmoved. Except all the findings of naturalists be wrong it stood here when the broad acres of England were divided up by William the Conquerer or in the more distant day when Ethelfleda sur- veyed the plains of Cheshire from her fortress on Eddisbury Hill, and it may yet, if the storms are not too unkindly,live on to inspire our children's children with the emotion it creates in us. A.M.</p>
        <p>..,,,..". MY LIFE 5'171i7..1</p>
