      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>rd'‘OAU'i'i‘ICMANIF.</head>
        <p>rd'‘OAU'i'i‘ICMANIF.</p>
        <p>A correspondent writes to us interestingly on the subject of a quotation in our notes of last week :—"With the writer of your column entitled 'Our Miscellany' I have been much interested in reading the Economic Sophisms or the Fallacies of Protection by Frederic Bastiat. Everyone who reads this work will agree with Mr. Asquith That the cardinal doctrines of Free Trade have never been more cogently presented or more brilliantly illustrated.' To those who overlooked the paragraph given in your column last week I may briefly summarise. Bastiat supposes that a plank is washed up on the sea shore of Robinson Crusoe's island, and Crusoe's first impulse is to use that plank, but he is supposed to reason with himself on the usual Tariff Reform lines ; If I get this plank it will cost me only the trouble of carrying it, and the time needed to descend and remount the cliff. But if I form a plank with my hatchet, first of all it will procure me fifteen days' employment, then my hatchet will get blunt, which will furnish me with the additional employment of sharpening it ; then I shall consume my stork of provisions, and to replace them will be a third source of employment. Now labour is wealth. It is clear that I should ruin myself by getting the plank, I must protect my personal labouT, and now that I think of it, I can even increase that labour by throwing back the plank into the sea.'</p>
        <p>* -* * *</p>
        <p>"There is no doubt that representing Crusoe as the nation, this forcibly illustrates the folly of Protection from the standpoint of , national wealth. I would like to point out that the argument is incomplete and hardly covers the whole ground as represented by present conditions. Suppose we introduce Friday into the illustration. Suppose in addition to the plank Crusoe got everything he required washed up on the sea-shore and all he had to do was to collect these good things. Were his wants all so supplied how would Friday fare? Crusoe possessing by the right of preoccupation and ruling by superior force and intelligence the whole of the island, Friday could only exist on the charity of Crusoe. If there were no further wafits to be supplied Friday's capital (that is his strength and ability) would not be called into requisition and he could not demand his sustenance from Crusoe in exchange for service. Now this I think would be the line of argument Tat if Reformers would take in regard to this illustration. Some of them would grant from a national standpoint and ignoring the differences in the units, that Free Trade is an undoubted advantage but would contend that the workers represented by Friday would be better off if the demand for their work were created, because only by such a demand could they get a share of the good things that come from the land. We have here an interesting point raised,granted that treating the nation as a unit and ignor- ing the question of distribution the question is: Is it not desirable even at the sacrifice of aggregate total wealth to regulate taxation so that those who have the predominance of wealth would be obliged to spend that wealth in purchasing those things which at present they might procure more cheaply from abroad, and by so doing give employment to the class represented by the Fridays or land- less ones in this country ? "</p>
        <p>Whilst pliying tennis at Llandilo, on Thursday after- noon, Arthur Morgan, a bank clerk, suddenly fell forward and expired from heart failure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MR. BENJAMIN FARRAR.</head>
        <p>MR. BENJAMIN FARRAR.</p>
        <p>My life commenced on the 18th day of February, 1849. Father and mother,although poor, tried their best to improve the lot of their children, of whom there were ten, the narrator of this story being the youngest. Our parents thought much of Scriptural names, so the oldest child was named Samuel, then came David, James, John, Jacob, Israel and Benjamin, also William and two daughters. One daughter died young. The other lived to celebrate her golden wedding day, but just twelve months after that event, she passed rather suddenly to her eternal rest. We were blessed with good parents. My father was a superintendent of one Sunday school for over fifty years. He was connected with the United Methodst Free Church. My mother was a Wesleyan for over fifty years and was one of the best mothers who ever discharged the duties of a home. In my early years she used to take me to her side on Sunday evenings and ask me to read to her some of the most beautiful chapters and lessons of the Bible. The influence of my mother has ever lived with me. Her spirit has fallen upon me, and all that I have 'been, or done, or tried to do, I attribute to the Godly example of my mother and the usefulness of my father. When seven years of age I was stricken down with fever, along with one of my brothers, and for fourteen weeks I was nursed, being com- pletely helpless. In fact, at one stage of that illness, father and mother, with brothers and sisters, kathered round my bed and, as they thought, saw me pass away. But no, it was not God's time, and He mercifully restored me again to the sorrowful ones. At the early age of 8, I was sent to the factory at Copley Mills, a village about half- a-mile from my home at Salterhebble, near Halifax, as a half-timer. I had to rise from my bed soon after 5 o'clock in the rnorning,to be at the mill by 6 o'clock. In the afternoon I used to attend the same village school, where I received at the end of my term a certificate, which I retain in my possession at the present time. The , old-fashioned winters were very trying both to old and young. As I lived on a hill-side I have often had to wear old stockings over my clogs to preveneme from falling, a 3 the roads were just like a glass bottle and steep. From 13 to 15 years of age, I was in the same factory as a full timer. At the age of 15, I was put to learn the trade of wire-drawing at George Patchett's Wire Mill, Halifax. During the years of my apprenticeship, it ,was my great joy to help in Christian work in my native Sunday School and Church. I was a Sunday school teacher and also the secretary of a debating society which did some useful work in discussions upon various subjects such as politics, temperance,theology,botany and astronomy. I was also many years a member of the chapel choir. I have since been honoured, by the teachers of my native Sunday school by requests to preach their anniversary sermons on several occasions.</p>
        <p>I have a lively recollection of the old Elec- tion days, especially 1868, when I was a young man of 20. The candidates were, James Stansfield, later Right Hon. Jas. Stansfield, Mr. E. Ackroyd, first a Liberal, second a Whig. They were opposed by an advanced Radical, E. 0. Greening, a native of Warrington, now of London, who made a strong fight, and a splendid speech upon the hustings, The vast crowd in the Old Piece Hall swayed like a wave of the sea, with a tremendous crush. Mr. Greening was defeated. He was then a young man full of life and fight and lam glad to say the man of ripened age still lives, endowed with vigour and usefulness.</p>
        <p>In 1870, there rose a dispute in the wire trade in the Halifax district, when hundreds of drawers were brought out, as the masters refused the men's terms. It fell to my lot to be drafted off to Warrington, and I com- menced work at Messrs. Rylands Bros. Ltd., at the end of August, 1870. With this firm I have now completed 39 years, 14 years as a wire drawer and 25 years as asiistant 'manager. During part of the 14 years in which I was wire drawing, I was a repre- sentative and,executor for the men at which period we had many cpiestions Of dispute, which needed tact and wise counsel. Just - about the time of a dispute in 1884, the Society men of Halifax became very restless and dissatisfied and were on the point of breaking away from the Society when the executive,including James Hopkins H. Whitham and myself, went to Halifax to meet the men at their club house in Corpora- tion Inn, which was crowded. The executive appointed me to speak to the men for unity, which effort was sufficient to bring the men into line again. Since then, they have become a very strong and united body. The district congratulated me upon the successful effort. In 1880 an experience which I shall, never forget occurred at Messrs. Rylands works, a room containing over a 100 tons of wire giving way and falling to the ground floor. Mercifully not a man was near, as it was 10 o'clock at night. If this accident had happened in the daytime, the writer, and scores of others, would have been ushered into eternity, without a moment's warning. Warrington, during the time I have known it (39 years) has made great changes and there have been striking improvements. The river, certainly for a time deteriorated, but was afterwards improved. Still, there is room for greater efforts in this direction. Man- chester-road has changed considerably. Where once were fields and hedges, there are now many rows of houses. There were also fields in many parts of the town. There is certainly plenty of room for the ideals of a Garden City, fewer houses to the acre, more air, better health and longer life. It was hard for a young man of 22 to leave his native place, with all its associations, but I have never regretted coming to War- rington. The late Francis Dixon was one of the first to give me a friendly welcome, and he also introduced me to the late Isaac Mellor,</p>
        <p>-•aaAleall</p>
        <p>of Bold-street Chapel. Mr. Mellor made me acquainted with his society class, and also found me' a class of boys to teach at the Sunday school, some of whom have grown up to be useful men around me and in the town. One (Mr. T. Wadsworth) is now the president of the Co-operative Society, War- rington, and has also been appointed general manager at Rylands Bros., Ltd., Warrington. It was my joy to assist in cottage services, conducted by Mr. Mellor at Bank Quay. On the 29th day of December, 1870, I married Miss !Elizabeth E. Varty, of 'Halifax, who has shared with me all the difficulties of</p>
        <p>life, with its greater blessings, and has always shown a fine spirit of self-denial and encouraged me in, all the work of Christian service and usefulness. We have</p>
        <p>had seven children, one of whom died young, and we have 12 grandchildren. Early in 1871 we both joined Wycliffe Church, and were welcomed by the Rev. George Sale Reaney, in the old chapel. The union with Wycliffe Church was a happy one.. I soon got to teaching in the Sunday school, having a class of young girls of about 14 years of age, among whom was the present honoured Mayoress of Warrington, also the Misses Dignum, of Sankey. It was my joy to meet Mr. R. W. Murray, who was the superintendent, on joining W ycli ffe Church. Mr. William Beswick, who gave me a right, hearty welcome, was a young Man about my own age. We formed a friend- ship which has never been broken. A short time after this I was asked to help at Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission, where for some time previously Mr. Matthew Knowles had acted as missioner under the Cockhedge firm. Mr. John Barlow, Mr. Knowleb and myself decided to form a night school, We taught the boys and girls to read and write and the simpler sums of arithmetic, in the dining shed, now the Sunday school. We soon afterwards formed from this nucleus a Sunday school. In time it was felt that we ought to have a responsible minister to take charge of</p>
        <p>the work, and Mr. Alfred Smith was appointed. Most successful meetings were held indoors and outdoors, and the room soon became crowded on Sunday evenings. After some time Mr. Smith left, and I assisted to carry on the work without a minister, but the work was too big, and Wycliffe deacons agreed to have a young student from college. After some trial sermons, the choice fell upon the Rev. W. R. Parry of Lancashire College, who worked with great acceptance until the people of Peasley-Cross called him away. Soon after Mr. Parry came to Cockhedge, it was thought well by the deacons of Wycliffe Church to elect, me as deacon for Cockhedge. In time- I settled to work among Wycliffe people, and on the death of Mr. Thomas, Hawthorn, I was elected as deacon at the mother church at Wycliffe which office I have filled up to the present. In 1893 we formed a P.S.A. at Wycliffe. These meetings were to be thorougly un-sectarian and non-political, so that we should be better able to gather in the men of the town. In the early stages the members numbered between 11 and 12 hundred, now they are between five and six hundred, but the reduction is largely due to "Men's Bible Classes and other meetings which were formed. For these reasons we are glad, if by any means, men are brought to serve our great Master, Christ. It was my lot, in the early history of our P.S.A., to act as absentee secretary, then for a time as assistant general secretary. For some years now I have held the office of general secre- tary, and am also one of the Vice-Presidents. Politically, r have ever been a Radical or Liberal, having been influenced by the lives and teaching of men like the Right Hon. W. E. Gladstone,4Vlr. John Bright and others. I have always been a supporter of Temperance, influenced by such men as Sir Wilfrid Lawson, J. B. Gough, and Dr. Lees. Bands of Hope at Wycliffe and Cockhedge have always had my help and sympathy. I am pleased we have still amongst us Mr. P. T. Winskill, senior, with whom I worked for temperance in those days. I have long taken great interest in the Co-operative movement and for some years have been on the Board of Management of our local society. Our objects are to help each and all of our members. We oppose sweating and tyranny, and-work as members and brothers to do a legitimate trade, under the highest conditions of labour., As I before said, lam a strong Liberal, but in town matters, I am convinced that the most honoured business men, irres-</p>
        <p>pective of parties, should be elected, men who will unselfishly serve the town with brains, tact and wisdom. Perhaps I ought to say, that some short time ago I was requested to allow my name to be sent up with others to the Duchy of Lancaster, for appointment as a Borough magistrate, but I had to decline as the time was not my own when duty should be performed on the Bench. It is rather a difficult matter for one to write his life story, because of the self that must be</p>
        <p>prominent, yet I hope it may encourage all who read to work,—for the night cometh, when no man can work.</p>
        <p>When I was leaving home my father said</p>
        <p>he had no fear for in-y future, as God was with me to counsel and direct my path. That</p>
        <p>I have more than realised. New homes and new associations soon became familiar. At that time Warrington's political life was very high. . I shall never forget the speeches given upon the old Public Hall platform, now the Royal Court Theatre, by the late Mr. Peter Rylands, M.P., the emphasising of the words with the outstretched arm, and the stamp of the foot. They filled my young life with spirit, energy, and inspiration. Mr. Rylands, then the member for Warrington, was supported by the late Messrs. S. Rigby, J. G. McMinnies, John Crosfield and George Crosfield. It was a pleasure to work in those days for the Liberal party, backed witfi men like the above. There are still many men in the town whom I have known, and worked with in the early days, and I wish for them many more happy years of public service to the town. I have, like many Christian workers, been discouraged in Christian work, but sometimes we are encouraged. Two years ago a man joined our P.S.A. He askecl me if I remembered speaking at Town's End</p>
        <p>eleven years ago. I iaid I had spoken there, but I asked him what I was speaking about, and he told me. I was then satisfied, because I well remembered the time. He said my words made such an impression upon him that he shortly afterwards joined a Church in the town, and has ever since been active for God. Let this encourage others as well as myself.</p>
        <p>. ALWAYS KEEP HANDY A BOX OF , . _ • ~.,,- , a t ~ . . THE EVER READY HERBAL HEALER . Made solely from rich and rare herbal juices, Zam-Buk is an ever-ready and un- failing remedy for eczema, ulcers, bad leg, ringworm, and piles. Soothing and anti- septic, Zam-Buk is also recognised as the ideal dressing for cuts, bruises, sprains, sore feet and chafed skin, because it prevents festering and blood-poisoning and heals naturally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>HATTON SHOW.</head>
        <p>HATTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>The quiet little village of Hatton was alive with enthusiasm on Saturday last, the occasion .being the 15th anniversary of the Horticultural Society which was held in ideal weathe,r. For a number of years the Show has been held at Hatton Hall by the kind per- mission of Mr. James Parr, but owing to the illness of Mrs. Parr a change was made this year. Mr. J. J. Whitley came forward and very kindly offered to the Committee the use of his grounds at Hatton Cottage. His, offer was gratefully accepted and the result was a huge success. Considering the late season the quality of the flowers, farm and garden produce was quite up to the standard of past seasons. The cottagers " collections of vegetables " were specially fine. In conversation with one of the judges, the Latr remarked that they were much above ' the average. In addition to the exhibits the large marquee was tastefully decorated at each end with greenhouse ad stove plants, kindly lent for the occasion by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart„ and Mr. T. H. Lyon. Mr. Birch, of Acton Grange, also sent a beautiful display of plants and cut blooms. These added very much to the floral beauty of the long range of exhibits. A new feature was the introduction of classes for poultry. Considering that it was the first attempt the exhibits were of good quality, in fact the poultry judge remarked that some of them were fit for classic com- petition. The Indian game and Black Orpington's</p>
      </div>
