      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>GROUP AT HATTON.</head>
        <p>A GROUP AT HATTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Billington, Mr. John Wright, Rev. T: W. Sturgess, Mr. George Hatton. (secretary), Mr. Baxter, J.P., Rev. Hon. C. F. Cross, Miss E. Hatton, Miss Darlington, Dr. Bower, Mr. West, Mr. J. Done, Mr. 11. Bannister. Mr. It. L. Warburton, Mr. W. Fowles, Mr. NV. Blacksbaw, Mr. H. Maddock.</p>
        <p>were specially good. We look forward to speedy pro- gress in connection with exhibition poultry in this district. With a somewhat confined radius 63 entries must be coesidered very good. We hope that com- mercial interests will be found possible with the con- junction of -horticulture and poultry farming. The farmers are to be congratulated for the keen interest they take in the show. A review of the catalogue will show how well the farmers' classes are filled. The produce exhibited, both in root and cereal crops, was of the highest quality. The judges were :—Farmers' produce, Messrs. Thomas Baxter and Herbert Beech; cottagere gardens and produce, Messrs. James Parkinson and J. V. Smith; table decorations, Mrs. Shepherd-Cross and Mrs. Burder ; wild flowers, Mrs. Chorley ; poultry, Rev. T. W. Sturgess ; butter and eggs, Miss Whitlow; honey, Mr. Herbert Potts.</p>
        <p>Alkinson's Cafe Limited very Creditably catered for visitors during the afternoon. Great credit is due to the hard-working and enthusi- astic committee, ably assisted by their 'energetic secretary, Mr. George Hatton. The arrangements were carried out without a hitch. We hope the enthusiasm will last, and that this flourishing Society will continue to thrive.</p>
        <p>duce a great many wore in this country titan they did. The Hon. and Her. C. F. Cross said he wculd like to say hpw very much they owed to their painstaing and most indefatigable secretary, Air. George Hatton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>OPENING CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>THE OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>The opening ceremony which was well attended was performed by Lady Brooke of Norton Priory. The Rev, the lion. C. F. Cross, who presided, said Lady Brooke hail been happily occupied during the</p>
        <p>they would all do well to remember and secondly for coming from a district which was outside the district of the show. (Applause). The resolution was unanimously passed. Mr. R. L. Warburton proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. J. J. Whitley for allowing the use of the beautiful grounds for the show. Mr. T. M. West seconded, and the resolution was cordially carried. Mr. J. Wright moved a vote of thanks to the judges for the admirable work they had done in awarding the prizes. The exhibition did credit to the neighbourhood and he felt sure that the judges had done their work in a manner which had given every satisfaction throughout. They had opened a new satisfaction throughout. They bad opened a new venture in the exhibition of poultry, and they had a great authority present in the person of the Rev. T. W. Sturges. Mr. R. Billington seconded and said he had always been satisfied with the decision of the judges. So long as they had such competent judges as they had at the present time, be did not see that the show could be otherwise than a success. (Applause.) The resolution was passed with unanimity. The Rev. T. W. Sturges, in reply, maid it had been a great pleasure to judge the first open show of poultry. He could assure them that there were some exceedingly good birds which ought to enter some of the classic exhibitions. He would like to commend to them the very beautiful three pens of Indian game and also the first class Orpingtons. It was a very encouraging start and he was very glad to see on looking through the catalogue that all the prizes had not gone to one man but that they were pretty evenly distributed. He was very glad to hear Lady Brool.e speak about poultry. They all liked a good new laid egg, and they could pro.</p>
        <p>T Britland ; 2, J Forster; 3, P Grounds. Parsley: 1, J Taylor.; 2, J Miller ;3, T Grounds. Tomatoes (red): 1, P Cliffs; 2, W Yates ; 3, John Green. Rbubarb : I, T Grounds: 2, II Maddock. Collection of vegetables: 1, H Maddock; 2, John Green; 3,T Grounds; ho, T Britland.</p>
        <p>FRUIT.-Apples (cooking) : 1 and 2, A Platt • 3, J Done; h c, T Grounds. Apples (dessert) : 1, A Platt; 2, %V Yarwood ;3, P Cliffe ; h c, J Taylor. Pears: I,J Miller: 2, T Grounds. Gooseberries: 1, T Grounds; 2, W Duncalf ; 3, R Pennington. Red currants: 1 and 2, J Taylor; 3, .1 Britland. Black currants: 1, Grounds ; 2, El Maddock ; 3,T Grounds; h o, J Taylor. Mums: 1, T H Rutter; 2, James Kippax ; 3, J Britland. MISCELLANEOUS CLASSES.-Collection of wildflowers and grasses (children between 12 and 15), prizes by Mrs. Shepherd Cross: 1, James Kippax ; 2, Mend Pollard; 3, Henry Dutton; h c, Fred Cliffe. Ditto children under 12 years of age: 1, Annie Kippax ; 2, Elsie Bannister; 3, Percy Cliffe; h c, Alice Cliffe. Prettiest floral decorations for the dinner table (prize by Lady Greenall): 1, Miss E M Hatton; 2, Mrs A Woods; 3, Miss M Earp. Six vases of sweet peas (distinct varieties): 1. 2 and 3; W. Yarwood; h c, H Williams; Collection of cut blooms: .1, P Cliffe; 2, J Cliffe. Brown hen eggs: 1 and 2,1 V Heesom ; 3, J C Taylor; h c, R Billington. White hen -eggs; 1, Executors of Blackshaw ; 2, G Blackshaw; 3, F Hatton. FARMERS ONLY.-Butter: 1, Mrs Taylor; 2, Exors of J Blackshaw ; h o Mrs Clare. Potatoes : 1, T Warburton; 2,1 V Fowles ; 3, R L Warburton. Swede turnips; 1, H Billington : 2. 'S Read ; 3, II Mawdsley. Common turnips: 1, H Billington ; 2, S. Read. Ox cabbage: 1, J Dobson; 2, J Robinson. Red marigolds: 1, J Derbyshire ; 2, T Warburton ; 3.W FONVICS. Yellow mangolds : 1, S Read ; 2, H L Warburton ; 3, 1-1 Mawdsley; h e, J Derbyshire. Wheat: 1, W Fowles ; 2 'l.' Whitlow ; he, Exors. of J Blackshaw. Oats (white) : 1, II Mawdsley ;2, W Blackshaw ; 3, W Fowles; h c, L Leather. Special prize given by Rev C F Clifton-Smith for the best collection of farmers produce : I. U L Warburton: h c T Warburton. Yellow mangalds : Messrs Dickson ad Robinson's prizes) ; 1, John Whitlow; 2, L Warburton ; h c, H L Warburton. Swede turnips : 1,</p>
        <p>R L Warburton; 2, A Broome; hc, II Brocklehurst. Yellow globe mangolds (Messrs. Carton's Limited prizes) : 1, John Darbyshire ; 2, 11 Mawdsloy. Swede turnips : 1, II Mawdsley ; 2, W Fowles; h e John Darbyshire. Yellow globe trangolds (Messrs. Clibran's prizes) : 1, Exors. of J Blacksbaw ; 2, W Fowles ; he, R Billington. Purple or green-top swedes: 1, R Billingtou ; 2. W. Fowles; h c, Exors. of J Blackshaw. Swede turnips (Messrs. Webb and Son's prizes) : 1, It Billington ; 2, W Fowles; 3, John Whitlow. Mangolds I, W Fowles; 2, John Whitlow; 3, A Broome; h c, J C Taylor. Swedes: 1, It Billington ; 2, H L Warbur- ton ; H Mawdsley. Box of 20 potatoes: I, T War- burton ; 2, W Fowles ; 3, It L Warburton. Box of potatoes (prize by Mr It If Leigh ) : 1, T Warburton ; 2, W Fowles ; 2, It L Warburton. POULTRY.-Wyandotte cick any variety: 1, Mrs I Hewitt; 2, Mrs I Hewitt, Wyandotte hen (any variety): I, Mrs I Hewitt ; 2, E Bannister ; 3, E Bannister ' • h c, .1 Done. Orpington cock (any variety) : 1, Mrs 1 Hewitt ;2, W Blackshaw ; 3, I? G Fairhtirst ; hc, F G Fairhurst. Orpington hen (any variety) : 1, W Black- shaw ;2, G Fairhurst; 3, F F Memo ; hc, Mrs I Hewitt. Leghorn cock (any variety) ; 1, Mrs I Hewitt; 2, J. Dobson. Leghorn hen (any variety) : 1, Mrs I Hewitt. Rock cock (any variety) : 1, Mrs I Hewitt ; 2, T. Ditchfleld ; h c, T Ditchfield. Rock hen (any vneety) : 1, J Done; 2, T Ditchfield ; hc, T Ditchlleld. Game cock (any variety) :2, F U Fairhurst. Game hen (any variety) : 1, F G Fairhurst; hc, F G Fairhurst. Bantam cock (any variety) : 1, E Bannister. jun. Bantam hen (any variety) : 2, Mrs I Hewitt. Cock (any variety, pure or cross bred), for table purposes : 1, Mrs I Hewitt. Ditto for laying purposes : 1, W Blackshaw ; 2 Mrs I Hewitt ; Ii c, John Done* Drake (any colour): 1, E. Thomason; hc, 1' Whitlow. Duck (any colour) : 1, T Whitlow; hc, E Thomason. Beet turkey cock : 1, Miss War- burton. Best turkey hen : 2, Miss Warburti,n. Special prizes by Mrs. Isaac Hewitt (Stretten) for best Ply- mouth Rock in show : Mts. Hewitt. Best black Orpington : Mrs. I. Hewitt. Best leghorn : 1, Mrs. I Hewitt. Special prizes given by Mr. F. G. Fairhurst (Whitlry) for best Wyandotte fowl in show : Mrs. I Hewitt.</p>
