      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LADY BROOK A:;1) MRS. BUILDER</head>
        <p>LADY BROOK A:;1) MRS. BUILDER</p>
        <p>week, and they were very grateful to her for suc'a a neighbourly act as coming to visit thew that afternoon. They must extend their best congratulations to Sir Richard and Lady Brooke and their son for all that that week meant to them. He hoped when Lady Brooke looked round the show that day she would remember that she-was looking at a growing boy who had lived in Hatton for fifteen years, because that was their fifteenth annual exhibition. They had also added to their show this year, an exhibition of poultry. This was a new feature, and he hoped that that would be produc- tive of much good. (Applause).</p>
        <p>Lady Brooke said it was a real pleasure to be present. It was only the fear that she might be prevented at the last moment from coming which caused her to refuse their kind invitation in the first instance. She had to thank Mr. Cross very much for his kind congratulations myher son attaining his majority. They bad been very fortunate in the weather, and she hoped that .the fine weather would continue during the harvest as it was of great importance to everyone. (Hoar, hear).Mr.Cross had referred to the show as a growing boy. She could only say that if it went on growing at this rate,it would soon be so large that they would have to move into larger quarters. (Hoar, hear). They had up to that time had really no summer weather, but tho little she had seen as yet of the show proved that it was wonderfully good. The attention, and care, and love of a garden, brought its reward in the long run. Although they as gardeners were apt to be discouraged and perhaps unduly cast down when their plants did not come up to the standard, they in the end gained success in this as in other pur- suits. The exhibition of poultry was a new feature and she most sincerely congratulated them on the way it bad been taken up for she was told there were over 60 entries. Poultry had really been too much neglected in this country. She believed they imported several million pounds worth of eggs from abroad every year. Most people in these times did not sutler from having too much money—(hear, hear and laughter)—and she thought it would be a very good thing if some of those millions could be kept at home by improving both the quality of their poultry and increasing the number of eggs produced. (Hear, hear.) It now only remained for her to declare the show open end sho wished the society every success and prosperity in the future. (Hoar, hear, and applause.) Mr. THOMAS WAREURToN proposed a vote of thanks to Lady Brooke. ahe Rev. J. S. Barnes seconded and remarked that they owed Lady Brooke a double vote of thanks, first gf all for having said in her opening speech things which</p>
