        <p>GREENHOUSE PLANTS.-Fuebsia (single) ; 1,2, and 3, F Kippax. Fuchsia (double) : 1, 2 and 3, F Kippax. Begonia: 1, 2 and 3, F Kippax. Geranium: 1 andj2, F Kippax; 3, P Cliff°. Ivy geranium: 1 and 2, F Kippax ; 3, P Chile. Three plants (in or out of flower) any variety: 1 and 3, F Kippax; 2, P Cliffs. WINDOW PLANTS.-Begonia: 1 and 3, J Kippax; 2, J Taylor. Fuchsia (double): 1, H Maddock. Fuchsia (single) : 1 and 2, John Kippax; 3, J Rutter. Geranium : 1 and 3, .1 Taylor; 2, H Maddock. Pot musk : 1, II Maddock ; 2 and 3, J Taylor ; hc, J Miller. ivy geraniums: 1, II Maddock; 2, J Taylor; 3, J Rutter; hc, E II Cotterill. Throe window plants (in or out of flower) any variety: 1, J Rutter; 2, J Kippax; 3, J Taylor; h c, J. Rutter. CUT FLOWERS.-Double dahlias: 1, J Miller ; 2, J Taylor. Single dahlias: 1, F Kippax; 2, J Taylor; 3, J Rutter. Cactus dahlias : 1, T Grounds; 2, J Rutter; 3, It Pennington. Pompon dahlias: 1, T Grounds; 2. J Taylor; 3. J Rutter ,• hc, E H Cotterill. Asters : 1, P Chile ; 2, J Cliffe ; 3, T Grounds. Carnations: 1, P Cliffe ; 2, J Taylor. Pansies : 1 and 2,F Kippax '•3, P. Chile. Violas: 1, E H Cotterill ;2, T Grounds ;3, W Leach ; c, P Chile. Spikes Ten-Week Stocks ; 1, T Grounds; 2, T Britland ; 3, T Grounds; Ii c, J Green. Spikes gladiola: 1, E II Cotterill. Spikes phlox : land 3,3 Taylor; 2,J. Kippax; hc, IV Dean. Roses : 1, J Kippax ;2, J clifre ; 3. J Green; hc, J Taylor. Sweet peas (mixed) : 1 and 2,W Yarwood ; 3, J Kippax. French marigolds: 1, E H Cotterill ; 2, F Kippax ; 3, 1' Grounds. African marigolds 1, J Taylor ;2, F. Britland. Collection of cut_blooms : 1, P Chile.</p>
        <p>VEG ETABLE.-Three varieties of potatoes: 1, J. Taylor; 2, T. Britland ; 3, W Ball ; h c, W Leech. Cabbage rod: 1, T Grounds ; 2, T Done; 3, J Miller; c T Done. Cabbage (white) : 1 and 2, T Done ; 3, T Grounds; h c, J. Taylor. Cauliflowers : 1, T Grounds; 2, J. Taylor; 3, H Maddock. Onions: 1, T Grounds; 2, J Miller. Bulb shallots': 1 and 2 T Wilkinson ; 3 and h c, W Duncalf. Leeks; 1, .1 Kippax ; 2, F Kippax ; 3, J Forster. Peas : 1, II Williams ; 2, J Done; 3, J Miller; h c, J Taylor. Broad beans : 1, J Taylor; 2, J Chile ; 3, 1' II flutter; h o, W Yarwood. Frenchbeans : 1, J Kippax ; 2, J Cliffe ; 3, T Grounds. Scarlet beans: 1, .1 ; 2, F Kippax ; 314 T Grounds. White celery : 1, T Grounds; 2,.3 Forster ; 3, James Kippax. Red or pink celery: 1, Forster • 2, Kippax; 3, T. Grounds. Vegetable marrow (white): 1, W Leach; 2, II Maddock. Vegetable marrow (green); 1, M Ileesom ; 2, T Ii Rutter; h c, J Taylor, Vegetable marrows (green or white) : 1, H Araddock. Cabbage lettuce: J, H Maddock; 2, W Doan ; 3, HWilliams; h c, J Miller. Cos lettuce: I,J Green; 2, H Maddock; 3, H Williams. Carrots: 1, T Wilkinson; 2, .3 Miller; 3, T Britland, Turnips : 1, E Green; 2, T. Britland ; 3, H. Maddock; h c, F Kippax. Beetroots: 1, T Grounds i 2, lo ; 3,H, Maddock. Parsnips : 1,</p>
        <p>with cut bloom; Mr. C. E. Parker, .1.P., table with cut bloom; Mr. G. E. Medley, table cut bloom; Mr. P. C. Garnett, exhibition cut flowers, vegetables and also a very fine show of Lees in glass case showing the bees in -working. Amongst those who witnessed the opening ceremony by Mrs. R. Garnett were :—The Rev. J. L. Jones, M ssrs. Charles E.Parker, James Dodds, J BA it ken,John Randall, Captain Medley and P. C. Garnett ,Mrs. Parker, Mrs. Dudds, Miss Bolton and the Misses Tinsley. Mrs. Garnett said she h4cl very great pleasure in declaring the 8)10W open. It was the 16th flower show they at Penketh bad had. The fl-st flower show was held in the infant rohool. Then they had a few plants on a few tables, and she got Mr. Lro Grinder!), the g eat botanist, to come to (pen it. She told him it was the day of imall things. It was not the day of small things now, and she could congratulate thorn upon tbe Let that they had such a show that day. They had more exhibitors this y ar than last. They Lad not many birds this sear, but tbe reason for that was the cold season. They bed a great increase in the exhibitors on the other tables. both of flowers arid of fruit, and she did most heart:ly rejoice in the expaosion of that movement. It a great desire of hers to cultivate in of flowers, which was one of the great the ed people always a love hee n solaces of human life. They bad got that day some beautiful exhibitions of flowers. She hoped they would always maintain their integrity, and only show those which they had grown themselves. She would like every one of them to plant a tree. Penketh had not too many trees, and if trees were planted they would be growing while they were sleeping. (Applause). Mr. J. T. Ambler moved a vote of thanks to Mrs. Garnett, "for the graceful manner in which she had opened this record show." • The Rev. J. L. Jones in seconding said that he bad pleasure in doing so, as Mrs. Garnett was a very dear old friend of his. He remembered well the opening of the first show. Turning to Mrs. Garnett, the vicar said, "I think Mrs. Garnett, we were the only two influential persons there." (Laughter.) Concluding he said that he thought the Society had paid itself a compliment by asking Mrs. Garnett to open the show. There were no grounds prettier than hers and she was well 'panned to open the show to-day. (Applause.) Mr. J. B. Aitken supporting,also thanked Mrs. Garnett on behalf of the Society for the manner in which she had occupied the post of president. The resolution was heartily carried. THE PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the prize winners : GARDENS, SW.—Cottage flower garden (large) : 1, J Goulding; 2, S Moss. Cottage garden without green- house (medium) : 1, Sergeant Smith; 2, W Moor. Cottage garden without greenhouse (small) : 1, .1V Scott ; 2, G Leigh. Cottage garden with greeuhouse : 1, J Jackson • 2 I Hampson. Kitchen garden: 1, G Leigh; 1 Carman. Allotments, Penketh : 1, G</p>
        <p>sprays, Japanese variety, outside grown : 1, II Bingham; 2, H Hickling. Show dahlias: 1, .1 Edgerley. Cactus. dahlias: I, J Egerley ; 2, S Mess. Pornpone dahlias : 1, D Bonebill ; 2, H Bingham. Cut flowers (outside grown): I, I Hampson ; 2 D Bouebill. Bouquet of wild flowers (made up by children under 13 years): I, , E Woods; P. Mirs W Fuelen ;3, E Baker. Tray of wild ' flowers (20 varieties) botanical and common names for children under 12 years : L A Rites. Tray of wild flowers, 25 varieties, bo:anical and common narne:, for children over 12 and nyder 11 years: 1, J Irwin; 2. W Baker. Eis q on wild flowers of the district for boys and girls under 13 years: I, J Irwin; 2. A looden. VEGETABLES AND FRUIT.-Potatoes, red : 1, George Carman ; 2, .1 Carman. Potitoei, white : 1, 3 Goulding ; 2' WII Roberts. Box of vegetables : 1, .1 Carrgan ; 2, W II Roberts. Celery : 1, J Carman ; 2, George Carman. Lettuces : 1, J Carman ;2, George Goulding. Peas :1, S Moss •, 2, fl gham. Fn a b beans: 1, George Kirkbarn ; 2, S Moss. Scarla runners: 1. A Watson. Broad leans : I, 3 Carman; 2, S MOSS. Vegetable marrows, white: 1, 3 Goulding; 2, P Sparks. Vegetable marrows, green : 1, J Goulding. Eschalots : 1, S Moss; 2, H Trickling. Cauliflowers: I, .1 Goulding ; 2, I Hampson. Leeks : 1, J- Carman ; 2, J Goulding. Onions : 1, P Sparks ; 2, S MOSS. Apples, culinary : I, J Goulding; 2, W Scott. Apples, dessert: I,George Goulding; 2. G W Clare. Pears: 1, George Goulding; 2, G W Clare. Any other variety of fruit: 1, George Leigh ; 2, I. Hampson. Tomatoes: 1, J Goulding ; 2, S Moss. Beetroots: I,J Goulding; 2 J Carman. Turnips: 101 Goulding: 2,P Sparks. Gooseberries : 1, S Moss, Cabbages : I, IV H Roberts ;2, A Watson, Carrots : 1, H Bingham; 2, J Carman. OPEN CLASS.-Roses (six varieties hybrid tens, six varieties perpetuals) : I, J Hughes; 2, D Bonehill. TABLE DECORATIONS.-Decorated table by ladies: 1, Miss A Dumbill ; 2, Mrs J Waine ; 3, Miss Carrington. EGGS.-Eggs (white) : Mrs F Kilshaw ; 2, J Jackson. Eggs (brown) : I,W Gibbons; 2, W W Shaw. BREAD BAKING.-Loaf (home-baked) not exceeding 21bs. : Mrs Phoenix; 2, Mrs Cheatham. Loaf (home. baked in 21b. tins) from Fairclough and Sons, Limited, "Dreadnought Flour " : 1, Mrs W Higham ; 2, Mrs Cheetham ; 3, Mrs A J Hobson.</p>
