      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, August 21, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Loem HORT' ULTURAL SHOWS.</head>
        <p>Loem HORT' ULTURAL SHOWS.</p>
        <p>Penketh, Hatton, and Lymm,</p>
        <p>PENKETH FLOWER SHOW</p>
        <p>The 16th annual show of the Penketh, Sankey and District Garden and Cage Bird Society took place on Saturday in brilliant sunshine. The weather was extremely hot, and almost wearisome to the flesh, but the' sun's rays gave a brilliant setting to the event, for as it shone upon flowers, elegantly arranged on the tables in a marquee, there was a lovely blending of colours. The show was on a large scale and the entries in the flower and fruit sections were a record, There was a distinct falling off in the bird depart- ment, but it was due, as Mrs. Garnett said," to the cold season." A large number of people visited the show, and besides the artistic side for them to gaze upon, there were sports to witness and the Penketh Tannery Band to listen to. By far the most popular and keen competition was that of the best kept cottage flower garden and the judges had great difficulty in deciding which were the best, but their decision gave satisfaction for the gardens of Messrs. J. Goulding (1) and S. Moss (2) were splendidly set out with flowers of almost every description. The following ladies and gentlemen kindly put stands and groups from their garden, consisting of plants, vegetables and cut, flowers: Mrs. R. Garnett, 'group in centre of teat; Mr. J. B. Aitken, J.P., table decorated</p>
        <p>Leigh ;. 2, J CtinllClD. Climbing rose : 1, F Ditruhtll ; 2, J Jackson. Arupelopis : 1, T Bell ; 2, G Carman. PLANT.-Window plant (foliage) variety not grown for its flowers: 1, W Lightfoot; 2, G Kirkham. Window plant (flowering) : 1, Mrs. Jones ; 2, W Jones. Four - cottage greenhouse plants: I, Hampson. Cottage girden greenhouse: 1, S Moss; 2, I Hampson. CUT FLowErrs.-Cottage greenhotre roses : 1, .3 Edgerley ; 2, S Moss. Roses (hybrid perpetuals): 1, J. Edgerley. Collet thin of swer t peas: 1,1. limps( a; 2, Sergeant Smith. Sweet peas : 1, Miss B D 'ties ; 2, H Bingham. Roses (bybi id teas) : 1, J Edgerley ; 2, GAV Clare. Climbing roses (escluding hybrid tta scented, and. noisette varieties) : I, J Edgerley. Hollyhocks : 1, J Carrington ; 2, I Hampson. ROECS (hybrid perpetuals) : 1, J Davies; 2, 3 Carrington. Roses (hybrid teas) : 1, J Edgerley ; 2, 3 Moores. Antirrhinums : 1,3 Carrington; 2,1 Blimps:in. Stocks: 1, I Hampson; 2, G Kirkham. Asters: 1, J Carrington; 2, G Leigh. Carnations : I,F Dumbill ;.2,H Bingham. Picotees : 1, I? Dumbill ;2, II Bingham. Phlox: 1, Carman ; 2, J Davies. Violas, selr colour : 1, 11 Bingham ; 2, 3 Carman. Tau ive violas, sus varietics : 1, II Bingham ; 2, D Bonehill. Eightten violas, three varieties : 1, II Bingham ; 2, D Bonehill. bix pansies, distinct: 1, le Dumbill ; -2, LI Bingham. Dahlias, cactus: I, J Edg9rley ; 2. I) Bonehitl. Gladioli : 1. II Binglam ;2, J Davies. Dahlias, pomporre : I, D 13i.nehill ; 2, /1 Bingham. Single: 1, J Goulding; 2, II Bingham. ClayEanthenium</p>
