      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BOY PRISON ER'S ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>BOY PRISON ER'S ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Remarkable Affair at Warrington. A boy named William Ellis Caldwell, who was in the custody of the Warrington police, made a daring escape from the cells at the borough police buildings on Sunday.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the boy, who is a native of Iforwich, near Bolton, was before the magistrates, charged with 'stealing a pair of boots and being found on enclosed premises. Tho Chief Constable asked for a remand until Thursday, which was accordingly granted, lie was placed in one of the cells, all was well until night when he asked permission to use the lavatory. Whilst the officer on duty was engaged attending to other prisoners, Caldwell got through a fanlight and dropped into an exercise yard which is surrounded by a twenty foot wall. This he succeessfully scaled by means of the down spout, and made good his escape. Immedi- ately he was missed police officers on bicycles 'searched the district, but were unable to effect a recapture. . The following is the description furnished by the police :—Age about sizteen, height 41t B.2'in. fresh complozion, brown hair, grey eyes, dressed- in grey,</p>
        <p>knickerbocker snit (far worn) grey overcoat, grey cloth cap,dark red muffler ,black stockings, and lace-up hoots.</p>
        <p>SCARCITY OF WASPS. The ways of the wasp are secret and not easily to be ' followed. It may be that we shall still see plenty of them coming forth in a few weeks' time. but, judging by present signs, it seem as if we were going to enjoy nearly the same immunity from them as last year. Obviously there were nut a large number at the end of last season to carry on the stock. The condition which seems to determine, or largely influence, the number of wasps in any year is the character of the weather at the time when the queens are choosing their nesting-places. If it be fine and dry, then they will make their nests with hardly any shelter at all, so that all are drowned out when the summer rain comes. On the other hand,if it is wet and cold when the queens, are looking for a nursery, they will make hotter selection of more sheltered and protected spots, and the young broad is likely to survive. This year they were probably very careful in the search, but the hope is that there were not very many of them searching. 'Nevertheless, the work of the wasp is far from being evil ; it is a great destroyer of other noxious insects.— Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>SPORTS.</head>
        <p>THE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The results of the sports were as follows :-80 yards school boys' handicap : 1, Kilshaw; 2, Brown ; 3, Ainsworth. 80 yards egg and spoon race (ladies) : 1, A Foden; 2, M Hayes; 3, L Woods. 100 yards handicap: 1, Gregson ; 2, Sylvester ; 3, Sparks. 100 yards handicap (up to 18 years) ; 1, F Kaine ; 2, T Cook; 3, W Llewellyn. School boys' handicap: 1, Kilshaw ; 2, Brown; 3, Ainsworth. 80 yards sack race: 1, Dayns ; 2, Iloughton ; 3, Lightfoot. 220 yards handicap: 1, Gregson ;2, Sylvester ; 3, Sparks. Flat race for girls up to 15 years : 1, M Garnatt ; 2 L Woods ; 3, M Goulding. Tug-of-war eight aside; Goulden's side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LYMM FLOWER SHOW.</head>
        <p>LYMM FLOWER SHOW.</p>
        <p>In a pleasantly situated field in Maltman's-road, kindly lent for the occasion by Mrs. Hartley, the Lymm and District Horticultural Society held its annual show ou Saturday. Despite unpromising conditions in the earlier part of the day, the afternoon turned out gloriously fine. The show ground, however, was hardly the centre of attraction that one would have expected, but a variety of causes seem to have militated against a,large attendance. The exhibits, to the num- ber of about 200,were neatly staged in a large marquee. There was a falling off in the number of exhibits, but this must be attributed in a great measure to the bad season. The quality of the flowers and produce was even better than in previous years, and the display was evidence that the society is doing a good work in the district. An outstanding feature which commanded the admiration of the visitors was a display of sweet peas by Mr. Henry Eckford, the great specialist. There were some remarkably fine varieties and the blending of colour was most pleasing to the eye. in the cottagers' section the first prize for sweet peas was taken by Mr. E. Hazlehurst with a handsome collection which included the following varieties : Gladys Unwin, Dorothy Eckford, King Edward and Paradise Redflake. Mr. John Wibberley had an attractive show of cactus dahlias which took first prize in the cottagers' section and he was also successful</p>
        <p>with three choice carnations. The phloxes exhibited by Mr. John Clarke were very creditable.</p>
        <p>By the entrance of the te-nt was a group of plants artistically arranged. This was the exhibit of Mr. George Clarke and it was composed of stephanotis, fuselna hyd. a ngea, Plumbago capensis, Callo a..thiopica, maidenhair fern and geraniums, the whole being surmounted by .Lilitun auratum. A second group exhibited by Mr. A. de C. Parmiter consisted of tuberous begonias, fibrous begonias,. and Kentia. There was also a -beautiful coltecticn (not for com- petition) of hydrangea of the Arabia of Sieboldtii• v.triety.</p>
        <p>_ The vegptable section was fairly good. Mr. Isaac Ball obtained first prize in the potato class with some good specimen-3, hirge, of good weight .and firm, and Mr. liasil Hartley showed some fine turnips. There was no formal opening ceremony. During the afternoon the President (Mr. John Coxon) and Mrs. Co•kon-visited the show ground:- The Lymm Subscrip- tion Band played a capital programme of inus'c and a clever gymnastic display was given by the members of the Oughtrington Gymnastic Club 'under the direction of Mr. T. Leigh. A popular 'feature was the fancy dancing and action song by a contingent of girls who had been welkrained by Miss Brazendale, Miss Welford, ITE33 Swift. and Mr. J. Webb, jun. The arrangements were admirably carried out by the following Committee :—Chairman, Mr. John Tilzey ; Messrs. W. Allman, W. Boond, John Brookes, C. Brown, George Clarke, T, E. M. Enstham, George Hazelhurst, J. H. 'Holt, J. Kirkland, George Leah, Mills, 'P. Nash, W. G. Porter, H. Profit, H. Readitt, C. Richards, J. Walker, Henry Waring, J. Webb, jun , J, Whitlow, George Willan, Wilson, E. Wright</p>
        <p>and W. J. Wrig;ht.—Mr. James B. D. Smith discharged the duties of hon. treasurer ; Mr. J. H. Brazendele acted as hon. secretary, and Mr. W. A. 'Veen as 511°1 secretary.</p>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST. • COTTAGERS SECTION. CUT FLOWERS.—Four roses: 2, John Wibherly: Three stocks : 1, E. Hazlehurst. Six annuals: 2, Job° Wibberley. Three carnations: I, John WibberleY; Four hardy perrenials : 2, IV. Seymour. Three phiVabli John Clark. Sweet peas: 1, E. Hazlehurst ; Wibberley. Six dahlias : l, John Wibberley. 1„" Antirrhinums : I, E. Hazlehurst ; 2, W. Serl2(lir: Hand .bouquet Nsild flowers : I, M. Knowles;</p>
        <p>Knowles. • BEST KEPT AND CROPPED GARDEN.-1 and Fr-- Rush-green district, W. Seymour; 1, Statham district, John Clark. PLANTS IN POTS.—One fuchsia: 2, John Wibberlit One getaniurn : 2, E. Hazlehurat. One bydiangea,,:, 0 Charles Booth. Two n.u-ks : 1, E. lbizelhurst. petnnius : 2, E. Hazelhurst. Two lobelias :It Hazelhurst. One begonia: 2E. Hazelhurst. "PRIJIT.--Nars : 1, William Sumner. Black curra 1, William Sumner. Red currants 1, Willi"' Sumner. Raspberries :1, John Clark. nos: VEGETABLES.—Beetroot: 1, Isaac Bell. MW Oue .1, 'saw. Bell. Kidney beaus : • 1, W. Seymour. r 1, Isaac Bell. ltound potatoes: 1, Isaac. Bell. 15. potatoes: 1, Isaac Bell. Eschalots: 1, W. SeY,'"uti: 2, John Clark. Onions: 2, John Clark. Turnips, , W. Sumner. Cabbage: 1, John Clark. Red cabillge; 2, Isaac 8011. Cauliflowers: 1, John Clark. Celery •</p>
