      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>MR. J. WEBB, JUN., AND JUVENILE DANCERS.</head>
        <p>MR. J. WEBB, JUN., AND JUVENILE DANCERS.</p>
        <p>SHREWSBURY FLORAL FETE.</p>
        <p>This popular ftste, the largest of its kind in the United Kingdom, was held on Wednesday and Thursday. There were over 3,000 entries and the prize, money reached the very handsome sum of £1,200. Ten large marquees were requisitioned for the proper showing of the plants, flowers, and fruit, and the taste with which the exhibits had been arranged made the spectacle a positive delight to the eye. The groups of miscellaneous plants were particularly charming while a veritable feast of colour was provided by flowers of all varieties. Roses, carnations, geraniums, and that now wonderfully cultivated bloom, the sweet pea, vied with each other in tho gorgeousness of their hue. The official view was that exhibition was fully up to the high standard observed in former years. Competition in the big group classes was very keen, and extra prizes were awarded. The opinion of the judges was that this section of the show was far better than anything they had seen in Groat Britain and that the fine traditions of the Shrewsbury Show, were more than upheld. Messrs. Cypher (Cheltenham) took first, and William Vans° (Leemington) second for fifteen stove plants. Messrs. Cypher first in big groups, Sir George Kendrick (Edgbaston) second, and W. R. Holmes (Chesterfield) third, Mr, - .T. Sutton Timm's, of Allerton, was especially successful in the plant classes. The coveted challenge cup, gold medal, and £2O, won last year by the Duke of Westminster for a collection of fruits, went this year to Mr. Goodacre (Derby). The Duke of Portland was second with gold medals, and the Duke of Westminster third with silver modai. Lord Trevor was winner of firsts in fruit. The Earl of Shrewsbury took third honours in peaches, and second in nectarines. The Earl of Derby was first in melons, and the Duke of Portland and the Marquis of North- ampton in the classes for plums. Lord Harlech was first for dracenas and for six stove plants. Musical selections were given by the imnds of the Coldstream and Irish Guards, and there were some excellent variety entertainments.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Pankhurst, the suffragist loader, will be admitted to the United States, the authorities holding that her imprisonment ma for a political offence.</p>
      </div>
