      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Wociston.</head>
        <p>Wociston.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPTED StilCID E.—William Thornton was charged WI remand at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on aeenesday with attempting to commit suicide at w,loolston on the 15th July by cutting an artery in his „Iltr• Since prisoner was remanded Dr. Corker, assist- medical officer at Warrington Workhouse, examined and as far as he could tell he was quite safe and uete was no danger of him attempting to take his life 11„gain• Prisoner's father volunteered to take care of Prisoner if he would promise to he a teetotaler and sign the Pledge.—PriEoner promised and he was released.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath. SPEED or MOTOR CARS.—Arising out of the minutes of littae Stockton Hsath Parochial Committee at the Runco:n oural District Council r n Tuesday,lllr.Hallows drew the ii?uncil's attention to the speed!of motor cars passing t,ir°ugh the village. He stated lie had seen cars going ,i;religh the village at 10 miles an heur and it was a no accidents had occurred. He was wondering 17.uether danger signals should not be erected in the /I.ll,dag?. It was not motorists resident in the village exceeded the limit, but strangers who didn't know ge district. The matter was referred to the Parochial ,ealtrtittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>- - 1,11/PEN SERVICE.—On Sunday the usual open service t;,"3 ' .held in the large tent that bed been used the PL: .held day for the Penketh and Sankey Garden and %age Bird Show. A large number of people attended, p, e service being undenominatiotal. Mr. C. E. Parker Vt . sided and also gave an an address. Special music t/las iendered by the Penketh Tannery Prize Band and p,,e Wesleyan DI en's Bible class choir sang hymns. Mrs. hurter played the accompaniments. Mr. Nicholls, who pitilrom one of the Londcn missions, was also on the dding Giferril and gave a short address. The Bev. T. opened the service with prayer.</p>
        <p>Moore. scVxDAy SCHOOL TRIP.—On Monday afternoon the olar3 of the Church Sunday schools of Moore, Thresury and Preston Brook tad their trip to Overton. left Daresbury and Norton stations by the two train in the afternoon and alighted at Fiodsbam from whence they rambled up the hill to th.`rrY View. Unfortunately the weather was not of i.oest for such an occasion, It was rather cloudy and little showery, nevertheless the children enjoyed erOsslv es rambling about the hillside and indulging in 0,11:11.,es of various kinds. Tea was served in the pavilion •31er5e View V• after which the younger- children c e racing "i°T i • , g and other sports for prizes. The return "3 made from Frodsham Station about seven o'clock.</p>
        <p>Daresbury. bst-,NDAy SCHOOL TRIP.—The scholars attending the acaresburv, Moore,'Preston Brook and Hatton Sunday iaolB hid their annual trip to Frodsham on Monday About 100 children assembled at Dare3bury and erton Stations at 2 p.m. and they were accompanied "la Vicar of the parish (the Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones), cl 'BB Lpe-Jones and teacl-eis from the various schools. vl? arriving at Frodsbam they made their way to Mersey villa', Overton Bills, where an excellent tea was pro- th edltt 4 p.m , after which sports, tte., were held, and zetcy other amusements Leing well patronised. The olars returned alter a very enjoyable afternoon by the 6.'50 p.m. tiain from Frodsham. Thelwall.</p>
        <p>A DA - .NGEROUS CORNER.—At a mceting of the Runcorn ral District Council on Tuesday, a letter was read tt,,°„tll the clerk of tha Connty Council stating that the e;:7lcil could tot comply with the request of the Rural Thtlirmil to erect motor caution posts in the village of Rr.WaMr. W. B. Cooke, clerk to the Thelwall 11,8 h Council wrote asking of the Rural Council would ttclertake to erect a danger post at the village corner AT the County Council had not undertaken the work.— 1.1.• Pilling stated that he did not know any part of t;,,eshire where he could find a more dangerous corner eall the one at Ihelwall. He proposed that the .._(11Incil should undertake the work.—Mr. Linaker ended and it was agreed to. Stretton.</p>
        <p>- t! DITCH NUISANCE.—At the Runcorn Rural District Itc",ncil on Tuesday, Mr. Joseph Hewitt, of Stretton, vii? Le complaining of the state of a ditch at Stratton itt„age and alleged that sewerage mutter from.it got di," a stream at Fir Tree Farm, and pollufed the 1.:01 the cattle had to drink from.—Mr. Swinton, sur- bntr, said he had examined the ditch and found ta—tinag Wrong with it. He considered it was in a satis- stee,rY state.—Mr. Mawdsley said be had seen the ditch t.,‘ was certainly not fit for any cattle to drink from.— • ,rarrington,(inspector of nuisances) said the ditch 17..11'd be diverted from Mr. Hewitt's farm.—Mr. Pilling: and put it on somebody else's. (Laughter.)—Mr. tlit7,4llcery jun., moved that the Surveyor inspect the attain and give a report at the next Council Meeting. ar•Pilling secionded and the proposition was carried. Padgate. WEDDING AT THE WESLEYAN CHURCH. very pretty wedding of considerable local interest ,3 solemnized at the Padgate Wesleyan Chapel on ‘lltday last when Mr. William Johnson Bate, only 11-„11 of Mr. Luke Bate of Laburnum Villas, Culcheth, el Married to Miss Elizabeth Julia (Lily) Ward, t:Pghter of the late Mr. Henry Ward ard Mrs. Ward, the Barroaby. Padgate. The officiating ministers were William Mellor, of Orniskirk, assisted by the Riv,l'• T. Cladding, of Padgate. The bride who was tra*,. away by her brother was charmingly attired in an sit "Ire gown of embroidered net, oinamented with tpver tissue and wore a lovely veil surmounted by a tor). of orange blossom. Her only ornament was a • Pendant and she carried a beautiful shower bouquet thPosed of Ilium nuratum, white heather and lilies of tve,valley, the gifts of the bridegroom. She was attended of her nephews Masters Charlie and Roy Dakin 42 acted as page boss. The bridesmaid was Miss "ie Ward, sister of the bride w`bo was neatly attired 2,4,1 dress of pink voile and wore a pretty picture 04i °I cream chip, trimmed with old rese velvet and car2ch plume. She wore a garnet ring and • ed a bouquet of yellow marguerites, the • of the bridegroom. Mr. Ernest Bilsbury of The urY very ably performed the duties of best man. ur, service was fully choral, the wellknown hymns, Pather all creating "and "0 perfect love" being 4;g• Mr. A. Domville officiated at the organ, playing h,lectiors before and after the ceremony. As the A/DPY couple left the sanctuary to the strains of theadelssohn's Wedding March they were greeted with (Itisual shower of rice and confetti. After the stieing a reception was held at the home of the bride do later in the day Mr. and Mrs. Bate left for Borrow- e, Cumberland, where the honeymoon is being spent. ti,,!, bride travelled in a Princess robe of grcen sedan vaa, her bat being composed of cream chip trimmed aPricot ribbon and ostrich plume. The following</p>
        <p>bl.l Hit of the presents : de to bridegroom, gold breastpin ridegroom to bride, gold pendant f;Tather of bridegroom, cheque r'"other of bridegroom, tea service and cutlery other of bride, household linen I.lr, and Mrs. .1. Dakin, toilet service VI% and Mrs. A. Ward, electro-plated teapot ttnantdr. and Mrs. W. Cutland, copper spirit kettle and 14. Set Isa Jessie Ward, pair of painted panels and trinket tkaaters Charlie and Roy Dakin water jug "iaster Jess and Miss Molly Dakin, glass tumblers aster Henry. Ward, rose bowl ill% and Mrs. Roberts (Swinton), pair brass horses clotlhias Nellie Roberts (Swinton), embroidered table 11.41448 es Emily and G. Wright (High Legh), E.P. r,dine dish 'lr. • 111 and Mrs F. Gleave (High Legh), cutlery casket r Dean, copper kettle 1412% and Mrs. W. Bennett, E.P. marmalade pot Pitt, silver backed toilet brushes ‘1,1,1• Jordon, dessert dish E. Jordon, silver mounted vases 'um R. Jones (Culcheth), E.P. jam dish and spoon kr, T. H. Bland, salad bowl bisa Wyman (Grantham), cheese dish ,r• E. Bilsbury, bedroom towels bnd Mrs Fryer (Port Sunlight), sugar dredger TiVss Wright (Kelsall), dressing table sundries Proffitt, afternoon tea cloth and bedroom towel t,!'s• England, half-dozen serviettes it'las Richardson, electro-plated toast rack Ass Paterson (Liverpool), plant Mrs.l.- Thornton, table centre and Mrs. 3. Higbam, fruit dish Alr• and Mrs. Jones, duchesse set and jelly dish Wsa A. Smith, taper pocket A,,r• A. Higham, teapot 'Mater E. Higham, steam exhauster %rips A. Smith, wool mat tr!sa G. Hughes, d'oyley L. Sykes, fern pot At!"8 M. Joynson, toilet mats 188 S. Dodd, wool work %„_enior Girls of Padgate Wesleyan Sunday school, r),(3 s and forks tC,Leachers and Scholars of brides Sunday school class, 7.tro-plated sugar basin th"lenibers of Choir and Teachers Padgate Wesleyan Lo Cu, plated sugar basin and cream jug 4'4,123P10y4i of Gemmel' and Harter, Lt., Manchester, 'd mahogany clock and cutlery</p>
