      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS. ‘,,0%•.e1,.,‘,N,Ne-"</p>
        <p>Burtonwood.</p>
        <p>• PIGHTING MEN.—James Haverty and Patrick I ertleY, both of Burtonwood, were charged with fight- en the 7th inst. at Burtonwood.—P.C. Georgeson 't'd i.e separated the n.en and took them home.—They "R ere bound over to keep the peace for three months. GAMBLING.—Jobn Powell of Burtonwood was charged 1;1„th playing a game of chance in Fairclough-road.— ;u• Cartmell said that defendant and another man were 1315Ying for money and he caught defendant, but the .otEer man made his escape.—Detendant was fined is 6d. Including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Helsby.</head>
        <p>Helsby.</p>
        <p>FLOWER SHOW AND SPORTS.—Under the auspices of the Helsby Athletic Club, the annual flower show and sports took place on Saturday, there being a very large trunLer of entries, and an attendance of people from all parts of Lancashire and Cheshire. Vegetables, fruit and flowers were exceptionally strong classes,there Leing some very fine exhibits. The sports resulted as follows:</p>
        <p>Half-Mile.—E. Price, Flint, 30, 1 ; H. S. Wright, Alvanley, 60, 2; R. Birchall, Liverpool, 18, 3. 100 Yards.—Harry Spruce. Halton, 5,1; H. Watkins, Latchford, 6,2; T. H. Johnson, Runcorn, 5, 3. Mile Bicycle.—John Ashley Davenham, 25, 1; F. Johnson, Widnes, 50, 2:; J. Wright, Tarporley, 60, 3. • 440 Yards Obstacle.—Thomas Blair, Warrington, 10,1; Joseph Knight, Latchford, 15, 2; Charles Jones, Frod- sham, 15, 3. - Half-Mile Bicycle.—J. Ashley, 15, 1 ; J. Wright, 35,2; H. Durham, Queensferry, 35, 3. 220 Yards.—T. H. Johnson, Runcorn, 6, 1 ; W. Jones, Tbelwall, 9, ;C. Rogers, Wein' 9, 3. Sack Race.—Thomas Blair, Warrington, 10, 1 ; S. Hilditcb, Green- Bank, 25, 2 ; C. M`Lean. Upton, 25, 3. 440 Yards.—C. Escott, Helsby, 37, 1; J. Lynch, Walton, 25, 2 ; E. Price, Flint, 13, 3. Race to top of Helsby Hill.—J. Narcross, 1; W.Hibby, 2; W. Stockley. 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Cuerdley.</head>
        <p>Cuerdley.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED Ass AUT.T.—At the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednesday, before Messrs. Charles White (chseman), H. Roberts and Colonel Buckton, John Booth, farm labourer, of Cuerdley, was charged with assaulting Patrick Shannessey, another farm labourer, on the 15th inst.—Shannessey said that he and two others had been drinking in a public-house. They were on their way home. Booth along with two companions accosted them. Booth struck complainant, who retaliated in self-defence, but his opponent was too quick for him and hit him behind the ear, stunning him. He still felt ill from the effects of the blow.—Superin- tendent Strickland asked for a remand for a week.— Prisoner: Can I have bail ?—Superintendent : I object, the man is not out of danger yet.—Prisoner was remanded for a week in custody. ALLEGED THEFTS FROM A SHANTY.—Andrew Clark,of no settled abode, was charged with stealing sometime between 6 p.m. on the 7th tind,7 a.m. on the Bth inst. from a shanty at Cuerdley Hall Farm, a silver watch,one jacket and vest, value. £3, the property of Thomas Sreen.—Prisoner preferred that it should be tried at the Quarter Sessions and evidence as then proceeded with. horcas Davies, manager of a pawnshop at Ashton-in- Makerfield spoke to receiving the jacket and vest from prisoner on the 19th inst.—P.C. Carmel said he appre- hended prisoner.—Prisoner was also charged with steal- ing at the same time and place one pair of trousers. one pair of boots and one muffler, value £l, the property of Patrick Noon.—P.C. Cartmell spoke to apprehending prisoner and Noon identified the gcods.—Priscner will be brought up at the next Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>VICTORIA-ROAD.—The Clerk at the Rural Disirict Council meeting on Tuesday said that the objectors to the scheme for making rictoria-read Grappenhall, bad written withdrawing their objections. It was therefore necessary to ask?, the Local Government Board for the loan for carrying out the work and also to advertise for tenders. Mr. Woodward moved and Mr. Pining seconded a resolution authorising this to be done, and it was agreed to. ALLIG ED THEFT.—Before Mr. L. Greening, at the Warrington Occasional Cow ton Tuesday,Petsr Hodgkin- son, Pradshaw-lane, Grappenhall, was charged with stealing five chickens, valued at Os, the property of Emma Watson, 1, Cedar Terrace, Grappenhall, between 2 pm,. on the 30th and 8 a.m. on the 31st July.--The evidence showed that the chickeas were missed on the morning of July 31st. At noon that day prisoner went to the shop of Frederick Dutton, Waste-lane, Thelwall, carrying on business as an ironmonger in Market-street, Warrington, asked him if he would buy some chickens Dutton told him that he did not want any, whereupon prisoner said he would take them down to witness's house. Mr. Dutton arrived home at 6 p.m.,and prisoner was waiting for him. Prisoner said be bad brought fire chickens, and witness paid him 83. for them. Witness asked him where he got the chickens from and he said he got them in exchange for a baby's cot, which he gave to Mr. Watson.—P.C. Wood stated he was preseLt when Mrs. Watson picked the chickens out from amongst '2OO on Mr. Dutton's premises. When charged prisoner said "I have not stolen them."—Prisoner wes remanded until the next sessions at Stockton Heath on September 14th.</p>
        <p>Lymm. LYM NI SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNEsDAY.—Before Messrs. A. Watkin and W. P.</p>
        <p>Rylands. LYAIM STATION SCENE.—Ernest Msrsb, of New-road, Lymm, was summoned for having been drunk and dis- orderly on Lymm Station cn the 24th of last month. Defendant pleaded guilty to being disorderly, saying it was in self-defence.—Barry Tonge, a porter en the station, said that on the day in question he saw defen- dant and another man struggling on the platform. He tried to persuade them to desist tut they would not listen to him. Witness then tried to separate them but was unable to do so. He then went for the station master and be seat for the police. The defendant was not exactly drunk.—Sergeant Washington said a porter came to the Police Station and told him that there was a regular disturbance going on at the station. He went down there in company with Constable Dodd and there saw defendant en the station platform. He used an oath and was beginning to use another when he saw witness and disappeared in the crowd. Witness told Dodd to look after defendant while be got the other man's name and address. There was no doubt about Marsh being the worse for drink and, he also appeared to be very excited. He was a decent man tied there was nothing against his character before then.—Constable Dodd corroborated Washington's statements.—Marsh on oath said he went down to the station to see two of his friends off. While on there be spoke to two lady friends. While doing so,this man with whom he was found strusgling gave him a push and told him it was not a place for him, and then clung round his neck. He retaliated and they both struggled on the:platform. He had only had two glesses of beer that night. After some deliberation the chairman said that a tight on a railway station was a dangerous thing,but as defendant had given his evidence straightforwardly he would only be fined les. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—Robert Forster, of Fairfield Mews, Higher Of enshaw, near Manchester, was sum- moned for working a horse in an unfit state on the 31st July.—He pleaded guilty.—Sergeant Washington stated that on the day in question he was on duty in Church Green-lane when be saw the defendant with two horses attached to a waggonette. He noticed one of the horses was very restless, and on examining it he found a large wound about the size of a two-shilling piece on the right shoulder just under the collar. It was also rubbed raw with the friction from the collar which was much too large. Witness told defen- dant the horse ought not to be working. Inspector O'Brien of the R.S.P.C.A. said he examined the horse and foutei a large wound on its right shoulder. There were also a number of skin abrasions caused by the large collar which the horse was- wearing. This collar had evidently been put on to hide the large wound. The horse was totally unfit for work and was suffering from pain.—The Chairman said the case was a very Lad one and defendant would be fined 20s. and costs.</p>
        <p>OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—EIijah Rathbone of Pepper- street, was fined 10s. for making use of obscene language in Pepper-street on the 17th of July.—Robett Thomason, of Rush-green, Lymm, was also fined ss. for the same offence on the 22nd of July. CONSERVATIVE OPEN AIR MEETING. LIVELY SCENES.</p>
        <p>Quiet little Lymm was invaded by the Tories on. Monday evening, and lively scenes were enacted within the shadow of the village Cross where an open air meeting was held. The Unionist van was in charge of Mr. J. W. Roberts, who is touring Cheshire. Mr. IV. P. Rylands presided. The Tories did not have matters their own way by any means, for quite two- thirds of the audience were against tben..lllent ion t the Budget called fortlr enthusiasm, and appeals to pity the poor landlord were received with derisive laughter. There was no resolution of protest against the Budget It would have been bad tactics for the promoters of the meeting to have put one to the vote, for the verdict would certainly have been in favour of Mr. Lloyd- George's splendid financial proposals. Mr. Rylands spoke amidst constant interruptions nd devoted a large portion of his speech to criticising the Budget. A reference to the licensing trade called forth a question from Mr. Brearley as to whether it would not be better if the workingmen spent his money on household commodities rather than on drink. Mr. Hylands signified his agreement with these sentiments. ' Continuing, he said the Unionist Party at the present time Lad put in the forefront of their programme a particular method of raising the money which was so absolutely essential if any large schemes of social reform were to be carried out. The Radical party had retaliated by saying that those schemes could be carried out by Free Trade finance. They must not get the impression that by putting a big burden upon one man they were going to take it entirely off everybody else, because they could not do it. The money could be much more advantage- ously raised by putting a duty upon•foreign import- ations, because by that method they raised, a substantial revenue, and it would also give work to their work. people by minimizing competition, milli° at the same time they would get a certain proportion of their taxes out of the pockets of the foreigner. Mr. J. W. Roberts prefaced his speech by threatening that if the crowd would not be quiet he would" come down and give it them." At which the crowd jeered and invited him to do so. This caused him to adopt a different tone and be then appealed for a patient hearing. Continuing, the speaker referred to wages in Germany and the United States and maintained that they were higher than they were in England. He stated that the engineers' wages in America were 225. per day. This would not do for Mr. Brtarly who promptly denied it. Mr. Roberts volunteered to debate with Mr. Brearly or any member of the Free Trade Union.</p>
      </div>
