        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>This was accepted and in a few weeks it is txrected the discussion will take place. After Mr. Roberts' speech question time came, and -- - this was promptly taken advantage of. Mr. BrearlY ALLEGED ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT RUNCORN asked Mr. Rylends if the price of food would not be %I ORKHO USE, raised if they gave a preference to the Colonies? On Tuesday morning, at Runcorn, William Whitfield, Mr. Rylands replied that it might be a little, but it an old man of 75 years, who wept copiously, was was not worth mentioning. s W charged with attempting to cothmitt suicide at Dutton In proposing a resolution to Mr. Roterts for hiorkhouse. Mr. Boyce, assistant master, said the address. Mr. Charles Cowper stated that he was the prisoner was admitted into the hospital with a sprained longest employer of labour in Lymm as a fustian cutter knee sustained while on the tramp from Manchester to and he paid the biggest wages. Liverpool. On the 6th inst. he was to be discharged as Mr. Mills seconded, and it was carried, cured, but he was found on the exercise-ground with his A resolution was also pissed to Mr. Rylands. throat cut. Superintendent Ennion said the prisoner Mr. Brearley called Mr. Rylands' alt : ntion to the was a compositor, and belonged to Brownlow-hill. The omission of any resolqtion aga'nst the Budget and asked prisoner said he did not know that he had a soul him why one had riot been put. . belonging to him. He had applied for an old-age _Mr. Roberts was on his feet in a moment and said it pension, but he did not get one because he had had was not usual to put resolutions at outdoor meetings. arish relief. The prisoner was discharged, and sent Mr. Brearley : V% hat about Manchester, Hastings back to the Workhouse. and other places? Mr. Roberts replied that they were not going to put a RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. - resolution. The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was (Voice: "You no very well, if you put it you would held on Tuesday afternoon at the Dutton Workhouse. be beaten.") Mr. T. Wright presided and there were also present : The meeting then terminated. Messrs. T. Boster, E. Britcle J. Balshaw, J. Warburton, W. Whitlow, L. Bolton, H. Mawdsley, S. Woodward,, DEATH OF Mn. T. T. GREGSON.—We regret to T. Watson, J. Hallows, C. E. Linaker, (jun.) J. Haining announce the death of Mr. T. T. Gregson which took T. Antwis, B. Booth, C. Roylance,J. W. Davies, W place on Wednesday morning at his residence Eagle J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances) J. S. Swinton, Brow, Lymm. Mr. Gregson was the only surviving son and G. Diggle (surveyors), G. F. Ashton (clerk), and W. of the late Mr. James Gregson, C.C., whose death took Bough place only a few weeks ago, and the sympathy of the H. (depnty clerk). SITES AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE. whole village goes to Mrs. Gregson over At a meeting of this committee on Monday evening, the loss of her only son following so close the death of plans of the proposed alterations to cottages belonging her husband. Mr. Gregson leaves a widow but no to Thomas Goulden in Main-street Frodsham, were children.--1 he funeral took place yesterday afternoon approved subject to an increased, air space being pro- at Lymm Parish Church. vided at the rear. It was resolved that the houses at BUDGET ENTHUSIASTS AT Lvst.m.—On Thursday Halton Brook be connected with the new sewerage evening a Party of about 26 staunch Lihera's journeyed system at an esti mated cost of £26. -to Altrincham to give their support to the Budget. The The minutes were approved. party left the Nag's Head Square at 6.45 p.m., arriving ISOLATION HOSPITAL. in time for the demonstration, which was a success in Mr. Wood ward stated that there were eight cases of every sense, only one opposing out of an audience of infectious dise,tees in the Isolation Hospital, three eight or nine hundred. 3he principal speakers were from d3ptheria and five frcm scarlet fever. Mr. Hamar Greenwood, M.P. for York, and Mr. W. J. Crossley,M.P. 1 he chairman was Mr.W.E.Thompson. The party were delighted with the stirring speeches in suppoit of the Budget. On the way home the driver had the misfortune to take the wrong turn, finding himself in Bucklow Hill and Hoo Green, reaching Lymm about 12.15 a.m. 1 his would have looked bad had it been a temperance demonstiaticn. But both driver and horses are said to be Tory.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held on Tuesday afternoon at the Dutton Workhouse. Mr. T. Baxter presided. There were also present :—Messrs. C. E. Linaker, jtm., T. Wright. 3. Bolshaw, P. Britch, G. Egerton.. Warburton, J. IA hitlow, J. Warburton, 3. Spruce, J. W. Davies, H. Mawdsley, S. Woodward, G. Pilling, E. Bretherton, R. Collins, J. Hallows, W. Nixon, J. Taylor, J. Mynekyme, T. Antwis, C. Roylance, L. Bolton, W. B. De Prez, J. Pearson, and T. W. Cooke (relieving officers), G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk) and T. D. Rollinson (master).</p>
        <p>VOTES OF CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman siid that he had; been asliel to inform the Board that since their last meeting a very old gentleman who was at one time an inhabitant of that union and also the chaplain of that house had passed away. He referred to the Rev. E. Marsden, of South- port, and be was sure it was the wish of the Board that they should send a vote of condolence through the Clerk to the widow. Poss:bly some of those present might have known Mr. Marsden. He (the speaker) had known him for a great many years and during that time he took great interest it? the welfare of the children of that-union, and also did a great deal towards their edification and entertainment. He was sure they would join with him in passing a vote of condolence to his widow who was now living in Southport. Mr. Wright in seconding said be thought he knew Mr. Marsden better than any member of the Board—in fact he had known him all his lifetime and be always found him the most genial man that ever occupied a pulpit. Mr. C. E. Linaker, sen., who was unable to attend the meeting of the Board through an accident, wrote sug- gesting that it would be a graceful thing if some guardian moved a vote of condolence to Mrs. Marsden. The Rev. C. Marsden acted as chaplain to the Work- house for something like 30 years and he always kept in touch with the young people as they left to go into active life. He knew of many cases of both boys and girls who had done extremely well after leaving Dutton Workhouse had kept in correspondence with Mr. and Mrs. Marsden up the last. The vote of condolence was passed. The Chairman said he had to announce the death of Mrs. F. Norman. of Runcorn. He believed she was a member of the Ladies' Committee and he knew her to be a lady who was always ready to be of any use she could to anybody in the neighbourhood. He was sure they all felt with him what a great amount of sympathy there was for her husband. Tte least they could do would be to send a vote of condolence. He was sure they would all agree with him when he said that such a lady as Mrs. Norman was a great loss to the district. Mr. Woodward drew attention to the death of Dr. Gastaldi and said he was a most genial and kindly man. He had dealings with the Guardians for many yeirs, and those who knew him would agree with him that a vote of cond,dence should be placed on record. Mr. Wright seconded and the vote was carried. A vote of sympathy was passed with Mr. ',linker in his recent accident.</p>
        <p>NUMBERS IN THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce stated that the inmates of the House numbered, 176, compared with 160 the corresponding week last year, and the vagr ants 61 compared with 80</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>HOMING.</head>
        <p>HOMING.</p>
        <p>CROFT.—The third young birds' race in connection with this Society was flown cn Saturday from Gloucester, 100 miles. Ten members sent Si) birds wh;ch were liberated by Convoyer Taylor at 11.40 a.m. in cloar weather, with south-west wind. Result and velocity : E. W. Sankey 1011 yards per minute, J. Hankinson 974, S. H. Sankey 964, T. Abbey 90, F. Fazakerley 920, P. Roberts 877, G. Roberts 871, and J. Jackson 856.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES SOCIETY.—This society flew their second Gloucester young birds' race on Saturday, when 21 members had 199 birds liberated at 11.40. The wind was south-west and the weather fine and clear. Result (subject to correction) :—G. Wright, a*1021; G. Wright, *997; Boyle, *9S7; Roughsedge, 903; Turner, 900; Wooley Bros , 957; Barton and Barlow, 945; Woolley 8r05.,.944 ; Wheatley, 943; Lorgmore, 942; Summer- field Brcs., 939 ; Johnson Bros., 936; Detkin, 934; Bond one Grimshaw, 928 ; Houghton, 925 ; Skelland, 924; Reid and Harrison, 924; Anghton and Rircmer, 919 ; Hardy and Thomas, 918 ; DiAkenson, 880. *Wins the penny pool; a Wins the silver cup, value Li is., presented by the" Homing Pigeon" Company.</p>
