      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>TRANSFER OF LICENCE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednesday Lefcre Messrs. Charles White (chairman), H. Roberts and Colonel Buckton, Mr. H. J. Longton applied for the transference of the Oddfellows Arms, Ea' lestown, from Thowas Lytbgoe to James P. Thorpe of St. Helens.—The licence mas granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ANOTHER DROWNING CASE.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER DROWNING CASE.</p>
        <p>Another drowning fatality occurred at Eatlestown on Tcetday, when the body of John Dutton, about fifty years of age, of 13, St. John-street, Earlestown, was recovered from the St. Helens and Sankey Canal. Dutton folmerly worked at the Collins Green Colliery at a pony driver, but as the result of a kick received from a pony he bad not. followed his employment for some time. On Tuesday he left home about noon, and was not seen al e again by any of his family, his body being taken from the canal near to Penkford Bridge about five o'clock in the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DROWNING MYSTERY.</head>
        <p>DROWNING MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse conducted an inquest at Eariestown on Monday morning into the circumstances surrounding the death of Thomas Dagnen (41), a single man of Houghton-street, Earlestown, whose body was recovered from the St. Helens and Sankey Canal on Friday last. From the evidence it'appeared that deceased, who was employed as a labourer at the Sankey Sugar Works, was working night turn last week. On Friday he returned from work and slept until noon, when, after complain- ing of the excessive heat, he went out for a walk. Shortly after two o'clock a collier named George Rowley found deceased's jacket and cap on the canal bank, and on giving information to the police the body was recovered from the canal. There being no evidence to show how deceased got into the water, a verdict of "Found drowned" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>WORKMAN BURIED ALIVE.</p>
        <p>TRAPPED IN A TUNNEL BY A FALL OF EARTH</p>
        <p>--Amt-e-rt-il;le -fatality occurred at the new sewage works at Sutton, St. Helens, on Saturday, a miner named Joseph Bray (64), 31, Frederick-street, Sutton, being buried alive.—A new intercepting sewer is being con- structed for the St. Heleas Corporation by a London contractor, and work was resumed en Wednesday after several weeks stoppage.—On Saturday morning, Bray and another man named James Appleton, 25, Harrison- street, Sutton,twere boring a tunnel from the bottom of the shaft, 181 t. deep, and they had '(tot a distance of about 18ft. when the roof suddenly collapsed, burying Bray under many tons of dirt.—Appleton had a very narrow escape.—A rescue party set to wolk to clear the tunnel, but it was nearly seven o'clock on Saturday night before the body was reached.</p>
        <p>LYING IN THE ROAD.</p>
        <p>BABY KILLED AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held an inquest on Saturday, at the I Bull's Head, Parr, St. Helens, on Edith Hilton, one year and nine months old, the daughter of a Parr collier. On Thursday, the 29th of last month, the child's mother took it with her to her motber-in-law's house in Parr, Stocks-road,and while she was doing some work the child toddled out into the road.—John Glover said he saw the child lying in the road near the tram- lines when William George Kay. an earthenware dealer, of 62, Higber Parr-street, drove down with a horse and cart. The wheel of the cart went right over the child, and he called on the driver to stop. The driver, who was quite sober' did not know he had run over the George Kay stated that he never saw the child, and if the man had not called to him, be would never have known of the accident. He had not- good sight, but he was a careful drher, and was wear- ing his glasses.—The jury returned a verdict of" Acci- dontal death," but declined to express any opinion as to the culpability of the driver. BUDGET MEETING AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Monday night, a largely-attended open-air meet- ing was held in Bishops-road, St. Helens, in support of the Budget, the principal speakers being Mr. T. Glover, member for the Borough, and Mr. J. A. Seddcn, mem- ber fcr the Newton Division.</p>
        <p>Mr.T. Glover, dealing with mineral royalties, con- tended that they ought to belong to the people, and not to those who owned the land. The present owners of the royalties did not care whether the miners were receiving a fair day's wage or not ; they did not care whether their wages sere reduced or not, but they would have their pound of flesh. Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., dealt at some length with the question of the land taxation, and asked what was the reason for the proposed taxes on 'land. What the opposition objected to was valuation. Would they believe that the land of this country had not been revalued for three hundred years. Nominally there was a tax of four shillings in the pound an land, hut in the opulent city of Lii-erpool that tax brought in one thirty-sixth of a penny in tbe pound. In poverty- stricken Essex, which was agricultural country pure and simple, they had to pay the full four shillings in the pound. He was glad the Government were goingto make the valuation,but the first intention was that the owners of tho land should make it,and he Would have been afraid of it ; but now they would have a real valuation, and when it was presented to the people of this country they would be flabbergasted at the amount of money which had been abstracted by the idle few. A penny per square yard would bring in eighty-one millions sterling, but they wore paying less than a million at the present time. Those men, who had grown rich beyond the dreams of avarice, were opposing the land taxes because they were afraid of the onward rush of democracy. The Budget was the first clarion note of hope for the active worker. It spelled greater security for the children who were to come after them, and it was in consonance with the highest teaching the world had ever known—the Fatherhood of God and the brotherhood of man. (Applause). The resolution in favour of the Budget was carried wlth three cheers.</p>
      </div>
