      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Politics of the Week.</head>
        <p>The Politics of the Week.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crostield and the " Colour Bar."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Witli a patience and dexterity which command the admiration of everyone,Mr. Lloyd-George has piloted the Land Clauses of the Budget through the shoals and quicksands of the Committee Stage. Wednesday night saw them, with one or two exceptions, practically thrc,ugh that stage, the all-night sitting on Tuesday having pretty well broken the back of the Opposition. Parliamentary life, as every one knows, has its humorous as,well as its serious side, and even in the grey hours of the early dawn, when Members pale, unkempt, unshaven, are by no means looking their best they are quite ready for a bit of fun when there is a chance. And one occurred at a very early hour indeed on Wednesday morning, .when Mr. Remnant, a. Conservative member, was speaking from the Opposition Benches and Mr. Winston Ohurchill came into the House in rather uncommon attire. He was wearing what looked to some members like a pair of bedroom slippers, and one of the new queer cut shirts one sees at times, which I have never observed very closely but which look as if they were shirt, collar and tie all in one. This shirt was made of some pink • material and Mr. Remnant thought he spied—not strangers—but pyjamas! and at once exclaimed at the enormity of a Cabinet Minister coming to the House in this garb, and there was not a little hilarity at Churchill's expense, which he took in very good part. But when Mr. Joynson -Hicks chimed in with something he said across the table which f did not catch, he at once received, as most people do when they tackle "Winston," a Roland for his Oliver, as the President of the Board of Trade promptly said, "You go to— Maschester." In view of Joynson Hicks' sorry plight there the other day at his great Budget protest meet- ing of which every tile in the House is well aware, this was a palpable hit which members also enjoyed. And so we sat on carrying clause after clause until we went home not even with the milkman, but to breakfast about 8.30, too late even to "turn in." The cheerful willingness which members on the Govern- ment side have shown to sit up, not one night, but any number of nights to carry this democratic Budget through, told its tale at length on the Opposition and that same Wednesday evening we made most expediti- ous progress with nearly all the remainder of the 28 clauses which form Part 1 of the Budget. If this has added very greatly to the Chancellor's popularity in the country, it has added also to his reputationin the House, for such a combination of tireless patience, unrivalled good humour, rapidity of apprehension, • quickness of decision, courage and dexterity, and let me not forget to add Wit and sympathy, which he has shown during these long days and nights of debate has 'surely never been surpassed and seldom equalled in the House of Commons. And as a result of it all, and in spite of all this violence and vehemence of denunciation which we have had all through the country, what is the upshot? Why, increment and undeveloped Land Taxes, which if they err at all err on the, side of extreme moderation. Indeed in ,one par- ticular—the omission of all increment duty where the increment does not exceed 10 per cent.--I think that same omission (made before it was finally decided that the State should bear the cost of valuation), is a mistake and a matter which I hope we shall see put right on Report Stage. I think it was perfectly fair and right that the State should hear the cost of valuation, and from the outset I have supported both that and the exclusion from taxation of agricultural land. But on the other hand, if in the future there should be some further development of the present taxes, if for instance the unearned increment tax should ultimately be not one fifth, but say a third- -33 per cent. instead of 20 per cent.—l hold that such taxation would still be absolute* just and reasonable. But yesterday our thoughts were occupied with a widely different subject, the Committee Stage of the debate on South African Union; and one amendment moved in Committee by Mr. Barnes—the able Labour Member who sits for the Blackfriars Division of Glas- gow—there was the most searching, as it was also one of the most important, debates I have ever followed. The object of this amendment wa,s to pre- vent a backward step being taken in Cape Colony and i Natal by the introduction n these Colonies of what is termed a." colour bar." The effect of this colour bar is to deprive not merely a black man, but anyone who may have inherited even a trace of the -Wood of a coloured race, of the right such a man may now ehjoy to sit in Parliament if duly elected. Let it be noted at • once that such an amendment if carried would not in the slightest degree undermine the authority rightly and properly exercised by the white people of South Africa as the governing race there. , How completely that authority exists 'already is best illustrated by Cape Colony itself. Since the Cape Parliament was established there fifty-five years ago a fraction of the electorate in that Colony have been coloured men and are so at the present time, and yet not one single coloured than has ever been elected to that Parliament. What then will be the position of things under the Union Parliament? The suffrage, except for an insignificant minority, will be limited to the whites who will retain--as they ought to do—their full measure Of authority in the country. No man with even the least trace of colour about him would have a chance of being elected to Parliament except by the votes of the white population. That means in practice that no coloured, or slightly coloured man would have the least chance of being elected for any part of outli Africa unless he were a man of real capacity and distinction, There are such men, coloured or slightly coloured, of capacity and distinction. It is trim they are the exception and not, the rule, but is the Imperial Parliament to be asked to tell them that even if elected by the white voters of South Africa as a recognition of their marked ability, or it may be public service—that even then they are to be ineligible to sit in Parliament? This is so gratuitous and so unnecessary, as it is also so gross an insult, that not one single member in the House had a word to say in defence of it. Think for a moment of the insulting anomalies and uncertainties which such a colour bar needless:y creates. As Sir Charles Dilke pointed out in a powerful speech, the Jew from Bombay is to be eligible; the highly educated Parsee is not'! ! And among people born in South Africa who is going to decide whether a person mainly of European descent but with some trace of colour about him is to be regarded as a coloured man or not? Certainly in an Empire like ours where we have hundreds of millions of coloured subjects, some of , them half castes, others with the slightest trace of -colour about them, such an insulting regulation is dead against Imperial interests. That was admitted by everyone in yesterday's debate; but it was urged that we must leave the matter to he re-adjusted later tn by the Colonial Parliament. We must not inter- fere because our Colonial friends would dbject to it. Why should they object to our revising one Article in their Constitution ,anymore than the people,of Australia objected' to a revision we made of a very important Article in their Constitution? Is it fair to ask the Imperial Parliament to abrogate all authority where _ it can not get rid of responsibility for tin act like this and its consequences? I don't think so. It seems to me we have as much right and even more duty to interfere in this case than we had when we interfered at the time the Australian Constitution was formed. Nor for one moment do I believe that such interfer- ence would have been allowed to prevent the Union of South Africa. It might lead to some delay, and I say at once better a Effie delay in order to wipe out what is so serious andf from every point of view, so unfortinVe a blot on this Constitution which on every other ground, and with this single exception, we all rejoice to see created. Apart from this Amendment, for which I voted yesterday, the Second and Third Readings of this South African Bill were happily carried without a division, and we can but hope now that the strong expressions of opinion from all quarters of the House, as well as the protest in the Division Lobby may induce our Colonial friends to reconsider this matter having regard to the interests of our Empire as a whole, and not less, one feels in the long run, the peaceful government and well-being of their own great South African Commonwealth.—Yours etc.,</p>
        <p>- A. H. CROSFIELD. .39, Hyde Park Gate, S.W.. August 20th, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>f 8 Mr. Rylands and the Budget.</head>
        <p>f 8 Mr. Rylands and the Budget.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln Mr. Rylands' lengthy letter to your contemporaries last Saturday, he fails to make it clear , where he stands in regard to the Budget. Mr. , Rylafids says:—" It may and probably will be con- ' ceded by many that cases do arise under our present system in which the landlord is able to take undue advantage of his position, and it is probable that intelligent proposals to remedy the evil would - seoure a considerable amount of support from all parties." It would have helped us if 111-. Rylands had made clear what the principle is governing the cases he has in mind in which the landlord is able to take an*undue advantage, and have told us wherein the principle differed from that on which the Budget proposals are based. I judge the cases cannot be thosein which the value is socially created and which being appropri- ated by an individual landowner is what is usually termed "unearned increment." Still, Mr. Rylands leaves us in doubt because he goes on to combat the argument not by a direct method, but by claiming that other increment besides that which comes to a ground landlord is socially created, such as the appreciation of shares. The case against the Budget is surely very weak if it cannot be attacked directly and shown to be wrong on its merits, and when all that can be said against it is that there are other forms of increment -which ought also to be subject to this special taxation. Mr. Rylands makes no attempt to prove the Budget propcisals wrong, but simply says the spread of your net is not wide enough. An opponent who has nothing worse to say than this is, in a very weak position. But I ,do not admit that -Mr. Rylands' cases are parallel, but if they are they suggest a field for taxa- tion which may be usefully worked in the future. Mr. Rylands mentions securities in general, but in invest- ing in securities the capitalist runs as much chance of , these depreciating as appreciating, and further he may lose thel whole of his capital in any business undertaking, as many have found to their sorrow. Land cannot be lost, and in a country like our own where population and wealth are steadily increasing and towns continually growing land values must rise and rise indepently of any effort of the owners but as a result of the wants of the com- munity, and by their efforts. Because of this the Government have embodied in the Budget proposals that since such values are socially created the individuals who are in a position to appropriate • such values ought to give a portion back to the , country and the municipality who act as trustees for : those who have created these values. It will be the : future work of wise statesmanship to differentiate , between values which are created individually by personal effort and those created socially, but absorbed individually, anti taxation should be lightened on ' values created personally so that every inducement [ should be given to call forth individual activity, and taxes ought to be laid upon wealth socially created but individually monopolized. Mr. Rylands says "any increase to be paid in the • form of taxation must reduce te exactly the same extent the amount spent in other directions, and as it ' is absolutely certain that all money spent ultimately takes the form of wages, taxation however imposed must be borne by the community as a whole. It is idle therefore for the people to deceive themselves into believing that they have escaped by puffing a tax on the landlord." Unless I have misunderstood Mr. Rylands' meaning this sentence seems to me absurd. . When Money is taken by the State and spent or invested for the public good, will it be less productive or afford less employment and remuneration for labour? The probability is that it will be more wisely and usefully spent, because in many cases money that comes to an individual without personal effort is often , squandered or spent in useless luxuries, but in the , case of the State it is spent in public service or in national defence. Mr. Rylands' fear about the alarm of capital is , quite groundless. Had there been a depreciation in , the value of British securities there might have been , ground for his fears. Without this, the truest . barometer, Mr. Rylands need not get alarmed. 'L Mr. Rylands says the only consideration influencing - the Government is retention of office, that Mr. Lloyd- George's object at Limehouse was to inflame the . passions of his hearers. There is a good deal in his E letter of unworthy imputing of base motive which I L consider is not creditable to Mr. Rylands and certainly tends to lower the tone of political controversy. , Surely it is not necessary or fair to treat your opponent's motives as base because You differ from them. Of course if Mr. Rylands so considers them I do not object to his stating the fact. But if he really L believes that those who do not share his political views are so utterly unworthy, and he wiShes others to L • adopt his low estimate, he ought to give more cogent , reasons and indulge less in ungupported assertions. CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Cefn Mann, Wales. August 18th, 1909,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LOCAL PRESBYTERIANISM</head>
        <p>LOCAL PRESBYTERIANISM</p>
        <p>A New Minister Appointed.</p>
        <p>Presbyterianism is, we hope, about to undergo a needed revival in Warrington, for on Thursday a new minister in 'the person of Mr. William Reid was appointed to take charge of the St. John's Presby- terian Church. This seems rather strange when it is remembered that their church in Winwick- street has been converted into a warehouse, but the people say they are members of St. John's Church still, Since their removal from Winwick-street, services have been held each Sunday evening in Cairo-street school, under the leadership of the Rev. R. W. Gray, of Bowden and it is very gratifying to know that during the last two months the attendance has been considerably increased. . Cairo-street is only being used temporarily until the new church hall on Wilderspool Causeway, which is to hold 250 people, ,is ready. Building operations are to com- mence in October and it is expected that the place will be ready for worship at the beginning of next year.. The architect is Mr. S. P. Silcoek. The new minister was born in Flintshire is quite a young man and appears to be full of energy for the work which he has undertaken. His father, who died when Mr. Reid was three years old, was an Irish Presbyterian, and his mother is a daughter of Wales. Mr. Reid was educated in Flintshire and Chester and soon after leaving school was engaged in the engineering depart- ment on the railway. He relinquished those duties a few years afterwards and took over the charge of Hartley-street Mission, Sunderland, whence he now comes. During his ministry there the congregation and the revenue of the Mission doubled.</p>
        <p>Previous to handing the charge of the church over to Ml'. Reid, a tea was held in the Cairo-street school- room, when about 80 persons sat down to and enjoyed the good things provided. After tea a meeting was held, over which the Rev. R. M. Gray presided and was supported by Mr. Reid and Mr. W. Russell. The CHAIRMAN said they were gathered together to welcome Mr. and Mrs. Reid. He was quite sure they would earn the friendship of everyone. They had real prosperity before them if they would Work hand in hand with the minister. (Applause). He then handed the charge (verbally, of course) over to Mr. Reid. Mr. W. RUSSELL also extended a welcome to the new pastor and bis wife. Mr. WILLIAM REID, who was received with loud applause, thanked them for the hearty reception that they had accorded to him and his wife. It came with a great degree of refreshing and revival to one's beart, as to leave one's own friends was a great trial. The recep- tion he had had took away a great deal of the pangs and sadness. He would like to communicate something of the assurance and hope that he felt in his own heart at that juncture. He felt hopeful and he felt sure that God had unmistakably marked out his action in coming there. He believed they would attain success. They stood for Presbyterianism and what Presbyterianism stood for. Ile did not care what difficulties there were to face. (Hear, hear.) He was saw the difficulties were nothing to be afraid of, for God was guiding them. (Applause.) Concluding, be said that if they stood together with one mind and with one spirit then there was no power that could keep them back. (Applause). If they were a united people there was nothing that could retard their progress, because God had promised that if they were gathered together in one accord with one spirit—the spirit of the Master— there His power would operate through them.(Applause.) Mr. B. WaLiamsos also welcomed Mr. and Mrs. Reid. He said that there was a feeling of hopefulness amongst there. If Mr. Reid could only infect them with his spirit and enthusiasm there would be no doubt about the future. There was room for ' them in War- rington and he was sure there was a great scope for Presbytarienisro. If they could only make up their minds, as Mr. Reid had certainly made up his, he was sure they would do well. (Applause.) Mr. P. H. BROWN said that Mr. Reid would find amongst the people of Warrington a warm hearted and lovable spirit. Their numbers were very small, but he was sure that under the leadership of Mr. Reid they would grow. (Applause.) What had been Sunderland's loss would be Warrington's gain. . . . _ _ _</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the -Mr. RUSSELL a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Rev.R. Gray,who had been in charge since Mr. Milne left. Mr. S. T. SmUna's° spoke. Mr. RUSSELL moved a vote of.thanks to the ladsie who had provided the tea and also to the artistes. This was carried.</p>
        <p>Mrs. RUSSELL replying said that the ladies would do all they could to promote Pc esbyterianism in Warrington. During the evening a short programme was gone through. Miss Snailham ard Miss Dewar sang" I know a bank," very sweetly. Mr. G. Hetherington contri- buted "Bonnie Mary of Argyle" and "An evensong.' Miss Snailham also rendered "Fiddle and I," whilst Miss Dewar greatly amused the audience with the recitation "I want to fly? Mr. J. T. Smith rendered the humorous song "Rabin Thomson's S.nithy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Smith Williams. A vcry quiet raid pretty wedding, which harmonised with the solemnity of the occasion, was celebrated on Monday last at St. Paul's Church, when the contract- ing parties were Mr. Ellis C. Smith, son of' the late 'Mr. Paul Smith, of Swanpea, Snd Miss Alice J. Williams, daughter of the late Mr, Thomas Williams, of Folly-lane. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. E. E. eeters,-and the ( rganist Mr. J, M. Muir, who played the bridal march from- " Lohengrin." The bride, who was given away by her stepfather, Mr; T. Kidd. was attired in a cream Toile empire gown, with Paisley bordering. She also carried a shower bouquet of lilies of the valley, sweet peas and smilax. The bridesmaids were Miss L. 'Williams (sister, of the bride), and Miss Ivy Hannah (niece of the bride). Their dreses were of mauve voile. They also carried shower bouquets and wore gold pendants the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. T. G. Williams (brother of the bride) discharged the duties of best man. After the service a reception was held in Cairo-street schools, and later in the day the happy couple left for London,l and Swansea, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was a blue tailor-made costume. The list of presents is as follows : Bridegroom to bride, diamond ring Bride to bridegroom, signet ring Mr. and Mrs. T. Kidd, case of cutlery Mrs. A. T. Kidd, table linen Mr. A. T. Kidd, stair rods Master Fred Kidd, copper tray and crumb brush Mr. Atkin and Miss Kidd, toilet service Mrs. Hannah, hand-painted table centre Nurse Walkers,. (Swansea) silver flower vases Sister Gerrard, (Warrington) silver and ivory handled cake knife Miss E. Williams dinner service Mr. T. G. Williams, Saratoga trunk and serviette rings Mr. and Mrs. Seabourne, (London) silver tea spoons Mrs Paul Smith, (S mansea) silver cake basket Mr. Kenneth Small (Plymouth), set of carvers Mr. Westratith (Saltburn), plant bowl Mr. and Mrs. H. Shaw, silver fruit dish Nurse Smith (Macclestield),hand-painted photo frame Miss Ravenhill (Oldham), porcelain photo frame and photo Miss M. Smith, silver toast rack Mr. and Mrs. T. Shaw (Australia), diamond bracelet Dr. Dutton (Manchester), silver vases Mr. and Mrs. Price (London), silver tea caddy ; Mr. Dobinson (Middlesborough), brass and oak letter rack Mrs. Beere (Middlesborough), tea cosy Mrs. and Miss Woods, salad dish Mr. and Mrs. D. Morgan (Swansea), Case of fish'eaters Mr. and Mrs. G. Shaw, jam dish and spoon Mr. and Mts. Bowman, calendar in brass frame Mother Superior and Sisters (North Ormsby Hospital) pair of pictures Dickson and Benson and Co., Ltd., bedding '•-• - Wright and Co., Ltd., door mats Miss L. Kidd, duchesse set Nurse Tucker (Colwyn Bay), fruit stand</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page 3.) INDEPENDENT ORDER OF ODDFELLOWS M.U.) "CONSTITUTION" LODGE. ANNUAL BOWLING HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>To-day (Saturday) at the King and Queen Hotel, at 2 p.m. FIRST ROUND. A. Lawton 2 v. G. H.,W.Steel</p>
        <p>A. Pennington J. H. Tames 2 v. J. T. Mather W. H. Ball 2 v. J. B. Mellor 4 John Ellison 6 v. T. Ellison 6 Thomas Jones 2 v. P. Lester 6 H. Taylor 3 v. W. Watson W. A. Warburton 2 v. W. H. Lawless ... ..... : 3 A. E. Burdett J. Makin T. Howard 2 v. N. J. Blarke 3 D. M. Barker Byes. R. Bennett W. Clayton W. Damtith 3 F. B. Mellor J. W. Rigby 1 G. Holbrook scr. Frank Lloyd 3 H. Jarman 3 R. K. Wilson</p>
        <p>S. Dale J. H. Wood T. S. teel J. W. Davies 6 H Savory 4 ODDFELLOWS' HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greene!' Distributes the Prizes. Sir Gilbert Greensll, Bart., distributed the prizes last night in the Blue Lion Hotel to ' the v 4 inners of the bowling handicap of the Warrington District of the National Independent Order of Oddfellows. The handicap was held on June 19th at the Chapel House, Sankey. Amongst those present at the distribution were :---Messrs. Bro. Andrews (Wakefield), D.G.M. Bro. Bentley (Eccles), P.G. M. Heywood (Manchester), D.S. J. W. Perry, D.T. W. M. Pennington. G.M. Mile Dunn, D.G.M. G. Wilkinson, P.P.G.M. L. Hands, P.P.G.M. Bramball, P.P.G.M. Meachin. Letters of apology were received from Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P., and Bro. Johnson (corresponding secre- tary). During theevening Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart., Mr. Robert Pierpoint, and Councillor Fulton-Smith were initiated as honorary members.</p>
        <p>P. G. M. HEywOon (Manchester), replying to the toast of the Order, said that the Chancellor of the Exchequer was going to do something for the friendly societies. The Chanceltor told them that be was not going to hurt them by his new scheme. He did not know whether it would be so or not. He would like to know what he proposed first before he (the speaker) agreed with him. (Laughter). He did trust that what- ever step the Government took in regard to Friendly Societies they would manfully endeavour to do their duty to the State. (Applause). Sir Gilbert Greenall then presented the prizes. The list is as follows:— J. J. Singleton, Sir Gilbert Greenall's challenge shield and cruet ; J. Looker, tea service •, Asbbrook, preserving pan ; J. Shaw, sen., copper kettle; W. Bonnell, cruet; J. Richardson, timepiece; R. Perrins, half-dozen knives and forks; F. Ogden, pipe and case; J. W. Meachin, half-dozen spoons; R. • Blackshaw, carvers; G. Naylor, pint bottle of whiskey J. Wilkinson, bottle of whiskey; A. Davies, half-dozen handkerchiefs; H. Wareham, walking stick; T. Cawley, pine and case ; J. Little, bottle of whisky. The DEPUTY GRAND MASTER proposed the health of the new members. He personally thanked Sir Gilbert Greenall for the very great interest he had taken in the Oddfellows in this district. He was pleased that he bad come amongst them as a brother—(apnlanse)—and that they could call him so. He believed Mr. Pierpoint was seeking Parliament honours and be hoped that if be did get to Parliament he would do what he could to hell) the Friendly Societies. (Applause.) Mr. ROBERT PIERPOINT saidi that they should do all they could for the good of their society and for the good of their own brethren. If they could get an advantage to their society by getting help from the Government,he cared not what Government was in office, they should get it, but they must not make themselves subservient to any Government in office. (Applause). A musical programme was submitted, the accompanist being Mr. Percy Wolfenden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>A capital "bill of fare" is provided at the abOve abode of amusement and this weekteaded by the AndOs troupe of Japanese equilibrists and jugglers, from the Opera House Tokio, Japan. They provide a wonder- ful turn, excelling in contortion, jugaling and balanc- ing. Wonderment is at its highest when the elder of the troupe balances a bicycle at the end of a long pole on his shoulder while a lady, after doing numerous balancing tt icks, "caps" all by working the pedals at express speed. A daring feat is performed by a young lady of the troupe by walking a tight rope suspended from the stage to the gallery, and on reaching the top she slides backwards down the-rope. Needless to say, this feat is greeted with loud applause. The scenery and setting of the stage, which are all of embroidery work done by hand, are splendid. The Three Sisters Tree present a refined act entitled " Hunting" and are no mean exponents of the art of dancing. In the place of Ernese-Nltees, the droll comedian, who does not appear this week, the Langfords make a fine show at clog and sand dancing, and Fred Law, a clever comedian, also presents an extra turn. Bibabi, proves to be a clever eccentric musician and his antics while playing on the bells, create roars of laughter. " Pown. the Vale " and," Annie Laurie "are sweetly sung by Primrose Harding who has a nice contralto voice. Mamie Morris gives a good turn as a comedienne. The great glove fight between Hague and Langford for the heavy weight championship of England is depicted on the bioscope.The four rounds are clearly portrayed, the knock out blow being exceedingly fine. A highly dramatic picture entitled" The Two Friends "is also presented.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Alleged Theft at Warburton. At the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, before Mr. F. A. Frost, Isaac Hamblett and Joseph Heiden, Cadishead, were remanded for a week on the I charge of stealing three cans of paint, one cask of white lead, and two ladders, of the value of £7 10s. from a hut on the canal bank at Warburton. P.C. Smith spoke to arresting the prisoners at 10.45 on Saturday night. CYCLES.-End of season. Clearance sale. Great r:ductions.-J. S. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warring- ton.- Advt. Found In the Canal. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest at Collins Green on Saturday morning, on John Mallin, an engine- driver, of 85, Dallam-lane, Warrington, whose body was found in the St. Helens and Sankey Canal, on Friday morning, by a waterman. Mary Ellen Mallin, deceased's sister, said her brother lived with her, and he seemed upset all Sunday, but she knew of no trouble that he had. He was last seen on Tuesday morning. The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned." N.S.P.C.C. Work. The Warrington and District Branch of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children has during the quarter ended 30th June, 1909, inquired into 64 complaints of neglect, ill-treatment and other wrongs of childhood, of which all were found to be true, affecting 186 children and 86 offenders. Action was taken as follows :-Fifty-seven cases were warned, four were prosecuted (convicted four, discharged nil), and three were otherwise dealt with. Thesociety's Inspector made 388 visits of supervision, Athletic Successes. C. Berry, Warrington's crack cyclist, was second in the mile N.C.U. Championship, Mancliester Centre which was decided at Fallowfield on Saturday. He was beaten by half a length by E. Davies, of Man- chester Wheelers. Berry won his heat hi the quarter-mile championship, but was beaten in the second round. At Nortonthorpe, near Huddersfield, on Saturday, A. Siddall of Warrington, was third in the 100yds. sprint off the 101yds. mark. At the Cheshire Lines Sports held on the Roodee, Chester on Saturday, the following local successes were reported :-80 yards (girls under. 15) : Miss Freeman, Warrington,l ; 200 yards (boys over 16) : T. Cooper, Warrington 1. 80 yards thread the needle race: : Mrs, G ilhooley, Warrington, 2. Consolation sack race: W. Lawler, Warrington, 1. GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., £8 Bs., and £l2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,-J. R. Wool), 47. London rd., Stockton Heath, Advt. Shooting Competition. The annual shooting competition for the members of the Volunteer Lodge of Freemasons, No. 2,905 was held at Altcar on Saturday when Mr. J. W. Plinston, of Warrington was victorious and carried off the cup presented by Colonel John Barlow, the first Master of the Lodge. Sealed 'handicap conditions, ten shots each at 200 and 509 yards, R.N.A. scoring, &amp;c. The result in the first instance was a tie between Mr. Plinston, Mr. C. Bellhouse, and Mr. R. S. Newton with 99 points each. The three'afterwards competed for places and Mr. Plinston won with 14 points, Mr. Bellhouse being second with 13, and Mr. Newton third with 12. In addition to the cup, the Worshipful Master, Bro. E. Malcolm Brundrett, presented a replica to the winner. After the shoot the c mapetitors dined at Hotel, the Worshipful Master Bro. Brundrett, presiding. The Late Mr. John White. At the Warrington Petty Sessiens on Wednesday, Mr. Charles White (chairman), referred to the death of Mr. John White, who was a member of the bench. He said that they met that day under a very mournful affliction. Mr. John White had been taken away. During the past two or three days several notes of praise had been sounded to the extensive usefulness of Mr. White's life. He thought one quality had been omitted and that was Mr. White's complete unselfish- ness. To do good to others whether Individuals or institutions, to promote the happiness of others seemed to be the motives of his conduct. Then there was his scrupulous integrity. He was a most exemplary magistiate. He never expressed any opinion far less , any judgment until he had heard all that could be said on either side of the caser. They could only regret his absence. They could sympathise most deeply with Mrs. White,who had suffered such an irreparable loss. -Mr. Longton, on behalf of the solicitors practising in that court, said that in other courts solicitors had spoken of Mr. White and had only said what was right and what he wished to repeat. They found him a most painstaking, conscientious and fair magistrate TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, SUIT CASES, etc. Prices to suit all.-J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge at., Warrington.-Advt. Board of Guardians. The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednes- day, Mr. G. Mather presiding. The were also present: -Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Collingwood, Rev. M. , Ryan, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, W. C. , Brookes, T. Ritchie, W. Furness, A. Bottomley (clerk), J• F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superintendent of the Cottage Homes).-The Clerk read a letter from the Roman Catholic Bishop of Liverpool stating that as the Rev. Patrick Walshe had returned to his native diocese in Ireland, he no longer held any licence or , faculties in the Liverpool diocese. The Bishop had pleasure in recommending for the appointment by the Board as Roman Catholic instructor of Rev. Thomas Dobson, assistant at the Church of the Sacred Heart, ' The Rev.2ll. Ryan gave notice that at the next meeting he would move the appointment of the Rev. Thomas 1, Dobson at a salary of £50.-It was decided that in future the contract for the supply of milk should be made for a period of 21 years, also to invite tender for the supply of tea at fixed prices (Is. per lb. inmates, and is. 2d. officers), samples to be submitted to experts. Bakers' Bowling Prizes. The fourth annual distribution of bowling'prizes in connection with the Warrington Bakers' Association took place at the Blue Bell, Horsetharket-street, on Saturday evening.-The Chairman (Mr. Crighton) made sympathetic allusion to the absence of Mr. Higginbottom, their latebrauch secretary, who founded the bowling main, and moved that they send a vote of sympathy to him in the sad accident he had sustained. -This was seconded by Mr. Grainger and agreed to. -A handsome collection of prizes had been given by the Master Bakers of Warrington, and they were dis- tributed by Mr. Grealey,-Mr. Rathbone, the secre- tary, carried off the rose bowl and medal. The other prize-winners were :-2, W. Pendleton; 3, W. Nicholls; 4, W. Millington; 5, J. Jackson; 6, T. Warburton; 7, W. Lagar ; 8, W. Crighton (jun.) ' • 9, F. Everett' 10, J. Green; 11, R. Broadhurst; 12, J Berry; 13, F. Barnard; 14, T, Woods; 15, S. Davies; 16, W. Dagnall ; 17, J. Leigh; 18, A. Deakin ; and 19, W. Martin. Consolation prizes were won as follows: -1, F. Harbanerne ; 2, J. Howard; 3, W. Peake ; 4,F. Barnes; 5, C. Grainger; 6, E. Millar; 7, A-Rogers ; 8, E. Wilson; 9, J. Gleeson ; and 10, 0. Lee. Songs were rendered during the evening by Mr. Lee, Mr. Barnes, Mr. Rathbone and Mr. Deakin. Mr. Wolferdon was the accompanist. A vote of thanks was accorded , to the donors of the prizes and also to Mr. Greeley for presenting them. BABY 'CARRIAGES AND Go-cAnTs.-Clearance Sale. All prices reduced.-J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.-Advt.</p>
        <p>Armitage and Rigby's General Employees Annual Picnic. The popular event took place on Saturday when the partyjourneyed by the 1.27 train to the pretty little village of Gateacre which was reached about 2.30. The place of entertainment selected was the Bridge Inn (Mr. J. Wilson) where a subscription bowling main was commenced in which a large number of the visitors joined and thus occupied the larger portion of the afternoon. At 5.30 a splendid knife and fork tea was served for which the caterer Mr. J. Wilson deserved every credit. On the proposition of Mr. S. Bailey, secretary, by Mr. J. Darbyshire, a hearty vote of thanks was \accorded to Messrs. Armitage ani Rigby for their 'generosity in enabling the men to enjoy themselves that afternoon and this was carried unanimously. Tea over, the last stages of the main were decided, the winner, Mr J. Bradshaw winning a well-played game by 13-7 over Mr. B Williams with J. Bennett and A. Eckersley third and fourth. A concert was then held in the large hall adjoining the Bridge Inn, Mr. B. Green being accorded an encore for his singing of "Sing us one of your old songs, George," and East and West." Mr. J. Wilson, sen., contributed an old Lancashire song, " knOws by mysel'." Mr. J. Wilson, jun., sang "Sweet chiming bells" and Miss Wilson "The song that reached my heart." A very enjoyable evening was brought to a close with a vote of thanks to the landlord and others who had added to the enjoyment of the party and the return journey com- menced at 9.40, home being reached about 10.30, afte2 splended afternoon's outing,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-08-21_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PRIMROSE LEAGUE PLEASANTRIES.</head>
        <p>PRIMROSE LEAGUE PLEASANTRIES.</p>
        <p>The Latest Political Menu for Tory Dames.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spencer Leigh Hughes, writing in the "Sib Rosa" column of the "Morning Leader" on, Wednesday found a congenial topic in the sayings , and doings at a recent Primrose League demonstration. He says : There is a touch of humour in the desperate struggle which is going on among the various rival leagues which live by reviling the Government. Each is trying to shout louder than its rivals in order to avoid being for- gotten—for, after all, secretaries and agita- tors must live. It is not surprising, there- fore, to find that the Primrose League is making unusual attempts to attract attention lest, perchance, some newer and noisier organisation should pass it in the race. There were other days when it was quite enough to have a little tea-party, at which middle- class dames could meet Lady Mary or Lady Jane, and then, after a few words from the curate and the sale of some Imperial safety- pins and patriotic brooches or studs, the harmless proceedings came to a genteel end. At present, however, the champions of the league are called upon for sterner work, and, according to a report in the " Biggleswade Chronicle," the Harvey Habitation of the Primrose League recently held its annual fête at Ickwell Bury,. when Mr. Turing- Mackenzie, described as "Secretary of the League," was one of the performers. There was another performer who, or, rather which, caused some disappointment, for I read with regret that the pig race was expected to cause a lot of fun, but it proved a very tame affair, for a hurdle out of place in the enclosure prevented the pig getting away, and Mrs. Berridge easily picked it up and carried it to its box, and thus won. In these circumstances, as the pig had not risen to the occasion, I can imagine Mr. Turing-Mackerizie saying, in Miltonic language, as he marched to the platform. "What though the pig be lost? All is not lost ! " It should be remembered, too, that ladies were present, and Mr. Mackenzie adrnitted in one place that this fact caused him to temper his rhetoric somewhat. The report says: Referring later to a statement of Mr. Churchill's, the speaker said he should call it a lie, and if ladies had not been present he should have said an adjective lie, and a big adjective,too. We may thus regard Mr. Mackenzie's speech as one of his drawing-room efforts, when he is satisfied with calling his opponents liars— and he waives the rest! But even when the gentleman is so restrained, even when giving us a specimen of his drawing-room style, Mr. Mackenzie indulges in some rather curious similes. Here is one : It would be a big task to reduce the Radicals' enormous majority. He was recently speaking of their majority to a Northamptonshire farmer, who said majorities were like muckheaps, the bigger they were the quicker they rot. We all know this big majority is rotten already, but, continued the speaker, it will want a lot of shifting, and we must do the work of muck-shifter and move it. Not long ago a Conservative speaker at Cambridge boasted that his party had started a movement known as "pub-crawling," and now we find the same great party engaged in what is described at a gathering of Primrose League ladies as "muck-shifting." If I were to say that the Conservative party consists of pub-crawlers- and muck-shifters people would think I was rude—so I do not say so. All I say is that these are the descriptions they apply to themselves. There was another delicate touch in the gentleman's restrained speech specially adapted for the ears of ladies, for •Mr. Mackenzie ended his speech by telling his audience that when the Radical skeleton came'again and said it was hot they were not to run away as they did at the general election, but to stand and tell them it isn't half so hot as the place they were going' to very soon. (Applause.) The grammar has suffered a little owing to the sentence being reported in the third person, but Mr. Mackenzie's meaming is clear enough. He wishes, the ladies and gentlemen of the Primrose League to say, or to suggest in a manner that cannot be mistaken, that the Radicals are going to hell. If Mr. Mackenzie thinks this is the style in which to address the ladies of his party, I have no objection to offer. They may like to hear opponents described as liars, with broad hints at lurid adjectives; they may enjoy a polished reference to the Liberal party in the House of Commons as a "muck- heap," and may even welcome an allusion to themselves as "muck-shifters," and they may appreciate the subtle humour of Mr. Mackenzie's hint about Radicals being on the way to the infernal regions. All this is a matter of taste, and should be left to be settled by Mr. Mackenzie and his lady friends. But I do protest against a man who talks such stuff in public also saying on the same occasion: All of us insist that religion should be taught in the schools. We will have our religion taught to our children, for our religion must be main- tained if our Empire is to be maintained. This snuffle about religion is far more offen- sive than Mr. Mackenzie's characteristic pleasantries about muck-shifting. He may be thoroughly qualified for that operation, but when he begins to roll up his eyes in the interests of true religion, the petformance is not a success.</p>
        <p>'Now that the leasehold system is being upheld as a divine institution, it is worth while to take a few dips into the evidence of the Town Holdings Committee. No town has had a worse experience of the leasehold system than Liverpool. This is the judgment of Mr. Enoch Harvey,a solicitor of Liverpool and a past President of the Liverpool Law Society: Reluctantly I have been forced to the opinion that the leasehold system as exemplified in Liver- pool is an unmitigated evil, and the sooner it is abolished the better. That is the general opinion.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WHITE SG WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw at., opposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
